const BANK = [
// ---------- S1 Platform ----------
["A001", 1, "An engineer runs `CREATE TABLE main.sales.orders (id INT, amount DOUBLE)` with no `LOCATION` and no `USING` clause. What is created?",
 ["A Unity Catalog managed Delta table", "A Unity Catalog external Delta table in the workspace root", "A managed Iceberg table", "A Hive metastore Parquet table"],
 "In a Unity Catalog catalog, leaving out `LOCATION` makes the table managed (UC picks the storage path), and leaving out `USING` gives the default format, Delta.\n`external Delta table`: a table is external only when you give a `LOCATION`; with none, UC manages the storage.\n`managed Iceberg table`: Iceberg has to be asked for with `USING iceberg`; Delta is the default.\n`Hive metastore Parquet table`: `main.sales.orders` is a UC three-level name, and the default format is Delta, not Parquet.", "managed"],
["A002", 1, "With default table properties, how old must unreferenced data files be before `VACUUM` deletes them?",
 ["7 days", "30 days", "24 hours", "90 days"],
 "`VACUUM` only deletes unreferenced files older than `delta.deletedFileRetentionDuration`, which defaults to 7 days.\n`30 days`: that is `delta.logRetentionDuration`, how long the transaction log (table history) is kept, not how long data files are kept.\n`24 hours`: not a default; vacuuming with a retention shorter than the configured one is blocked by the retention safety check unless you turn it off.\n`90 days`: not a default for either retention setting.", "hist"],
["A003", 1, "What is the default value of `delta.logRetentionDuration`?",
 ["30 days", "7 days", "14 days", "365 days"],
 "`delta.logRetentionDuration` controls how long table history (log entries) is kept, and it defaults to 30 days.\n`7 days`: that is `delta.deletedFileRetentionDuration`, the data file retention `VACUUM` uses. Because files go after 7 days, practical time travel is about 7 days even though history lasts 30.\n`14 days`: not the default for either retention setting.\n`365 days`: not a default; you would have to set it yourself.", "hist"],
["A004", 1, "A team needs to time travel 60 days back on a Delta table. What must they change?",
 ["Raise both delta.logRetentionDuration and delta.deletedFileRetentionDuration to at least 60 days", "Raise only delta.logRetentionDuration to 60 days", "Raise only delta.deletedFileRetentionDuration to 60 days", "Nothing, because time travel is unlimited by default"],
 "Time travel to a version needs two things: the log entry describing that version (`delta.logRetentionDuration`) and the data files it points to (`delta.deletedFileRetentionDuration`). Both must cover 60 days.\n`Only logRetentionDuration`: history would show the version, but `VACUUM` could already have removed its data files after 7 days, so the query fails.\n`Only deletedFileRetentionDuration`: files survive, but log entries older than 30 days can be cleaned up, so the version can no longer be found.\n`Nothing, unlimited`: both settings have defaults (30 and 7 days) that limit how far back you can go.", "hist"],
["A005", 1, "`RESTORE TABLE t TO VERSION AS OF 3` fails, saying data files are missing. What is the most likely cause?",
 ["VACUUM has already removed the data files that version 3 needs", "RESTORE only works on external tables", "RESTORE needs the table to be partitioned", "Version numbers start at 10"],
 "`RESTORE` rebuilds an old version from its data files. If `VACUUM` has deleted files that version 3 references, they are gone and the restore fails with missing files.\n`only works on external tables`: `RESTORE` works on Delta tables whether managed or external.\n`needs the table to be partitioned`: partitioning has nothing to do with restore; any Delta table with the files still present can be restored.\n`Version numbers start at 10`: Delta versions start at 0, so version 3 is a normal version number.", "hist"],
["A006", 1, "Which compute access mode does Databricks recommend for most workloads, unless you need a feature it doesn't support?",
 ["Standard", "Dedicated", "No isolation shared", "Custom"],
 "Databricks recommends Standard access mode (formerly Shared) for most workloads: it supports Unity Catalog and lets many users share compute with isolation between them.\n`Dedicated`: (formerly Single user) is the choice when you need something Standard doesn't support, such as ML runtimes, GPUs or R, not the general default.\n`No isolation shared`: a legacy mode with no user isolation that doesn't support Unity Catalog.\n`Custom`: not an access mode you pick; it only appears for compute with a legacy configuration.", "ccfg"],
["A007", 1, "A new serverless SQL warehouse is created with default settings. When does it auto stop?",
 ["After 10 minutes idle", "After 45 minutes idle", "After 120 minutes idle", "Never"],
 "A serverless SQL warehouse starts quickly, so its default auto stop is short: 10 minutes idle.\n`45 minutes`: that is the default for Pro and Classic warehouses, which take longer to start so are kept up longer.\n`120 minutes`: not a default for any warehouse type.\n`Never`: auto stop is on by default for new warehouses; you would have to turn it off yourself.", "wh"],
["A008", 1, "A new Pro SQL warehouse is created with default settings. When does it auto stop?",
 ["After 45 minutes idle", "After 10 minutes idle", "After 5 minutes idle", "Never"],
 "Pro and Classic warehouses run on classic compute that is slower to start, so they default to 45 minutes idle before auto stop (minimum 10).\n`10 minutes`: that is the serverless default, and also the lowest value allowed for Pro and Classic.\n`5 minutes`: below the 10-minute minimum for a Pro warehouse; only serverless can go that low.\n`Never`: auto stop is on by default; disabling it is a choice you make.", "wh"],
["A009", 1, "Which statement about job compute is correct?",
 ["It is created for the job run, terminates when the job ends, and can't be restarted", "It stays running between scheduled runs", "It can be shared interactively by notebook users", "It is the same as an all-purpose cluster with a job attached"],
 "Job compute is created for one job run and terminated when the run finishes. It can't be restarted; the next run gets fresh job compute.\n`stays running between scheduled runs`: that would be all-purpose compute. Job compute exists only for the run, which is why it is cheaper for production.\n`shared interactively by notebook users`: interactive work uses all-purpose compute; job compute serves only its job.\n`same as an all-purpose cluster with a job attached`: you can run a job on all-purpose compute, but that is a different compute type, priced differently and not recommended for production jobs.", "cm"],
["A010", 1, "On a compute resource running a current Databricks Runtime, Photon is:",
 ["On by default (DBR 9.1 LTS and above)", "Off until enabled by an admin", "Only available on SQL warehouses", "Only available for Python UDFs"],
 "Photon is enabled by default on compute running Databricks Runtime 9.1 LTS and above, so on a current runtime it is already on.\n`Off until enabled by an admin`: no admin step is needed; you would have to switch it off yourself.\n`Only on SQL warehouses`: Photon also runs on clusters and job compute, not just warehouses.\n`Only for Python UDFs`: Photon accelerates SQL and DataFrame operations; Python UDFs are not what it speeds up.", "ccfg"],

// ---------- S2 Ingestion ----------
["A011", 2, "On Databricks Runtime 17.3 LTS, an Auto Loader stream has no rate-limit options. 40,000 new files are waiting. How many files does each micro-batch process?",
 ["At most 1,000", "All 40,000 in one batch", "At most 10,000", "At most 100"],
 "`cloudFiles.maxFilesPerTrigger` defaults to 1,000, so with 40,000 files waiting Auto Loader works through them roughly 1,000 files per micro-batch. (On Databricks Runtime 18.0 and above this option is set dynamically instead.)\n`All 40,000 in one batch`: the default file limit caps each batch, so the backlog is split across many batches.\n`At most 10,000`: not the default; you could set the option to 10,000 yourself.\n`At most 100`: also not the default; 1,000 is.", "alp"],
["A012", 2, "Both `cloudFiles.maxFilesPerTrigger` and `cloudFiles.maxBytesPerTrigger` are set. Which statement is correct?",
 ["The file limit is hard, the byte limit is soft, and the batch stops at whichever limit is reached first", "The byte limit is hard and the file limit is soft", "Only the last option set is applied", "Setting both causes an error"],
 "Both can be set together. The file count (`maxFilesPerTrigger`) is a hard limit that is never exceeded; the byte size (`maxBytesPerTrigger`) is a soft limit a batch may go slightly over. A batch ends at whichever is reached first.\n`byte limit is hard, file limit soft`: this is the reverse of how the docs define them.\n`Only the last option set is applied`: both are honoured together.\n`Setting both causes an error`: combining them is supported and common.", "alp"],
["A013", 2, "Auto Loader infers the schema (no `.schema()` call). Which schema evolution mode is used by default?",
 ["addNewColumns", "none", "rescue", "failOnNewColumns"],
 "When Auto Loader infers the schema (no `.schema()`), the default `schemaEvolutionMode` is `addNewColumns`: a new column stops the stream, the schema is updated, and the restart includes it.\n`none`: is the default only when you provide a schema with `.schema()`.\n`rescue`: must be set explicitly; it never changes the schema.\n`failOnNewColumns`: must also be set explicitly; it fails until someone updates the schema by hand.", "als"],
["A014", 2, "An Auto Loader stream is given an explicit schema with `.schema(...)`. What is the default schema evolution mode?",
 ["none", "addNewColumns", "rescue", "failOnNewColumns"],
 "When you pass a full schema with `.schema()`, Auto Loader respects it: the default mode is `none`, so the schema doesn't change and new columns are ignored.\n`addNewColumns`: the default only when the schema is inferred, and it isn't allowed with `.schema()` at all.\n`rescue`: must be set explicitly; it sends unexpected data to the rescued data column.\n`failOnNewColumns`: must be set explicitly; it fails the stream on a new column.", "als"],
["A015", 2, "An Auto Loader stream uses the default mode with an inferred schema. A file arrives with a brand-new column. What happens?",
 ["The stream stops with an UnknownFieldException; on restart it picks up the updated schema", "The new column is silently ignored", "The new column's values go to _rescued_data and the stream continues", "The stream fails permanently until the table is recreated"],
 "In `addNewColumns` mode, a new column makes the stream fail with `UnknownFieldException` after merging the column into the schema. The restart uses the new schema, so run it in a job with retries.\n`silently ignored`: that is what `none` does, the default only when you supply `.schema()`.\n`goes to _rescued_data and the stream continues`: that is `rescue` mode, which never changes the schema.\n`fails permanently until recreated`: closer to `failOnNewColumns`, which keeps failing until the schema is fixed by hand; even then you don't recreate the table.", "als"],
["A016", 2, "Default evolution mode, inferred schema. A column inferred as INT later receives the value 'N/A'. What happens?",
 ["The column stays INT and the value goes to _rescued_data", "The column changes to STRING", "The stream stops and widens the type on restart", "The row is dropped silently"],
 "`addNewColumns` only adds new columns; it never changes the type of an existing one. A value that doesn't fit the column's type is kept in `_rescued_data` and the column stays INT.\n`changes to STRING`: Auto Loader doesn't change existing column types in this mode.\n`stops and widens the type on restart`: only new columns trigger a stop; type widening needs `addNewColumnsWithTypeWidening`, and STRING isn't a widening of INT anyway.\n`row is dropped silently`: the row is kept; only the bad value is moved to `_rescued_data`.", "als"],
["A017", 2, "Which mode lets Auto Loader widen an INT column to BIGINT when larger values arrive?",
 ["addNewColumnsWithTypeWidening", "addNewColumns", "rescue", "none"],
 "`addNewColumnsWithTypeWidening` (Public Preview, DBR 16.4+) adds new columns like `addNewColumns` and also widens supported types, such as INT to BIGINT.\n`addNewColumns`: adds new columns but never changes an existing column's type; mismatches go to `_rescued_data`.\n`rescue`: never changes the schema at all.\n`none`: never changes the schema and ignores new columns.", "als"],
["A018", 2, "The inferred schema has a column `userId`. A file contains `UserID`. With default settings, where does that value end up?",
 ["In _rescued_data", "In the userId column", "In a new UserID column", "It causes the stream to fail"],
 "By default the rescued data column matches column names case-sensitively, so `UserID` doesn't match `userId` and its value is kept in `_rescued_data`.\n`In the userId column`: that only happens if you turn off case-sensitive rescue with `readerCaseSensitive = false`.\n`In a new UserID column`: a name that differs only by case isn't treated as a new column to add.\n`causes the stream to fail`: the mismatch is rescued, not raised as an error.", "als"],
["A019", 2, "What does Auto Loader's `_rescued_data` column contain?",
 ["Data that couldn't be parsed into the schema, stored as JSON with the source file path", "A copy of every raw row", "Only rows that failed expectations", "The checkpoint offsets"],
 "`_rescued_data` holds values that couldn't be parsed into the schema (type mismatches, unknown fields, case mismatches), stored as a JSON blob with the path of the source file.\n`A copy of every raw row`: only the parts that didn't fit are stored; rows that parse cleanly have it null.\n`Only rows that failed expectations`: expectations are a pipeline feature and are tracked in pipeline metrics, not in this column.\n`The checkpoint offsets`: those live in the checkpoint location, not in the data.", "als"],
["A020", 2, "Auto Loader reads JSON files with schema inference and no other options. What type are the inferred columns?",
 ["STRING", "The type seen in the first file", "VARIANT", "The widest numeric type"],
 "JSON, CSV and XML don't encode data types, so Auto Loader infers every column (including nested JSON fields) as STRING to avoid type-mismatch problems. Set `cloudFiles.inferColumnTypes` to true or use schema hints for real types.\n`type seen in the first file`: only happens with `inferColumnTypes` turned on, and inference samples many files, not just one.\n`VARIANT`: you get a variant column only if you ask for one; it isn't the default.\n`widest numeric type`: no numeric types are inferred by default.", "als"],
["A021", 2, "By default, how much data does Auto Loader sample for schema inference?",
 ["The first 50 GB or 1,000 files, whichever comes first", "The first file only", "The first 10 GB or 100 files", "Every file in the directory"],
 "Auto Loader samples the first 50 GB or 1,000 files, whichever limit is hit first, to infer the schema.\n`first file only`: one file is often unrepresentative, so it samples far more.\n`10 GB or 100 files`: not the defaults; both limits are larger.\n`Every file`: would be slow on large directories, so inference is capped by the sample limits.", "als"],
["A022", 2, "An engineer wants Auto Loader to keep inferring the schema but force `amount` to DECIMAL(10,2). What should they use?",
 ["cloudFiles.schemaHints", "A full .schema() definition", "cloudFiles.schemaEvolutionMode = rescue", "COPY_OPTIONS ('mergeSchema' = 'true')"],
 "`cloudFiles.schemaHints` overrides the inferred type for named columns while Auto Loader keeps inferring the rest. Hints apply only when you don't pass `.schema()`.\n`A full .schema()`: would stop inference for every column, not just fix `amount`.\n`schemaEvolutionMode = rescue`: controls what happens to new columns, not the type of an existing one.\n`COPY_OPTIONS mergeSchema`: belongs to `COPY INTO`, not Auto Loader.", "als"],
["A023", 2, "An engineer provides `.schema(...)` and sets `schemaEvolutionMode` to `addNewColumns`. What happens?",
 ["It isn't allowed; to evolve, use schema hints instead of a full schema", "New columns are added to the provided schema", "The provided schema is ignored", "The stream switches to rescue mode"],
 "`addNewColumns` isn't allowed with a user-provided `.schema()`. To fix some types and still evolve, pass those types as `cloudFiles.schemaHints` and let Auto Loader infer the rest.\n`New columns are added to the provided schema`: Auto Loader doesn't evolve a schema you supply in full.\n`provided schema is ignored`: a provided schema is never silently ignored.\n`switches to rescue mode`: there is no automatic switch to another mode.", "als"],
["A024", 2, "Which schema evolution mode never changes the schema and never fails on new columns, sending unexpected data to the rescued data column?",
 ["rescue", "none", "failOnNewColumns", "addNewColumns"],
 "`rescue` keeps the schema fixed and never fails: anything that doesn't fit, including new columns, goes to `_rescued_data`.\n`none`: also never changes the schema, but new columns are simply ignored and their data isn't rescued unless you set `rescuedDataColumn`.\n`failOnNewColumns`: fails the stream on a new column.\n`addNewColumns`: changes the schema and stops the stream so the restart picks it up.", "als"],
["A025", 2, "Which schema evolution mode fails the stream on a new column and keeps failing until the schema is updated by hand?",
 ["failOnNewColumns", "addNewColumns", "rescue", "none"],
 "`failOnNewColumns` fails on a new column and won't restart successfully until someone updates the schema or removes the file with the new column.\n`addNewColumns`: also stops the stream, but updates the schema itself, so the restart succeeds.\n`rescue`: never fails; new data goes to `_rescued_data`.\n`none`: never fails; new columns are ignored.", "als"],
["A026", 2, "An Auto Loader stream has no file detection settings. Which mode does it use?",
 ["Directory listing", "File notification", "File events", "Polling the checkpoint"],
 "With no file detection settings, Auto Loader uses directory listing: it lists the input path to find new files.\n`File notification`: has to be turned on explicitly; it is recommended, but it isn't the default.\n`File events`: also must be enabled, both on the external location and in the stream options.\n`Polling the checkpoint`: the checkpoint records which files were processed; it isn't a way of discovering new files.", "alf"],
["A027", 2, "Which file detection approach does Databricks recommend for most Auto Loader workloads?",
 ["File notification mode, using file events on the external location", "Directory listing with a continuous trigger", "Directory listing with maxFilesPerTrigger = 1", "Manually listing files with dbutils"],
 "Databricks recommends file notification mode backed by file events on the external location: new files are discovered from events instead of repeated listing, which scales better and costs less, with no extra cloud permissions on the stream.\n`Directory listing with a continuous trigger`: lists the path again and again, which gets slow and costly as directories grow.\n`maxFilesPerTrigger = 1`: only shrinks batches; it doesn't make file discovery any cheaper.\n`dbutils listing`: hand-built and loses Auto Loader's checkpoint tracking.", "alf"],
["A028", 2, "A stream is moved from directory listing to file notification mode. What happens to exactly-once guarantees?",
 ["They are kept; you can switch modes between restarts", "Every file is reprocessed", "The checkpoint must be deleted first", "Switching isn't supported"],
 "Auto Loader lets you switch between directory listing and file notification across restarts. The checkpoint still records which files were processed, so exactly-once is kept.\n`Every file is reprocessed`: the checkpoint prevents that.\n`checkpoint must be deleted first`: deleting it would cause reprocessing; the switch works with the same checkpoint.\n`Switching isn't supported`: the docs say you can switch between restarts.", "alf"],
["A029", 2, "An Auto Loader job only needs to run once an hour and cost matters more than latency. Which trigger fits?",
 ["trigger(availableNow=True) on a schedule", "A continuous trigger in directory listing mode", "trigger(processingTime='1 second')", "No trigger, running all day"],
 "`trigger(availableNow=True)` processes every waiting file in batches and then stops, so a scheduled hourly job only pays for compute while it runs.\n`continuous trigger in directory listing mode`: keeps compute running and keeps listing the directory all the time, the costly option.\n`processingTime='1 second'`: also keeps the stream (and its compute) running all the time.\n`No trigger, running all day`: the default trigger runs micro-batches nonstop, which keeps compute on all day.", "alp"],
["A030", 2, "What must stay the same between scheduled `availableNow` runs of an Auto Loader stream?",
 ["The checkpoint location", "The cluster ID", "The number of input files", "The notebook name"],
 "The checkpoint records which files Auto Loader has already processed. Each `availableNow` run must use the same checkpoint location (and nothing like a lifecycle policy may delete it), or files get reprocessed.\n`cluster ID`: scheduled runs can use new job compute each time; the stream doesn't depend on a specific cluster.\n`number of input files`: varies every run; that is the point of incremental ingestion.\n`notebook name`: the stream's progress is tied to the checkpoint, not the notebook.", "alp"],
["A031", 2, "`COPY INTO` is run twice on the same source path with default options. One file was modified between runs. What happens on the second run?",
 ["Files already loaded are skipped, including the modified one", "Only the modified file is reloaded", "All files are loaded again", "The command fails"],
 "`COPY INTO` is idempotent: it remembers which files it loaded, and with `force` at its default of false it skips them, even if a file was modified after loading.\n`Only the modified file is reloaded`: modifying a file doesn't make `COPY INTO` load it again.\n`All files are loaded again`: happens only with `COPY_OPTIONS ('force' = 'true')`.\n`The command fails`: a re-run just skips loaded files and loads any new ones.", "copy"],
["A032", 2, "How do you make `COPY INTO` reload files it has already loaded?",
 ["COPY_OPTIONS ('force' = 'true')", "FORMAT_OPTIONS ('force' = 'true')", "COPY_OPTIONS ('mergeSchema' = 'true')", "Add VALIDATE ALL"],
 "`force` is a `COPY_OPTIONS` setting (default false). Setting it to true turns off idempotency, so files are loaded again whether or not they were loaded before.\n`FORMAT_OPTIONS force`: `FORMAT_OPTIONS` hold parser options for the file format (such as `header`), not load behaviour.\n`mergeSchema`: allows schema evolution, it doesn't reload files.\n`VALIDATE ALL`: checks the data without writing anything.", "copy"],
["A033", 2, "An engineer wants `COPY INTO` to check the source files for problems without writing any data. What do they use?",
 ["VALIDATE", "COPY_OPTIONS ('force' = 'false')", "DESCRIBE HISTORY", "FORMAT_OPTIONS ('dryRun' = 'true')"],
 "`VALIDATE` (DBR 10.4+) parses and checks the source data against the table without writing anything. With no row count, it validates all rows.\n`force = false`: is the default and just controls skipping loaded files; it still writes data.\n`DESCRIBE HISTORY`: shows past table operations, it doesn't check source files.\n`dryRun`: is not a `COPY INTO` format option.", "copy"],
["A034", 2, "Where does `mergeSchema` go in a `COPY INTO` statement to allow schema evolution?",
 ["COPY_OPTIONS", "FORMAT_OPTIONS only", "The TBLPROPERTIES clause", "A Spark config"],
 "To let `COPY INTO` evolve the target table's schema, put `'mergeSchema' = 'true'` in `COPY_OPTIONS` (default false).\n`FORMAT_OPTIONS only`: a `mergeSchema` format option exists, but it merges the schemas of the source files when reading; on its own it doesn't evolve the target table.\n`TBLPROPERTIES`: holds table properties, not per-load options.\n`A Spark config`: `COPY INTO` takes it as a statement option.", "copy"],
["A035", 2, "A table is created with no columns (`CREATE TABLE t;`) as a `COPY INTO` target. What lets COPY INTO set its schema?",
 ["COPY_OPTIONS ('mergeSchema' = 'true')", "COPY_OPTIONS ('force' = 'true')", "Nothing; COPY INTO can't load into a schemaless table", "VALIDATE 100 ROWS"],
 "With `COPY_OPTIONS ('mergeSchema' = 'true')`, `COPY INTO` infers the schema from the source and sets it on a schemaless target table (DBR 11.3+).\n`force`: reloads files already loaded; it doesn't set a schema.\n`can't load into a schemaless table`: it can, which is why creating an empty `CREATE TABLE t;` target is a documented pattern.\n`VALIDATE 100 ROWS`: only checks data and writes nothing, so no schema is set.", "copy"],
["A036", 2, "Which statement about the `FILES` clause of `COPY INTO` is correct?",
 ["It accepts up to 1,000 file names and can't be combined with PATTERN", "It accepts unlimited file names", "It must be combined with PATTERN", "It accepts regular expressions"],
 "`FILES` names specific files, up to 1,000 of them, and can't be used together with `PATTERN`; you pick one or the other.\n`unlimited file names`: the limit is 1,000.\n`must be combined with PATTERN`: the two are alternatives and can't be combined.\n`regular expressions`: `FILES` takes plain names; matching by pattern is `PATTERN`'s job, and it uses glob syntax.", "copy"],
["A037", 2, "What syntax does the `PATTERN` clause of `COPY INTO` use?",
 ["A glob pattern", "A Java regular expression", "SQL LIKE syntax", "A JSONPath expression"],
 "`PATTERN` takes a glob pattern, such as `'year=2026/*.json'`, to select files under the source path.\n`Java regular expression`: glob wildcards (`*`, `?`, `[abc]`) are used, not regex syntax.\n`SQL LIKE syntax`: `%` and `_` aren't the wildcards here.\n`JSONPath`: is for querying inside JSON documents, not for picking files.", "copy"],
["A038", 2, "`COPY INTO` reads from a path covered by a Unity Catalog external location. Which privilege is needed on that external location?",
 ["READ FILES", "SELECT", "USE CATALOG", "MODIFY"],
 "Reading files from a path covered by a UC external location needs `READ FILES` on that external location. Inline credentials are only needed when no external location covers the path.\n`SELECT`: is for reading tables and views, not raw files in storage.\n`USE CATALOG`: lets you access objects in a catalog; it doesn't grant file access on an external location.\n`MODIFY`: is for changing table data; it is needed on the target table, not for reading source files.", "copy"],
["A039", 2, "A Lakeflow Connect managed connector ingests a Salesforce object with default settings. How are updated records stored?",
 ["Overwritten in place (SCD type 1)", "Kept as history rows (SCD type 2)", "Appended as duplicate rows", "Dropped"],
 "Managed connectors default to SCD type 1: history tracking is off, so an updated record overwrites the existing row. Set `scd_type: SCD_TYPE_2` where the connector supports it to keep history.\n`history rows (SCD type 2)`: must be turned on; it isn't the default.\n`Appended as duplicate rows`: the connector applies changes by key, so updates don't create duplicates.\n`Dropped`: updates are applied, not discarded.", "scd"],
["A040", 2, "Data must be pulled from Salesforce into Unity Catalog incrementally, with as little custom code as possible. What fits best?",
 ["A Lakeflow Connect managed connector", "Auto Loader", "COPY INTO", "A notebook calling the REST API every minute"],
 "Lakeflow Connect managed connectors pull from SaaS apps like Salesforce incrementally and land data in Unity Catalog with no custom code: you configure the connection and the connector runs it.\n`Auto Loader`: reads files from cloud storage; it can't connect to Salesforce.\n`COPY INTO`: also loads files from cloud storage, not from an application.\n`notebook calling the REST API`: possible, but you'd have to write and maintain the incremental logic yourself, the opposite of minimal code.", "lfc"],
["A041", 2, "Millions of JSON files land in cloud storage every day, and new fields appear now and then. Which ingestion method fits best?",
 ["Auto Loader", "COPY INTO", "The file upload UI", "A managed Lakeflow Connect connector"],
 "Auto Loader is built for large, continuous file arrival: it scales to millions of files and handles new fields through schema evolution.\n`COPY INTO`: also loads files with some schema evolution, but it suits smaller volumes (thousands of files), not millions a day.\n`file upload UI`: is for small one-off uploads, not a daily pipeline.\n`managed Lakeflow Connect connector`: connects to apps and databases; files in cloud storage are Auto Loader's job.", "alp"],
["A042", 2, "A partner's REST API has no connector. The data must land in a UC table every hour. What is the usual pattern?",
 ["A notebook calls the API and writes to a UC table or volume, scheduled by a Lakeflow Job", "Auto Loader pointed at the API URL", "COPY INTO from the API URL", "A file arrival trigger on the API"],
 "When there is no connector, the pattern is custom code: a notebook calls the API and writes to a UC table or volume, and a Lakeflow Job schedules it hourly.\n`Auto Loader pointed at the API URL`: Auto Loader discovers files in cloud storage; it can't call a REST API.\n`COPY INTO from the API URL`: also loads files from storage paths, not from an API.\n`file arrival trigger on the API`: watches a storage location for new files; an API produces no files to watch.", "lfc"],

// ---------- S3 Transform ----------
["A043", 3, "Adaptive Query Execution (AQE) on a current Databricks Runtime is:",
 ["On by default", "Off by default", "Only available on SQL warehouses", "Only on for streaming queries"],
 "AQE is on by default on current runtimes. It re-optimizes the plan at runtime using real statistics from completed stages.\n`Off by default`: it has been on by default for years; you'd have to turn it off.\n`Only on SQL warehouses`: it runs on clusters and job compute too.\n`Only on for streaming`: the reverse: AQE applies to non-streaming queries that have a shuffle or subquery.", "aqe"],
["A044", 3, "What is the default value of `spark.sql.shuffle.partitions`?",
 ["200", "Number of cores", "1,000", "auto"],
 "`spark.sql.shuffle.partitions` defaults to 200 partitions for shuffles in joins and aggregations.\n`Number of cores`: the default is a fixed 200, regardless of cluster size.\n`1,000`: not the default.\n`auto`: a value you can opt into so Databricks picks the count, but it isn't the default.", "aqe"],
["A045", 3, "Which value lets Databricks pick the number of shuffle partitions automatically?",
 ["spark.sql.shuffle.partitions = auto", "spark.sql.shuffle.partitions = -1", "spark.sql.shuffle.partitions = 0", "spark.default.parallelism = auto"],
 "Setting `spark.sql.shuffle.partitions = auto` enables auto-optimized shuffle, where Databricks picks the partition count for each query.\n`-1`: isn't how auto mode is set; -1 is the value that disables automatic broadcast joins for `autoBroadcastJoinThreshold`.\n`0`: isn't valid; shuffle partitions must be a positive number or `auto`.\n`spark.default.parallelism`: is a different setting for RDD operations, and `auto` isn't a value for it.", "aqe"],
["A046", 3, "What is AQE's runtime broadcast join threshold on Databricks (`spark.databricks.adaptive.autoBroadcastJoinThreshold`)?",
 ["30 MB", "10 MB", "100 MB", "1 GB"],
 "AQE's runtime threshold, `spark.databricks.adaptive.autoBroadcastJoinThreshold`, is 30 MB: if a join side turns out under 30 MB at runtime, AQE can switch to a broadcast join.\n`10 MB`: that is Apache Spark's static `spark.sql.autoBroadcastJoinThreshold`, used when planning, not AQE's runtime threshold.\n`100 MB`: not the default.\n`1 GB`: not the default; broadcasting that much would strain memory.", "aqe"],
["A047", 3, "A query uses `broadcast(dim_df)`, and `dim_df` is larger than the broadcast threshold. What happens?",
 ["dim_df is broadcast anyway", "The hint is ignored and a sort-merge join is used", "The query fails", "Only the first 10 MB is broadcast"],
 "An explicit `broadcast()` hint overrides the size threshold: the table is broadcast regardless of `autoBroadcastJoinThreshold`. The threshold only governs automatic broadcasting.\n`hint is ignored`: hints take priority over the size-based choice.\n`The query fails`: exceeding the threshold isn't an error.\n`first 10 MB broadcast`: a table is never partly broadcast; the whole table goes to each executor.", "hints"],
["A048", 3, "Which setting turns off automatic broadcast joins based on size?",
 ["spark.sql.autoBroadcastJoinThreshold = -1", "spark.sql.shuffle.partitions = 0", "spark.sql.adaptive.enabled = true", "spark.default.parallelism = 1"],
 "Setting `spark.sql.autoBroadcastJoinThreshold = -1` disables broadcast joins chosen by size. Explicit broadcast hints still work.\n`shuffle.partitions = 0`: is about shuffle partition count and isn't a valid value anyway.\n`adaptive.enabled = true`: turns AQE on (it's already on); it doesn't stop broadcasting.\n`default.parallelism = 1`: sets RDD parallelism; it has nothing to do with join strategy.", "hints"],
["A049", 3, "Which of these does AQE do at runtime?",
 ["Switches sort-merge joins to broadcast joins and splits skewed join partitions", "Rewrites the table's file layout", "Adds liquid clustering keys", "Increases executor memory"],
 "AQE works with runtime statistics to change the plan: it can switch a sort-merge join to a broadcast join, split skewed join partitions, coalesce small shuffle partitions and skip empty relations.\n`Rewrites the table's file layout`: that's `OPTIMIZE` or clustering, which change stored files; AQE only changes how one query runs.\n`Adds liquid clustering keys`: clustering keys are set on the table (manually or with automatic clustering), not by AQE.\n`Increases executor memory`: AQE doesn't resize compute.", "aqe"],
["A050", 3, "A pipeline expectation is declared with no `ON VIOLATION` clause. What happens to rows that fail it?",
 ["They are kept, and the failure is recorded in metrics", "They are dropped", "The update fails", "They are written to a quarantine table"],
 "With no `ON VIOLATION` clause, the action is warn: failing rows are written to the target and the failures are counted in the pipeline's data quality metrics.\n`dropped`: needs `ON VIOLATION DROP ROW` (`expect_or_drop`).\n`update fails`: needs `ON VIOLATION FAIL UPDATE` (`expect_or_fail`).\n`quarantine table`: expectations have no built-in quarantine; you'd build one yourself with extra logic.", "exp"],
["A051", 3, "Rows that fail a check must be removed from the target, but the pipeline must keep running. Which Python decorator?",
 ["@dp.expect_or_drop", "@dp.expect", "@dp.expect_or_fail", "@dp.expect_all"],
 "`@dp.expect_or_drop` (SQL `ON VIOLATION DROP ROW`) removes failing rows from the target, records the counts and lets the update continue.\n`@dp.expect`: is warn: failing rows are kept and only counted.\n`@dp.expect_or_fail`: stops the update on the first failing row.\n`@dp.expect_all`: groups several expectations, but with the warn action, so rows are kept; the dropping version is `expect_all_or_drop`.", "exp"],
["A052", 3, "Any row with a null `order_id` must stop the pipeline update. Which SQL clause?",
 ["CONSTRAINT valid_id EXPECT (order_id IS NOT NULL) ON VIOLATION FAIL UPDATE", "CONSTRAINT valid_id EXPECT (order_id IS NOT NULL)", "CONSTRAINT valid_id EXPECT (order_id IS NOT NULL) ON VIOLATION DROP ROW", "CHECK (order_id IS NOT NULL) WARN"],
 "`ON VIOLATION FAIL UPDATE` (Python `expect_or_fail`) stops the pipeline update as soon as any row fails the expectation.\n`EXPECT (...)` with no clause: the default warn action keeps the row and just counts it.\n`ON VIOLATION DROP ROW`: drops bad rows but lets the update keep running.\n`CHECK (...) WARN`: is not valid expectation syntax; pipeline expectations use `CONSTRAINT ... EXPECT`.", "exp"],
["A053", 3, "Which Python module is the current API for Lakeflow pipelines?",
 ["pyspark.pipelines (imported as dp)", "dlt only", "databricks.workflows", "pyspark.streaming.dlt"],
 "`from pyspark import pipelines as dp` is the current Python API for Lakeflow Spark Declarative Pipelines (formerly DLT).\n`dlt only`: the older module name; existing code using `import dlt` still works, but it isn't the current API.\n`databricks.workflows`: not a pipelines module.\n`pyspark.streaming.dlt`: doesn't exist.", "dlt"],
["A054", 3, "Events are only appended to a source, and each must be processed once with low latency. Which pipeline object?",
 ["Streaming table", "Materialized view", "View", "Temporary view"],
 "A streaming table reads an append-only source incrementally, processing each new row once with low latency.\n`Materialized view`: recomputes results so they reflect updates and deletes; for a pure append-and-process-once case it does more work than needed.\n`View`: stores nothing and reruns its query on read, so it doesn't track what has been processed.\n`Temporary view`: exists only within the pipeline and also stores nothing.", "ldp"],
["A055", 3, "A gold table aggregates revenue by region. The source table gets updates and deletes. Which object keeps the result correct?",
 ["Materialized view", "Streaming table", "Temporary view", "External table"],
 "A materialized view recomputes its query (incrementally on serverless compute, in full on classic compute) so its result always reflects the source's current state, including updates and deletes.\n`Streaming table`: expects an append-only source; updates and deletes upstream aren't reflected and by default break the stream.\n`Temporary view`: stores nothing and only exists within the pipeline, so it can't serve as a persisted gold table.\n`External table`: describes where data is stored, not how it's refreshed; nothing keeps the aggregate up to date.", "ldp"],
["A056", 3, "Which gold-layer object stores no data and runs its query each time it's read?",
 ["View", "Materialized view", "Streaming table", "Managed table"],
 "A view is a saved query: it stores no data and runs its query every time it's read.\n`Materialized view`: stores precomputed results and refreshes them.\n`Streaming table`: stores the rows it has processed.\n`Managed table`: stores data in UC-managed storage.", "ldp"],
["A057", 3, "A materialized view has `SCHEDULE CRON '0 30 2 * * ?'`. When does it refresh?",
 ["Every day at 02:30:00", "At 00:30 on the 2nd of each month", "Every 30 minutes", "At 30 seconds past 02:00"],
 "Quartz cron reads seconds, minutes, hours, day-of-month, month, day-of-week. `0 30 2 * * ?` is second 0, minute 30, hour 2, every day: 02:30:00 daily.\n`00:30 on the 2nd`: reads the fields as if there were no seconds field; that schedule would be `0 30 0 2 * ?`.\n`Every 30 minutes`: would need a step like `0 0/30 * * * ?`; a plain 30 means minute 30 only.\n`30 seconds past 02:00`: would be `30 0 2 * * ?`; here the 30 is in the minutes field.", "mv"],
["A058", 3, "Which cron string refreshes a materialized view every day at midnight UTC?",
 ["'0 0 0 * * ?'", "'0 0 * * *'", "'* * 0 * * ?'", "'0 0 24 * * ?'"],
 "Quartz uses six fields starting with seconds. `0 0 0 * * ?` is second 0, minute 0, hour 0, every day: midnight daily.\n`'0 0 * * *'`: is five-field Unix cron; Quartz needs the seconds field, so this doesn't mean midnight.\n`'* * 0 * * ?'`: the asterisks fire every second of every minute during hour 0.\n`'0 0 24 * * ?'`: hours run 0 to 23, so 24 isn't valid.", "mv"],
["A059", 3, "In a Quartz cron expression, what is the first field?",
 ["Seconds", "Minutes", "Hours", "Day of month"],
 "Quartz cron starts with seconds: seconds, minutes, hours, day-of-month, month, day-of-week. One of the two day fields must be `?`.\n`Minutes`: is the first field in five-field Unix cron, which is why it's easy to confuse; in Quartz it's second.\n`Hours`: is the third field.\n`Day of month`: is the fourth field.", "mv"],
["A060", 3, "`orders.join(customers, \"customer_id\", \"inner\")`. Some orders have a customer_id with no matching customer. What happens to those orders?",
 ["They are not in the result", "They appear with null customer columns", "They appear twice", "The join fails"],
 "An inner join keeps only rows that have a match on both sides, so orders with no matching customer are left out.\n`with null customer columns`: that's a left (outer) join from `orders`.\n`appear twice`: duplication comes from multiple matches, not from no match.\n`The join fails`: unmatched keys aren't an error; they're just dropped.", "join"],
["A061", 3, "`customers.join(orders, \"customer_id\", \"left\")`. Some customers have no orders. What happens to them?",
 ["They appear with nulls in the orders columns", "They are dropped", "They appear once per order in the table", "The join fails"],
 "A left join returns every row from the left side (`customers`). Where there's no match, the right side's (`orders`) columns are NULL.\n`dropped`: that's what an inner join does.\n`once per order`: is what happens to customers who have orders, one row per match; customers with none still get one row.\n`The join fails`: missing matches aren't an error.", "join"],
["A062", 3, "`colors` has 4 rows and `sizes` has 5 rows. How many rows does `colors.crossJoin(sizes)` return?",
 ["20", "9", "5", "4"],
 "A cross join pairs every left row with every right row, so the count is the product: 4 x 5 = 20.\n`9`: is the sum, the count from stacking the tables with a union, not a cross join.\n`5`: is just the size of `sizes`.\n`4`: is just the size of `colors`.", "join"],
["A063", 3, "Two DataFrames with the same columns in a different order must be stacked by column name. Which method?",
 ["unionByName", "union", "join", "crossJoin"],
 "`unionByName` stacks rows by matching column names, so a different column order is fine.\n`union`: matches columns by position, so with a different order values land in the wrong columns.\n`join`: combines columns side by side on a key; it doesn't stack rows.\n`crossJoin`: pairs every row with every other row; it doesn't stack.", "pyspark"],
["A064", 3, "In PySpark, `df1.union(df2)` is run on two DataFrames that share some identical rows. What happens to the duplicates?",
 ["They are kept, like SQL UNION ALL", "They are removed, like SQL UNION", "The union fails", "Only the rows from df1 are kept"],
 "`DataFrame.union` keeps all rows, duplicates included, like SQL `UNION ALL`. Add `.distinct()` to remove them.\n`removed, like SQL UNION`: that's the SQL operator's behaviour; the DataFrame method doesn't deduplicate.\n`The union fails`: duplicates are allowed; union needs matching column count, not unique rows.\n`Only rows from df1 are kept`: union returns rows from both DataFrames.", "pyspark"],
["A065", 3, "A table must combine rows from two sources and keep every row, including exact duplicates. Which SQL operator?",
 ["UNION ALL", "UNION", "INTERSECT", "EXCEPT"],
 "`UNION ALL` returns every row from both inputs, keeping exact duplicates.\n`UNION`: removes duplicate rows, so exact duplicates would be lost.\n`INTERSECT`: returns only rows present in both inputs.\n`EXCEPT`: returns rows from the first input that aren't in the second.", "join"],
["A066", 3, "An array column `tags` must become one row per tag, but rows with a null or empty array must still appear. Which function?",
 ["explode_outer", "explode", "flatten", "split"],
 "`explode_outer` makes one row per array element and still outputs a row (with a null tag) when the array is null or empty.\n`explode`: also makes one row per element, but drops rows whose array is null or empty, the near miss here.\n`flatten`: merges an array of arrays into one array; it doesn't create rows.\n`split`: turns a string into an array; it doesn't create rows.", "pyfn"],
["A067", 3, "`full_name` holds 'Ada Lovelace'. Which expression gives the last name?",
 ["split(col('full_name'), ' ').getItem(1)", "split(col('full_name'), ' ').getItem(2)", "explode(split(col('full_name'), ' '))", "col('full_name').substr(' ')"],
 "`split` turns the string into an array `['Ada', 'Lovelace']`, and `getItem` is zero-based, so index 1 is the second word, the last name.\n`getItem(2)`: asks for a third element, which doesn't exist; it's a one-based mistake.\n`explode(split(...))`: creates one row per word rather than picking the last name.\n`substr(' ')`: `substr` takes a start position and length, not a delimiter.", "pyfn"],
["A068", 3, "Which method renames the column `cust_id` to `customer_id`?",
 ["df.withColumnRenamed('cust_id', 'customer_id')", "df.withColumn('customer_id', 'cust_id')", "df.alias('customer_id')", "df.rename('cust_id')"],
 "`withColumnRenamed(existing, new)` renames a column and keeps everything else unchanged.\n`withColumn('customer_id', 'cust_id')`: `withColumn` adds or replaces a column from a Column expression, and its second argument must be a Column, not a string. Even with `col('cust_id')` it adds a copy and keeps the old column.\n`df.alias('customer_id')`: gives the whole DataFrame an alias, used for self-joins, not renaming a column.\n`df.rename`: isn't a PySpark DataFrame method.", "pyspark"],
["A069", 3, "Bronze has duplicate events. Each `event_id` must appear once in silver. Which method?",
 ["df.dropDuplicates(['event_id'])", "df.distinct('event_id')", "df.dropna(subset=['event_id'])", "df.groupBy('event_id')"],
 "`dropDuplicates(['event_id'])` removes rows with the same value in the listed columns, keeping one row per `event_id`.\n`distinct('event_id')`: `distinct()` takes no arguments and compares whole rows, so events with the same id but other differences would remain.\n`dropna(subset=['event_id'])`: removes rows where `event_id` is null; it doesn't remove duplicates.\n`groupBy('event_id')`: returns grouped data that needs an aggregation; on its own it isn't a deduplicated DataFrame.", "pyspark"],
["A070", 3, "A dashboard needs a fast, approximate count of unique visitors across billions of rows. Which function?",
 ["approx_count_distinct", "countDistinct", "count", "collect_set"],
 "`approx_count_distinct` estimates the number of distinct values with a sketch (HyperLogLog++), trading a small error for much less memory and shuffle than an exact count.\n`countDistinct`: is exact, so it must track every distinct value, which is slow and costly across billions of rows.\n`count`: counts rows (non-null values), not unique visitors.\n`collect_set`: builds the full set of distinct values in memory; exact and even heavier.", "pyfn"],
["A071", 3, "Which DataFrame method returns count, mean, stddev, min, the 25%/50%/75% quartiles and max by default?",
 ["summary()", "describe()", "count()", "approxQuantile()"],
 "`summary()` with no arguments returns count, mean, stddev, min, the 25%, 50% and 75% percentiles, and max. You can also pass it just the statistics you want.\n`describe()`: the near miss. It returns count, mean, stddev, min and max only, with no quartiles.\n`count()`: returns only the number of rows, as a single number.\n`approxQuantile()`: computes the quantiles you ask for on given columns, but none of the other statistics.", "pyspark"],
["A072", 3, "Nulls in the `quantity` column must be replaced with 0 when cleaning bronze into silver. Which code?",
 ["df.fillna({'quantity': 0})", "df.dropna(subset=['quantity'])", "df.filter(col('quantity') == 0)", "df.replace(None)"],
 "`fillna` (the same as `na.fill`) replaces nulls with a value. Passing a dict like {'quantity': 0} targets only that column and keeps every row.\n`dropna(subset=['quantity'])`: removes the rows where quantity is null instead of filling them, so you lose data.\n`filter(col('quantity') == 0)`: keeps only rows already equal to 0 and changes no values. Null rows are dropped too, because null == 0 is never true.\n`replace(None)`: `replace` swaps existing values for new ones and needs a valid value to match. Nulls are handled with `fillna`.", "pyspark"],
["A073", 3, "`price` arrives as a string. Which code standardizes it to a decimal type in silver?",
 ["df.withColumn('price', col('price').cast('decimal(10,2)'))", "df.withColumnRenamed('price', 'decimal')", "df.select(explode('price'))", "df.fillna({'price': 'decimal'})"],
 "`cast('decimal(10,2)')` changes a column's data type. Inside `withColumn` with the same name, the decimal version replaces the string column.\n`withColumnRenamed('price', 'decimal')`: only renames the column to 'decimal'. The type stays string.\n`explode('price')`: turns each element of an array or map into its own row. It doesn't convert types and fails on a string column.\n`fillna({'price': 'decimal'})`: fills nulls with the literal text 'decimal'. It never changes the type.", "pyspark"],
["A074", 3, "Two DataFrames must be joined where both `store_id` and `sale_date` match. Which code?",
 ["a.join(b, ['store_id', 'sale_date'])", "a.join(b, 'store_id').join(b, 'sale_date')", "a.crossJoin(b)", "a.union(b)"],
 "Passing a list of column names to `join` matches rows where every listed column is equal, and keeps one copy of each key column in the result (an inner join by default).\n`.join(b, 'store_id').join(b, 'sale_date')`: two separate joins to `b`, each on one key. It doesn't require both keys to match on the same row of `b`, and it duplicates b's columns.\n`crossJoin`: pairs every row with every row, with no key matching.\n`union`: stacks the rows of two DataFrames. It doesn't join them.", "pyspark"],

// ---------- S4 Lakeflow Jobs ----------
["A075", 4, "Task B depends on task A, and nobody changed B's settings. When does B run?",
 ["Only if A succeeds (Run if = All succeeded)", "Whenever A finishes, success or failure", "Only if A fails", "At the same time as A"],
 "Every dependent task has a Run if condition, and the default is `All succeeded`: the task runs only when all its dependencies ran and succeeded.\n`Whenever A finishes, success or failure`: that's the `All done` condition, which you'd have to choose explicitly.\n`Only if A fails`: that's `At least one failed` or `All failed`, also not the default.\n`At the same time as A`: a dependent task always waits for its upstream task. Only tasks with no dependency between them run in parallel.", "runif"],
["A076", 4, "Task B depends on A with the default Run if condition. A fails. What status does B show?",
 ["Upstream failed", "Excluded", "Skipped", "Timed Out"],
 "With `All succeeded` (the default), and also `At least one succeeded` and `None failed`, an unmet condition marks the task `Upstream failed`.\n`Excluded`: the near miss. It's used when the failure-based conditions (`At least one failed`, `All failed`) aren't met, meaning upstream succeeded.\n`Skipped`: not the status the Run if docs give for an unmet success condition. B shows Upstream failed.\n`Timed Out`: only for a task that ran past its own timeout. B never started.", "runif"],
["A077", 4, "A cleanup task must run after its upstream tasks finish, whether they succeeded or failed. Which Run if condition?",
 ["All done", "All succeeded", "At least one failed", "None failed"],
 "`All done` runs once every dependency has finished, whatever the outcome, which is what a cleanup step needs.\n`All succeeded`: the default. Cleanup would not run (Upstream failed) whenever anything upstream fails.\n`At least one failed`: runs only after a failure, so cleanup would be Excluded on a fully successful run.\n`None failed`: runs only if no dependency failed, so cleanup would not run after a failure.", "runif"],
["A078", 4, "A notification task has Run if = 'At least one failed'. All its upstream tasks succeed. What status does it show?",
 ["Excluded", "Upstream failed", "Succeeded", "Timed Out"],
 "For the failure-based conditions, `At least one failed` and `All failed`, an unmet condition (nothing failed) marks the task `Excluded`, and it is skipped.\n`Upstream failed`: the near miss. It's the status for unmet success-based conditions like `All succeeded`, when something upstream actually failed.\n`Succeeded`: the task never ran, so it can't succeed.\n`Timed Out`: only applies to a task that ran past its timeout.", "runif"],
["A079", 4, "A new job is created with default settings. What is its maximum number of concurrent runs?",
 ["1", "5", "10", "Unlimited"],
 "`Maximum concurrent runs` defaults to 1, so a new job allows one active run at a time. Extra runs are queued if queueing is on, or skipped if it's off.\n`5`: a value you can set, but not the default.\n`10`: also allowed if you raise the setting, but not the default.\n`Unlimited`: there's no unlimited default. You have to raise the limit explicitly.", "cfg"],
["A080", 4, "Queueing is on for a job (the default for jobs created in the UI since April 2024). How long can a run wait in the queue?",
 ["Up to 48 hours", "Up to 1 hour", "Up to 7 days", "There's no limit"],
 "With queueing on, a run that can't start because of a concurrency limit waits in the queue for a maximum of 48 hours.\n`Up to 1 hour`: shorter than the documented limit.\n`Up to 7 days`: longer than the documented 48-hour maximum.\n`There's no limit`: queued runs don't wait forever. The cap is 48 hours.", "cfg"],
["A081", 4, "A For each task was created with default settings. How many iterations run at once?",
 ["1", "5", "10", "All of them"],
 "A For each task's concurrency setting defaults to 1, so iterations run one at a time in sequence. Raise it to run several iterations in parallel.\n`5`: only if you set concurrency to 5.\n`10`: only if you set concurrency to 10.\n`All of them`: running every iteration at once needs concurrency at least as large as the input list. The default is 1.", "foreach"],
["A082", 4, "Which statement about For each tasks is correct?",
 ["A For each task can't contain another For each task", "A For each task can only loop over integers", "Each iteration needs its own job", "For each tasks can't receive parameters"],
 "The nested task inside a For each can be most task types, but not another For each, so loops can't be nested directly.\n`Only loop over integers`: the input is a JSON array whose elements can be strings, numbers, Booleans or JSON objects.\n`Each iteration needs its own job`: all iterations run inside the one For each task in the same job run.\n`Can't receive parameters`: inputs can come from job parameters or upstream task values, and the nested task reads each item with `{{input}}`.", "foreach"],
["A083", 4, "Inside a For each task's nested notebook task, how do you refer to the current item?",
 ["{{input}}", "{{job.id}}", "{{item.value}}", "$ITEM"],
 "The nested task gets the current array element through the dynamic value `{{input}}`. For object elements, `{{input.<key>}}` reads one field.\n`{{job.id}}`: a real dynamic value reference, but it returns the job's ID, which is the same for every iteration.\n`{{item.value}}`: not a supported reference.\n`$ITEM`: not Databricks syntax. Dynamic value references use double curly braces.", "foreach"],
["A084", 4, "A task normally takes 20 minutes. The team wants an alert if it passes 30 minutes, but the task must keep running. What should they set?",
 ["A duration warning threshold of 30 minutes", "A timeout of 30 minutes", "Retries set to 30", "Max concurrent runs of 30"],
 "A duration warning threshold sends a notification when a run passes the set time but lets it keep running, which is exactly 'alert but continue'.\n`A timeout of 30 minutes`: the near miss. It would stop the task at 30 minutes and mark it Timed Out, which the team doesn't want.\n`Retries set to 30`: controls re-runs after a failure, not duration.\n`Max concurrent runs of 30`: limits how many runs of the job can be active at once.", "cfg"],
["A085", 4, "A task has a 30-minute timeout and runs for 31 minutes. What happens?",
 ["The run is stopped and the task shows Timed Out", "An alert is sent and the task keeps running", "The task is retried forever", "Nothing, timeouts only apply to clusters"],
 "A timeout is a hard limit: when a task runs past it, Databricks stops the run and the task shows Timed Out.\n`An alert is sent and the task keeps running`: that's a duration warning threshold, not a timeout.\n`Retried forever`: retries are a separate, finite setting.\n`Timeouts only apply to clusters`: a task timeout applies to the task run itself.", "cfg"],
["A086", 4, "How often does a file arrival trigger check for new files?",
 ["About every minute", "Every second", "Every 15 minutes", "Only when the job is run manually"],
 "File arrival triggers make a best-effort check for new files about every minute. Cloud storage performance can delay this.\n`Every second`: far more often than the trigger checks.\n`Every 15 minutes`: much slower than the documented roughly one-minute check.\n`Only when the job is run manually`: the point of the trigger is that it starts runs automatically when files land.", "fat"],
["A087", 4, "A file arrival trigger monitors a location that doesn't have file events enabled. What limit applies?",
 ["The location can hold at most 10,000 files", "There's no limit", "Only one file can arrive per hour", "Only DBFS paths are allowed"],
 "Without file events, the monitored location (or subpath) can hold up to 10,000 files, and at most 50 jobs per workspace can use file arrival triggers on such locations. File events remove the file limit.\n`There's no limit`: true only when file events are enabled on the location.\n`One file per hour`: not a limit. Many files can arrive, and the trigger checks about every minute.\n`Only DBFS paths`: DBFS isn't supported. The location must be a Unity Catalog volume or external location.", "fat"],
["A088", 4, "Which locations can a file arrival trigger monitor?",
 ["A Unity Catalog external location or volume", "Any HTTP URL", "Only DBFS root", "A local folder on the driver"],
 "File arrival triggers watch cloud storage governed by Unity Catalog: a volume or an external location. The workspace must have Unity Catalog enabled.\n`Any HTTP URL`: not a Unity Catalog storage location, so it can't be monitored.\n`Only DBFS root`: not supported. The path must be a UC volume or external location.\n`A local folder on the driver`: the driver is temporary compute, not storage the trigger can watch.", "fat"],
["A089", 4, "A table update trigger watches several tables, and file events are not enabled on them. How many tables can one trigger monitor?",
 ["Up to 10", "Only 1", "Up to 100", "Unlimited"],
 "A table update trigger can watch up to 10 tables without file events, firing when Any or All of them update. Current docs raise this to 25 if every monitored table has file events enabled.\n`Only 1`: one trigger can monitor several tables.\n`Up to 100`: well above the documented limit.\n`Unlimited`: there's a fixed cap on tables per trigger.", "tut"],
["A090", 4, "Another team writes to a source table in bursts of many small commits. The job should start once, after a burst ends. Which setting?",
 ["Wait after last change", "Minimum time between triggers", "Max concurrent runs", "Retry interval"],
 "`Wait after last change` holds the run until updates stop for the set time. Each new commit restarts the timer, so a burst of commits causes one run after it ends.\n`Minimum time between triggers`: the near miss. It enforces a gap after the previous run completes, but doesn't wait for the burst to finish, so a run can start mid-burst.\n`Max concurrent runs`: caps active runs, not when a run starts.\n`Retry interval`: the delay before re-running a failed task.", "tut"],
["A091", 4, "A table update trigger fires, and the job must not start again until 1 hour after the previous run finishes. Which setting?",
 ["Minimum time between triggers", "Wait after last change", "Duration warning threshold", "Timeout"],
 "`Minimum time between triggers` sets the shortest gap after the previous run completes before another can start. Updates during the gap trigger a run only once it expires.\n`Wait after last change`: the near miss. It waits for updates to stop, measured from the last table change, not from the end of the previous run.\n`Duration warning threshold`: alerts on long-running runs. It doesn't delay starts.\n`Timeout`: stops a run that takes too long.", "tut"],
["A092", 4, "A job's trigger is paused while a run is in progress. What happens?",
 ["The current run continues; no new runs start", "The current run is cancelled", "The current run is queued", "The job is deleted"],
 "Pausing a trigger only stops it from starting new runs. A run already in progress carries on to completion, and resuming the trigger restores its configured schedule.\n`The current run is cancelled`: you'd have to cancel it yourself. Pausing doesn't touch active runs.\n`The current run is queued`: queueing applies to new runs blocked by concurrency limits, not to one already running.\n`The job is deleted`: pausing keeps the job and all its settings.", "trig"],
["A093", 4, "Data from a vendor lands at unpredictable times. The job should run as soon as files arrive. Which trigger?",
 ["File arrival", "Scheduled", "Continuous", "Manual"],
 "A file arrival trigger starts a run when new files land in the monitored location. Data-driven triggers suit data that arrives at unpredictable times.\n`Scheduled`: runs at fixed times, so it either runs with no new data or starts long after files land.\n`Continuous`: keeps a run going at all times, starting a new one when the previous ends, whether or not files arrived.\n`Manual`: needs a person to start each run.", "trig"],
["A094", 4, "A finance report must run at 07:00 every weekday, regardless of data changes. Which trigger?",
 ["Scheduled", "File arrival", "Table update", "Continuous"],
 "A scheduled (time-based) trigger runs at set times, such as 07:00 Monday to Friday, regardless of what the data does.\n`File arrival`: runs when files land, so the timing depends on the data.\n`Table update`: runs when source tables change, the same problem.\n`Continuous`: runs back to back all the time, not at a fixed time.", "trig"],
["A095", 4, "Which statement about continuous jobs is correct?",
 ["A new run starts when the previous one ends, and failures are retried with exponential backoff", "They run once and stop", "They need a file arrival trigger", "They can't use retries"],
 "A continuous job keeps one run active: when a run ends, success or failure, a new one starts, and failures are retried with exponential backoff.\n`They run once and stop`: describes a one-off run, the opposite of continuous.\n`They need a file arrival trigger`: continuous is its own trigger type and can't be combined with other triggers.\n`They can't use retries`: failures are retried automatically, with exponential backoff.", "cf"],
["A096", 4, "A job must run task B when a value is above a threshold and task C otherwise. Which task type?",
 ["If/else condition", "For each", "Run job", "Dashboard"],
 "An If/else condition task evaluates a boolean expression, such as a task value compared with a threshold, and runs the tasks on its true or false branch.\n`For each`: repeats a nested task over a list of inputs. It doesn't choose between paths.\n`Run job`: triggers another job as a task, with no branching.\n`Dashboard`: refreshes a dashboard.", "cf"],
["A097", 4, "A new production job runs notebook tasks and serverless is enabled. What compute does a task get by default?",
 ["Serverless compute for jobs", "A shared all-purpose cluster", "A SQL warehouse", "The driver of the last cluster used"],
 "When serverless compute for jobs is enabled, new tasks that support it, such as notebook tasks, default to serverless, and Databricks manages the compute.\n`A shared all-purpose cluster`: you can choose one, but it isn't the default and isn't recommended for production jobs.\n`A SQL warehouse`: runs SQL tasks and dashboards. It isn't the default compute for a notebook task.\n`The driver of the last cluster used`: tasks don't reuse earlier compute like that.", "jc"],
["A098", 4, "A task fails because of a brief network glitch. What job setting re-runs it automatically?",
 ["Retries", "Max concurrent runs", "Duration warning threshold", "Queueing"],
 "Retries set how many times Databricks re-runs a failed task, with an optional interval between attempts. That covers short-lived errors like a network glitch.\n`Max concurrent runs`: limits parallel runs of the job. It doesn't re-run anything.\n`Duration warning threshold`: only alerts on long runs.\n`Queueing`: holds new runs that are waiting on a concurrency limit. It doesn't re-run failures.", "cf"],

// ---------- S5 CI/CD ----------
["A099", 5, "`databricks.yml` has targets dev (`default: true`), staging and prod. What does `databricks bundle deploy` with no `-t` use?",
 ["dev", "prod", "The first target in the file", "Nothing; the command fails"],
 "When `-t` is left out, the CLI uses the target marked `default: true`. Only one target can be the default.\n`prod`: used only when passed as `-t prod` or marked as the default.\n`The first target in the file`: file order doesn't decide this. The `default: true` flag does.\n`Nothing; the command fails`: there's a default target here (dev), so nothing is missing.", "bset"],
["A100", 5, "Which top-level mapping loads job definitions from `resources/*.yml` into the bundle?",
 ["include", "targets", "variables", "artifacts"],
 "`include` lists extra configuration files, with globs like `resources/*.yml` allowed, that are merged into the bundle. That lets resource definitions live in separate files.\n`targets`: defines deployment environments (dev, staging, prod) and their overrides, not which files are loaded.\n`variables`: declares custom variables.\n`artifacts`: defines build outputs, such as Python wheels, to build and deploy.", "bset"],
["A101", 5, "Which setting is required in every bundle configuration?",
 ["bundle: name:", "targets: prod:", "workspace: host:", "include:"],
 "`bundle.name` is the one required top-level setting. It names the bundle and is used in the default deployment path. Everything else is optional.\n`targets: prod:`: targets are optional, and no particular target name is required.\n`workspace: host:`: optional. The host can come from the CLI profile or environment.\n`include:`: optional. Only needed when the configuration is split across files.", "bset"],
["A102", 5, "A job's cluster size is set at top level and also under `targets: prod:`. Which value does a prod deploy use?",
 ["The prod target's value", "The top-level value", "The larger of the two", "The deploy fails with a conflict"],
 "Settings under a target are merged over the top-level settings. When both set the same key, the target's value wins for that deployment.\n`The top-level value`: it's the fallback, used only when the target doesn't set the key.\n`The larger of the two`: there's no comparison. The target value simply overrides.\n`The deploy fails with a conflict`: overriding is the intended behaviour, not an error.", "bset"],
["A103", 5, "A variable `catalog` has a default, a target value, and the deploy runs with `--var=\"catalog=qa\"`. Which value is used?",
 ["qa", "The target value", "The default", "The BUNDLE_VAR_catalog value if one exists"],
 "Bundle variable precedence, highest first: `--var`, then a `BUNDLE_VAR_` environment variable, then `variable-overrides.json`, then the target's value, then the default. `--var` wins, so the value is qa.\n`The BUNDLE_VAR_catalog value`: the near miss. It's second in precedence and loses to `--var`.\n`The target value`: outranks only the default.\n`The default`: lowest priority, used only when nothing else sets the variable.", "bvar"],
["A104", 5, "How can a CI system set the bundle variable `catalog` without editing files?",
 ["Set the environment variable BUNDLE_VAR_catalog", "Set DATABRICKS_CATALOG", "Edit .databrickscfg", "Use databricks bundle set catalog"],
 "An environment variable named `BUNDLE_VAR_<variable name>`, here `BUNDLE_VAR_catalog`, sets a bundle variable. That suits CI because no file is edited.\n`DATABRICKS_CATALOG`: not how bundle variables are set. They need the `BUNDLE_VAR_` prefix.\n`Edit .databrickscfg`: that file holds CLI authentication profiles, not bundle variable values, and editing it is editing a file.\n`databricks bundle set catalog`: there's no such command.", "bvar"],
["A105", 5, "A job needs the ID of a warehouse named 'Analytics WH', which differs in every workspace. Which bundle feature?",
 ["A variable with lookup: warehouse:", "A hard-coded ID per target", "mode: development", "include:"],
 "A variable with `lookup` resolves an object's name, here the warehouse 'Analytics WH', to its ID in the target workspace at deploy time, so one configuration works everywhere.\n`A hard-coded ID per target`: works, but it's what lookup avoids. You'd find and maintain each workspace's ID by hand.\n`mode: development`: changes naming and pauses schedules. It doesn't resolve IDs.\n`include:`: only merges configuration files.", "bvar"],
["A106", 5, "A target uses `mode: development`. What happens to deployed resource names?",
 ["They get a [dev <username>] prefix", "They get a _prod suffix", "Nothing changes", "They're replaced with random IDs"],
 "`mode: development` adds a `[dev <username>]` prefix to deployed resource names so each developer's copies don't collide. It also pauses schedules and triggers.\n`A _prod suffix`: no deployment mode adds this.\n`Nothing changes`: describes production mode, not development.\n`Random IDs`: names stay readable, just prefixed.", "bdm"],
["A107", 5, "In `mode: development`, what happens to job schedules and triggers by default?",
 ["They're paused", "They run normally", "They're deleted", "They run every minute"],
 "Development mode pauses schedules and triggers on deployed jobs so dev copies don't run on their own. A single resource can opt back in with `pause_status: UNPAUSED`.\n`They run normally`: that's production mode.\n`They're deleted`: they stay in the deployed job, just paused.\n`They run every minute`: nothing changes a schedule's timing.", "bdm"],
["A108", 5, "A developer wants one job's schedule to run in their `mode: development` deployment. What should they set?",
 ["pause_status: UNPAUSED on that schedule", "mode: production", "default: true", "max_concurrent_runs: 1"],
 "Setting `pause_status: UNPAUSED` explicitly on that schedule overrides development mode's pausing for that one resource, and the rest of the dev behaviour stays.\n`mode: production`: too broad. It switches the whole target to production behaviour and checks, not just one schedule.\n`default: true`: only picks which target is used when `-t` is left out.\n`max_concurrent_runs: 1`: limits parallel runs, nothing to do with pausing.", "bdm"],
["A109", 5, "A target has `mode: production` and `git: branch: main`. The engineer deploys from a feature branch. What happens?",
 ["Validation fails because the branch doesn't match, unless --force is used", "The deploy succeeds and pauses schedules", "The CLI switches the branch to main", "Resources get a [dev] prefix"],
 "Production mode checks that the current Git branch matches the target's `git.branch`. Deploying from a feature branch fails validation unless you override it with `--force`.\n`The deploy succeeds and pauses schedules`: pausing is development mode behaviour, and the branch check fails first.\n`The CLI switches the branch to main`: the CLI never changes your Git checkout.\n`Resources get a [dev] prefix`: that's development mode only.", "bdm"],
["A110", 5, "Which identity does Databricks recommend for `run_as` in a production bundle target?",
 ["A service principal", "The developer's own user", "The workspace admin", "An anonymous user"],
 "Databricks recommends that production targets run as a service principal, so jobs don't depend on one person's account and permissions.\n`The developer's own user`: ties production to an individual, and breaks if they leave or lose access.\n`The workspace admin`: still a human identity, and an over-privileged one.\n`An anonymous user`: not a valid `run_as` identity.", "bdm"],
["A111", 5, "Which command checks a bundle's configuration for errors without deploying anything?",
 ["databricks bundle validate", "databricks bundle deploy --dry", "databricks bundle run", "databricks bundle destroy"],
 "`databricks bundle validate` checks the bundle configuration and reports errors without changing anything in the workspace.\n`databricks bundle deploy --dry`: deploy is the command that writes to the workspace. validate is the check-only command.\n`databricks bundle run`: runs a deployed job or pipeline.\n`databricks bundle destroy`: removes deployed resources.", "bun"],
["A112", 5, "Which command removes the jobs, pipelines and files a bundle deployed to a target?",
 ["databricks bundle destroy -t <target>", "databricks bundle delete", "databricks bundle validate --clean", "databricks workspace rm"],
 "`databricks bundle destroy -t <target>` removes the jobs, pipelines and files the bundle deployed to that target.\n`databricks bundle delete`: not a bundle command. destroy is the removal command.\n`databricks bundle validate --clean`: validate only checks configuration and changes nothing.\n`databricks workspace rm`: workspace commands deal with individual notebooks and files, not the jobs and pipelines a bundle tracks.", "bun"],
["A113", 5, "What is the current name for Databricks Repos?",
 ["Databricks Git folders", "Databricks Projects", "Lakeflow Repos", "Workspace Git"],
 "Databricks Repos was renamed Git folders. From the workspace UI you can clone a repo, create and switch branches, commit, push and open pull requests.\n`Databricks Projects`: not the name of the Git integration.\n`Lakeflow Repos`: Lakeflow is the brand for ingestion, pipelines and jobs, not Git integration.\n`Workspace Git`: not a product name.", "git"],
["A114", 5, "What is the current name for Databricks Asset Bundles?",
 ["Declarative Automation Bundles", "Lakeflow Bundles", "Databricks Deploy Packs", "Unity Bundles"],
 "Databricks Asset Bundles were renamed Declarative Automation Bundles. The CLI didn't change: commands are still `databricks bundle ...`.\n`Lakeflow Bundles`: Lakeflow names Connect, pipelines and Jobs, not bundles.\n`Databricks Deploy Packs`: not a Databricks product.\n`Unity Bundles`: Unity is the governance brand (Unity Catalog), not deployment.", "bun"],

// ---------- S6 Optimize & troubleshoot ----------
["A115", 6, "In the Spark UI, a stage's 75th percentile task time is 15 seconds and the maximum is 12 minutes. Shuffle read size shows the same pattern. What is the likely cause?",
 ["Data skew: a few partitions are far bigger than the rest", "Too many executors", "The driver is out of memory", "Photon is turned off"],
 "Skew means one or a few tasks take much longer than the rest. The Spark UI guide's rule: in Summary Metrics, if Max task duration is 50% or more above the 75th percentile, suspect skew. Shuffle read size showing the same gap points to a few oversized partitions.\n`Too many executors`: wouldn't make a handful of tasks vastly slower than the rest.\n`The driver is out of memory`: would fail the job, not stretch a few executor tasks.\n`Photon is turned off`: would slow tasks broadly, not just a few with huge shuffle reads.", "aqe"],
["A116", 6, "A stage shows a large 'Spill (Disk)' value. What does that indicate?",
 ["Partitions didn't fit in executor memory and were written to disk", "The output table is too small", "The cluster has too few drivers", "The checkpoint is corrupted"],
 "Spill happens when Spark runs low on execution memory during shuffles, joins, sorts or aggregations and writes data to disk. The Spark UI guide suggests `spark.sql.shuffle.partitions=auto`, holding less data in memory, or instances with more memory.\n`The output table is too small`: spill is about memory during processing, not output size.\n`The cluster has too few drivers`: a cluster has one driver, and spill is about execution memory for tasks.\n`The checkpoint is corrupted`: that causes streaming errors, not spill.", "aqe"],
["A117", 6, "A notebook calls `df.collect()` on a 200 GB DataFrame and fails with an out-of-memory error. Where did memory run out?",
 ["On the driver", "On the SQL warehouse", "In the Delta log", "In cloud storage"],
 "`collect()` returns every row to the driver as local objects, so 200 GB exceeds driver memory. Write the result to a table, or use `limit()` to bring back a small sample.\n`On the SQL warehouse`: a Python notebook calling `collect()` runs on cluster compute. No warehouse is involved.\n`In the Delta log`: the log is transaction metadata in storage. It doesn't hold memory.\n`In cloud storage`: storage doesn't run out of memory. The problem is where the rows were sent.", "pyspark"],
["A118", 6, "What is the maximum number of liquid clustering keys on a table?",
 ["4", "1", "8", "32"],
 "Liquid clustering accepts up to 4 clustering keys in `CLUSTER BY`. Choose the columns most often used in filters.\n`1`: one key is allowed, but it isn't the maximum.\n`8`: above the 4-key limit.\n`32`: above the limit. 32 is the default number of columns that collect statistics, not a clustering key limit.", "lc"],
["A119", 6, "Clustering keys on a table are changed with `ALTER TABLE ... CLUSTER BY (region, order_date)`. What happens to existing data?",
 ["Nothing until OPTIMIZE ... FULL reclusters it", "It's rewritten immediately", "It's deleted", "It's converted to partitions"],
 "Changing clustering keys is a metadata change: existing data isn't rewritten. New writes and later `OPTIMIZE` runs use the new keys, and `OPTIMIZE ... FULL` reclusters all existing records.\n`It's rewritten immediately`: the ALTER only updates the table's metadata.\n`It's deleted`: changing keys never removes data.\n`It's converted to partitions`: liquid clustering doesn't use partitions.", "lc"],
["A120", 6, "Which statement about liquid clustering is correct?",
 ["It can't be combined with partitioning or ZORDER", "It needs ZORDER to work", "It only works on external tables", "It requires at least 4 keys"],
 "Liquid clustering replaces Hive-style partitioning and ZORDER. A table uses liquid clustering or those, not both.\n`It needs ZORDER to work`: the two can't be combined.\n`It only works on external tables`: it works on Delta tables generally, and UC managed tables are recommended.\n`It requires at least 4 keys`: 4 is the maximum. One key is fine.", "lc"],
["A121", 6, "A team wants Databricks to choose and adjust clustering keys automatically. Which clause?",
 ["CLUSTER BY AUTO", "CLUSTER BY NONE", "ZORDER BY AUTO", "PARTITIONED BY AUTO"],
 "`CLUSTER BY AUTO` turns on automatic liquid clustering: Databricks picks keys from the query workload and adjusts them over time. It needs predictive optimization.\n`CLUSTER BY NONE`: the near miss. It removes clustering keys rather than choosing them.\n`ZORDER BY AUTO`: no such syntax. ZORDER needs explicit columns.\n`PARTITIONED BY AUTO`: no such syntax.", "lc"],
["A122", 6, "Which tables can predictive optimization maintain?",
 ["Unity Catalog managed tables only", "External tables only", "All tables, including Hive metastore", "Only streaming tables"],
 "Predictive optimization runs maintenance only on Unity Catalog managed tables, where Databricks controls the storage and layout.\n`External tables only`: external tables are excluded.\n`All tables, including Hive metastore`: Hive metastore and external tables aren't covered.\n`Only streaming tables`: it covers UC managed tables generally, not only streaming tables.", "po"],
["A123", 6, "Which operations does predictive optimization run automatically?",
 ["OPTIMIZE, VACUUM and ANALYZE", "ZORDER only", "VACUUM and RESTORE", "OPTIMIZE FULL only"],
 "Predictive optimization automatically runs `OPTIMIZE` (without ZORDER), `VACUUM` and `ANALYZE` on serverless compute, deciding when each is worth running.\n`ZORDER only`: it doesn't run ZORDER at all.\n`VACUUM and RESTORE`: RESTORE rolls a table back and is never automatic.\n`OPTIMIZE FULL only`: it runs regular OPTIMIZE plus VACUUM and ANALYZE.", "po"],
["A124", 6, "At which levels can predictive optimization be enabled, disabled or inherited?",
 ["Catalog, schema or table", "Cluster only", "Notebook only", "Column only"],
 "Predictive optimization is set with `ENABLE`, `DISABLE` or `INHERIT` on a catalog, schema or table. Objects inherit their parent's setting unless overridden.\n`Cluster only`: it runs on serverless compute and isn't a cluster setting.\n`Notebook only`: it's a setting on data objects, not code.\n`Column only`: it applies to whole tables, not individual columns.", "po"],
["A125", 1, "An all-purpose cluster terminated 5 weeks ago has disappeared from the compute list. Nobody deleted it. Why?",
 ["Terminated compute is deleted 30 days after termination unless it's pinned", "Auto-termination deletes clusters immediately", "Predictive optimization removed it", "Serverless replaced it"],
 "Databricks keeps a terminated compute's configuration for 30 days, then deletes it. To keep one longer, an admin pins it (up to 100 pinned).\n`Auto-termination deletes clusters immediately`: auto-termination only stops the cluster. It can be restarted within those 30 days.\n`Predictive optimization removed it`: that maintains tables, not compute.\n`Serverless replaced it`: serverless doesn't remove existing clusters.", "cm"],
["A126", 6, "A job's runs have been getting slower over two weeks. Where do you compare durations against the baseline?",
 ["The job's run history in the Lakeflow Jobs UI", "The cluster's event log only", "Catalog Explorer lineage", "The Git folder history"],
 "A job's run history in the Jobs UI lists each run's start time, duration and status, so you can compare recent durations with earlier ones and spot a trend.\n`The cluster's event log only`: shows lifecycle events (start, resize, terminate) for one cluster, not run durations across runs.\n`Catalog Explorer lineage`: shows how tables and columns are derived.\n`The Git folder history`: shows code commits, not run times.", "cfg"],

// ---------- S7 Governance ----------
["A127", 7, "A data engineer creates a table. Nobody changes anything afterwards. Who owns it?",
 ["The engineer who created it", "The schema owner", "The metastore admin", "Nobody until assigned"],
 "In Unity Catalog the principal that creates an object becomes its owner, and ownership only changes if someone transfers it.\n`The schema owner`: owns the schema, not each table in it. Ownership doesn't extend to child objects.\n`The metastore admin`: can manage objects but doesn't automatically own them.\n`Nobody until assigned`: every object has exactly one owner from the moment it's created.", "perm"],
["A128", 7, "`GRANT SELECT ON SCHEMA main.sales TO analysts` is run. A new table is added to main.sales next week. Does the grant cover it?",
 ["Yes; schema grants apply to existing and future tables", "No; it only covers tables that existed at grant time", "Only after a metastore admin refreshes grants", "Only if the table owner opts in"],
 "Unity Catalog privileges are inherited: a grant on a catalog or schema applies to all current and future child objects, so the new table is covered.\n`No; only tables that existed at grant time`: inheritance covers future child objects too.\n`Only after a metastore admin refreshes grants`: no refresh step exists.\n`Only if the table owner opts in`: inheritance is automatic.", "perm"],
["A129", 7, "What does a user need to read `main.sales.orders`?",
 ["USE CATALOG on main, USE SCHEMA on main.sales, and SELECT on the table", "SELECT on the table only", "USE CATALOG and SELECT only", "MODIFY on the table"],
 "Reading a table needs `SELECT` on it plus `USE CATALOG` on its catalog and `USE SCHEMA` on its schema. The usage privileges are what let you reach the table.\n`SELECT on the table only`: missing both usage privileges, so the query fails.\n`USE CATALOG and SELECT only`: still missing `USE SCHEMA`.\n`MODIFY on the table`: allows writing (insert, update, delete), not reading, and still lacks the usage privileges.", "perm"],
["A130", 7, "A data steward must grant and revoke access on a catalog without being able to read its data. Which privilege?",
 ["MANAGE", "ALL PRIVILEGES", "Ownership", "SELECT"],
 "`MANAGE` lets a principal grant and revoke privileges, transfer ownership and drop an object without giving data access itself. Several principals can hold it.\n`ALL PRIVILEGES`: includes data access like SELECT and, to prevent escalation, doesn't include MANAGE.\n`Ownership`: the owner has every capability, including reading the data, and there's only one owner.\n`SELECT`: reads data and gives no ability to grant.", "perm"],
["A131", 7, "An admin wants to stop one group reading a table it was granted. In Unity Catalog SQL, what do they use?",
 ["REVOKE SELECT ON TABLE t FROM group", "DENY SELECT ON TABLE t TO group", "DROP GRANT", "ALTER TABLE t DENY"],
 "Unity Catalog doesn't support the SQL `DENY` statement (its ABAC DENY policies, in Beta, can only deny `MANAGE ACCESS CONTROL`, not `SELECT`). Access comes only from grants, so to remove it you `REVOKE` the privilege from the group.\n`DENY SELECT ON TABLE t TO group`: works only in the legacy Hive metastore, not Unity Catalog.\n`DROP GRANT`: not valid SQL.\n`ALTER TABLE t DENY`: not valid syntax.", "deny"],
["A132", 7, "Which statement grants read access on a table to a group?",
 ["GRANT SELECT ON TABLE main.sales.orders TO `analysts`", "GRANT READ ON main.sales.orders FOR analysts", "ALLOW SELECT main.sales.orders analysts", "SET PERMISSION SELECT TO analysts"],
 "The grant syntax is `GRANT <privilege> ON <securable type> <name> TO <principal>`. The read privilege on a table is SELECT, and backticks quote group names.\n`GRANT READ ON ... FOR analysts`: READ isn't a table privilege (SELECT is), and the principal follows TO, not FOR.\n`ALLOW SELECT ...`: not SQL.\n`SET PERMISSION SELECT TO analysts`: not SQL.", "perm"],
["A133", 7, "A managed table is dropped. What happens to its data?",
 ["It is deleted, but the table can be recovered with UNDROP for 7 days by default", "The files stay in place forever", "It moves to an external location", "It's deleted immediately with no recovery"],
 "Dropping a managed table drops its data too, because Unity Catalog manages the files. For 7 days by default you can recover it with `UNDROP TABLE`. After that the data is permanently removed.\n`The files stay in place forever`: describes dropping an external table.\n`It moves to an external location`: dropping never moves data.\n`Deleted immediately with no recovery`: ignores the UNDROP window.", "managed"],
["A134", 7, "An external table is dropped. What happens to its data files?",
 ["They stay in the external location; only the metadata is removed", "They are deleted after 7 days", "They are deleted immediately", "They move to managed storage"],
 "For an external table Unity Catalog manages only the metadata, so `DROP TABLE` removes the table definition and leaves the data files in the external location.\n`Deleted after 7 days`: that's the managed table UNDROP window.\n`Deleted immediately`: Unity Catalog doesn't delete external data on drop.\n`They move to managed storage`: dropping doesn't move files.", "managed"],
["A135", 7, "A managed table was dropped 3 days ago with default settings. How can it be recovered?",
 ["UNDROP TABLE", "RESTORE TABLE ... VERSION AS OF", "CREATE TABLE ... CLONE", "It can't be recovered"],
 "`UNDROP TABLE` restores a dropped managed table within the retention window, 7 days by default. 3 days is inside it.\n`RESTORE TABLE ... VERSION AS OF`: the near miss. It rolls back a table that still exists. It can't act on a dropped one.\n`CREATE TABLE ... CLONE`: copies an existing source table, and the source is gone.\n`It can't be recovered`: true only after the 7-day window.", "undrop"],
["A136", 7, "Which command converts an external table to a managed table?",
 ["ALTER TABLE ... SET MANAGED", "CONVERT TO DELTA", "ALTER TABLE ... UNSET EXTERNAL", "CREATE TABLE ... AS SELECT only"],
 "`ALTER TABLE ... SET MANAGED` converts an external table to a Unity Catalog managed table, moving its data into managed storage. It needs serverless or DBR 17.3 LTS+.\n`CONVERT TO DELTA`: converts Parquet files in place into a Delta table. It doesn't make the table managed.\n`ALTER TABLE ... UNSET EXTERNAL`: no such command. `UNSET MANAGED` is the rollback.\n`CREATE TABLE ... AS SELECT only`: CTAS copies data into a new table, losing its history. SET MANAGED converts the existing table.", "conv"],
["A137", 7, "A table was converted with `SET MANAGED` 9 days ago. Can the conversion be undone?",
 ["Yes, with UNSET MANAGED within 14 days, on serverless or DBR 17.3 LTS+", "No, conversion is permanent", "Yes, within 90 days", "Only by dropping and recreating the table"],
 "`UNSET MANAGED` rolls back a `SET MANAGED` conversion within 14 days, while the original data is still kept in the external location. It needs serverless or DBR 17.3 LTS+. 9 days is inside the window.\n`No, conversion is permanent`: rollback is possible for 14 days.\n`Yes, within 90 days`: the window is 14 days.\n`Only by dropping and recreating the table`: unnecessary. UNSET MANAGED does it.", "conv"],
["A138", 7, "A column mask must show real values only to members of the account group `pii_readers`, including nested members. Which function?",
 ["is_account_group_member('pii_readers')", "current_user() = 'pii_readers'", "session_user() LIKE '%pii%'", "current_catalog() = 'pii_readers'"],
 "`is_account_group_member('pii_readers')` returns true if the current user is a direct or indirect (nested) member of that account-level group, which is what a mask condition needs.\n`current_user() = 'pii_readers'`: returns the user's own name, never a group name.\n`session_user() LIKE '%pii%'`: also the user's name, so it matches usernames containing 'pii', not group members.\n`current_catalog() = 'pii_readers'`: returns the current catalog's name, nothing about who is querying.", "fm"],
["A139", 7, "Users in the EU group should only see rows where `region = 'EU'`. Which feature?",
 ["A row filter function on the table", "A column mask", "A view with DENY", "A separate catalog per user"],
 "A row filter is a SQL UDF attached to a table and evaluated for each row at query time. Returning true only where `region = 'EU'` for the EU group hides the other rows.\n`A column mask`: the near miss. It changes a column's values, but every row still comes back.\n`A view with DENY`: Unity Catalog has no SQL DENY.\n`A separate catalog per user`: duplicates data and doesn't scale. It isn't a filtering feature.", "fm"],
["A140", 7, "The `ssn` column must show '***' to everyone except the HR group. Which feature?",
 ["A column mask", "A row filter", "REVOKE SELECT on the column", "Liquid clustering"],
 "A column mask is a SQL UDF attached to a column. It takes the value and returns either the real value or a replacement like '***', based on who is querying, for example with `is_account_group_member('HR')`.\n`A row filter`: hides whole rows. The ssn values in the rows that remain are unchanged.\n`REVOKE SELECT on the column`: Unity Catalog grants work on tables, not columns, and blocking access isn't showing '***'.\n`Liquid clustering`: data layout for performance, not security.", "fm"],
["A141", 7, "At which levels can ABAC row filter and column mask policies be attached?",
 ["Catalog, schema or table (metastore level is in Beta)", "Column only", "Workspace only", "Cluster policy level"],
 "ABAC policies are attached to a level of the Unity Catalog hierarchy: a catalog, schema or table, with metastore-level attachment in Beta. The policy then applies to every matching object beneath that level.\n`Column only`: columns are what a policy matches (through their governed tags), not where the policy is attached.\n`Workspace only`: ABAC is part of Unity Catalog, which sits above workspaces; it isn't attached per workspace.\n`Cluster policy level`: cluster policies control compute settings, not data access.", "abac"],
["A142", 7, "A governance team wants one policy to mask every column tagged 'pii' across a catalog. What do ABAC policies match on?",
 ["Governed tags", "Column names", "Table owners", "File paths"],
 "ABAC policy conditions use attributes, and those attributes are governed tags. Tag the columns 'pii', attach one policy at the catalog, and it applies to every tagged column in scope, including ones tagged later.\n`Column names`: a name match would miss differently named PII columns; ABAC targets tags, not names.\n`Table owners`: ownership decides who manages an object, not which data a policy protects.\n`File paths`: storage paths are an external location concern, not how ABAC selects data.", "abac"],
["A143", 7, "A privilege is granted at the metastore level. Does it pass down to tables?",
 ["No; apart from `READ METADATA`, metastore-level grants don't pass down to data objects", "Yes, to every table", "Only to managed tables", "Only to views"],
 "Privileges inherit downward from catalogs and schemas, but grants on the metastore do not inherit to child objects. Metastore grants control metastore-scoped operations such as `CREATE CATALOG` and `CREATE EXTERNAL LOCATION`. (The one exception is `READ METADATA`, which does inherit from the metastore.)\n`Yes, to every table`: that is how a catalog-level grant behaves, not a metastore-level one.\n`Only to managed tables`: inheritance never depends on managed vs external.\n`Only to views`: same; the object type isn't what decides it.", "perm"],
];

