BANK.push(
// ---------- S1 Platform ----------
["A144", 1, "A Delta table uses default retention settings. Can you reliably time travel to a version from 20 days ago?",
 ["No: the history entry still exists, but VACUUM may have removed that version's data files after 7 days", "Yes: history is kept for 30 days, so any version in that window works", "Yes: time travel has no limit on managed tables", "No: history is only kept for 24 hours"],
 "Time travel needs two things: the log entry for the version (kept 30 days by default via `delta.logRetentionDuration`) and the data files it points to. VACUUM removes unreferenced files older than 7 days by default (`delta.deletedFileRetentionDuration`), so a 20-day-old version may have no files left.\n`Yes: history is kept for 30 days`: the history is there, but the data files may not be.\n`No limit on managed tables`: managed tables follow the same retention rules, and predictive optimization can run VACUUM for you.\n`Only 24 hours`: no default retention is that short.", "hist"],
["A145", 1, "A team wants a managed Iceberg table in Unity Catalog. What must they add to `CREATE TABLE`?",
 ["USING iceberg", "LOCATION 's3://...'", "TBLPROPERTIES ('format' = 'iceberg') only", "Nothing, Iceberg is the default"],
 "Delta is the default table format, so a managed Iceberg table needs an explicit `USING iceberg` in `CREATE TABLE`.\n`LOCATION 's3://...'`: adding a location makes the table external, not managed, and doesn't change the format.\n`TBLPROPERTIES ('format' = 'iceberg')`: the format is set by the `USING` clause, not by a table property.\n`Nothing, Iceberg is the default`: Delta is the default; Iceberg must be requested.", "managed"],
["A146", 1, "A new SQL warehouse is created and serverless is enabled for the account. What type is it by default?",
 ["Serverless", "Pro", "Classic", "Dedicated"],
 "When serverless is enabled for the account, a new SQL warehouse is serverless by default. You only get Pro or Classic if you choose them.\n`Pro`: a valid warehouse type, but you have to choose it.\n`Classic`: also a valid type you select manually; it isn't the default.\n`Dedicated`: that is a compute access mode for clusters (formerly Single user), not a SQL warehouse type.", "wh"],
["A147", 1, "A new SQL warehouse is created with default sizing and scaling. What are its cluster count settings?",
 ["Minimum 1, maximum 1", "Minimum 1, maximum 10", "Minimum 0, maximum 5", "Minimum 2, maximum 2"],
 "A new SQL warehouse defaults to size X-Large with scaling set to a minimum and maximum of one cluster. You raise the maximum to let it scale out for concurrency.\n`Minimum 1, maximum 10`: a common setting for busy warehouses, but you have to set it; the default max is 1.\n`Minimum 0, maximum 5`: not the default; idle cost is handled by auto stop.\n`Minimum 2, maximum 2`: not the default.", "wh"],
["A148", 1, "A data scientist needs to run R notebooks on Unity Catalog compute. Which access mode must the compute use?",
 ["Dedicated", "Standard", "Standard, with an R library installed on the compute", "Serverless SQL"],
 "R is supported only in Dedicated access mode (formerly Single user). Standard access mode supports Python, SQL and Scala, but not R.\n`Standard`: the shared, multi-user mode; it doesn't run R.\n`Standard, with an R library installed on the compute`: installing libraries doesn't add language support; Standard access mode still can't run R.\n`Serverless SQL`: a SQL warehouse runs SQL only, not R notebooks.", "ccfg"],
["A149", 1, "What privilege does `RESTORE TABLE` need on the table?",
 ["MODIFY", "SELECT only", "MANAGE", "USE SCHEMA only"],
 "`RESTORE TABLE` rewrites the table's current state back to an earlier version, which is a write, so it needs `MODIFY` on the table.\n`SELECT only`: enough to read old versions with time travel, but not to change the table.\n`MANAGE`: lets you manage privileges and ownership-type actions on the object; it isn't the data-write privilege RESTORE checks.\n`USE SCHEMA only`: needed to reach objects in the schema, but grants no rights on the table itself.", "hist"],
["A150", 1, "How many all-purpose compute resources can be pinned so they aren't deleted 30 days after termination?",
 ["Up to 100", "Up to 10", "Unlimited", "Only 1 per user"],
 "Terminated all-purpose compute is permanently deleted 30 days after termination unless it is pinned. Pinning keeps the configuration, and up to 100 compute resources can be pinned in a workspace.\n`Up to 10`: too low; the limit is 100.\n`Unlimited`: there is a cap of 100.\n`Only 1 per user`: the limit is per workspace, not per user, and pinning is an admin action.", "cm"],

// ---------- S2 Ingestion ----------
["A151", 2, "What is the current name for the pipeline API that used to be called `APPLY CHANGES`?",
 ["AUTO CDC", "MERGE STREAM", "CHANGE FEED INTO", "APPLY UPSERTS"],
 "`AUTO CDC` is the current name for the pipeline CDC APIs (`AUTO CDC INTO`, `create_auto_cdc_flow`). It replaces `APPLY CHANGES` with the same syntax, and the old names still work.\n`MERGE STREAM`: not a Databricks API; MERGE is the plain Delta statement you'd hand-write instead.\n`CHANGE FEED INTO`: not an API; change data feed is a Delta table feature that produces CDC rows.\n`APPLY UPSERTS`: not an API name.", "cdc"],
["A152", 2, "A source database has no CDC feed; you only get a full snapshot each day. Which pipeline API turns snapshots into changes?",
 ["AUTO CDC FROM SNAPSHOT", "AUTO CDC", "COPY INTO", "Auto Loader with rescue mode"],
 "`AUTO CDC FROM SNAPSHOT` compares snapshots taken in order to work out inserts, updates and deletes (keys missing from a snapshot are treated as deleted). It's the tool when the source gives full snapshots, not a change feed.\n`AUTO CDC`: needs a feed of change events with a sequence column; a snapshot isn't one.\n`COPY INTO`: loads files idempotently but doesn't derive changes.\n`Auto Loader with rescue mode`: rescue mode handles schema drift, not change detection.", "cdc"],
["A153", 2, "In an `AUTO CDC` flow, what is `SEQUENCE BY` (Python: `sequence_by`) used for?",
 ["Ordering change events so the latest change for each key wins", "Choosing the primary key", "Partitioning the target table", "Setting the refresh schedule"],
 "`SEQUENCE BY` gives the order of change events, so for each key the latest change wins even if events arrive out of order. `KEYS` says which record a change belongs to. NULL sequence values aren't supported.\n`Choosing the primary key`: that's `KEYS`.\n`Partitioning the target table`: unrelated; layout is set with table clustering or partitioning, not sequencing.\n`Setting the refresh schedule`: schedules come from the pipeline mode or a job, not the flow definition.", "cdc"],
["A154", 2, "An `AUTO CDC` SCD type 1 target receives an update with sequence 5 after the update with sequence 6 has already been applied. What happens?",
 ["The older update (sequence 5) is ignored", "It overwrites the newer value", "The pipeline fails", "Both versions are kept"],
 "AUTO CDC orders changes by the `SEQUENCE BY` column. Once sequence 6 is applied, a late event with sequence 5 is older, so it's dropped and the newer value stays.\n`It overwrites the newer value`: that's what naive arrival-order processing would do, and exactly what sequencing prevents.\n`The pipeline fails`: out-of-order data is expected and handled, not an error.\n`Both versions are kept`: SCD type 1 keeps one row per key; keeping versions is SCD type 2.", "cdc"],
["A155", 2, "In an SCD type 2 table built by `AUTO CDC`, how do you find the current version of each record?",
 ["Rows where __END_AT is null", "Rows where __START_AT is null", "The row with the lowest sequence value", "Rows where _rescued_data is null"],
 "In an SCD type 2 target, each version has `__START_AT` and `__END_AT`. When a newer version arrives, the old row gets an `__END_AT`, so the current version is the row where `__END_AT` is null.\n`__START_AT is null`: every version has a start; this column is never null for valid rows.\n`Lowest sequence value`: that's the oldest version, not the current one.\n`_rescued_data is null`: that's an Auto Loader column for unparsed data, unrelated to versioning.", "cdc"],
["A156", 2, "A CDC feed marks deletes with `operation = 'DELETE'`. How does an `AUTO CDC` flow apply them?",
 ["APPLY AS DELETE WHEN operation = 'DELETE' (Python: apply_as_deletes)", "They are applied automatically with no setting", "WHERE operation != 'DELETE'", "A separate VACUUM step"],
 "AUTO CDC treats every row as an upsert unless you say which rows are deletes. `APPLY AS DELETE WHEN operation = 'DELETE'` (Python `apply_as_deletes`) tells it to remove the key instead.\n`Applied automatically`: without the clause, a delete row would be upserted like any other change.\n`WHERE operation != 'DELETE'`: filters deletes out, so the record would never be removed from the target.\n`A separate VACUUM step`: VACUUM removes old data files, not logical rows.", "cdc"],
["A157", 2, "An `AUTO CDC` SCD type 2 target should not create new versions when only `last_login` changes. Which option?",
 ["TRACK HISTORY ON * EXCEPT (last_login)", "STORED AS SCD TYPE 1", "SEQUENCE BY last_login", "KEYS (last_login)"],
 "SCD type 2 creates a new version whenever any column changes. `TRACK HISTORY ON * EXCEPT (last_login)` excludes that column, so a change only to `last_login` updates the row in place without a new version.\n`STORED AS SCD TYPE 1`: stops history for every column, not just `last_login`.\n`SEQUENCE BY last_login`: changes how events are ordered, not which columns create versions.\n`KEYS (last_login)`: would make the login time the record's identity, which is wrong.", "cdc"],
["A158", 2, "A new Lakeflow Connect managed ingestion pipeline runs for the first time. What does it ingest?",
 ["All of the selected data from the source; later runs ingest only changes where the source supports it", "Only rows changed in the last 24 hours", "Only the schema, with no data", "Nothing until a CDC feed is enabled"],
 "The first run of a managed ingestion pipeline is a full load of all selected data. Later runs use change tracking to pull only what changed since the last run, where the source supports it.\n`Last 24 hours`: there's no fixed time window; the first run loads everything.\n`Only the schema`: tables are created and populated on the first run.\n`Nothing until a CDC feed is enabled`: some database sources need change tracking set up, but the connector still does an initial full load.", "lfc"],
["A159", 2, "Which component do Lakeflow Connect database (CDC) connectors use that SaaS connectors don't?",
 ["An ingestion gateway and staging storage", "A SQL warehouse", "An all-purpose cluster", "An Auto Loader checkpoint"],
 "Database (CDC) connectors use an ingestion gateway, which runs continuously in its own job, to capture changes into staging storage that the ingestion pipeline then reads. SaaS connectors call the app's API directly and don't need this.\n`A SQL warehouse`: not part of the connector architecture.\n`An all-purpose cluster`: managed ingestion runs on pipeline compute, not an interactive cluster.\n`An Auto Loader checkpoint`: Auto Loader is for files in cloud storage, not database CDC.", "lfc"],
["A160", 2, "How does Lakeflow Connect run a managed ingestion pipeline on a schedule?",
 ["It creates a Lakeflow Job, with the ingestion pipeline as a task", "Through a cron entry on the driver", "Through a file arrival trigger only", "It can't be scheduled"],
 "When you add a schedule, Lakeflow Connect creates a Lakeflow Job with the ingestion pipeline as a task. You can add more tasks to that job, and you can attach several schedules.\n`A cron entry on the driver`: scheduling is handled by Jobs, not OS-level cron.\n`A file arrival trigger only`: file arrival is a job trigger for storage locations; connectors use schedules.\n`It can't be scheduled`: it can, with one job per schedule.", "lfc"],
["A161", 2, "A managed connector pipeline hits a temporary source error. What does it do?",
 ["Retries automatically with exponential backoff, keeping its cursor position", "Fails and restarts from a full load", "Skips the batch permanently", "Waits for a manual restart"],
 "Managed connectors retry failures automatically with exponential backoff. The connector also stores its cursor position, so after a failure, even one that needs manual action like expired credentials, it resumes without missing or reloading data.\n`Restarts from a full load`: the stored cursor exists to avoid that.\n`Skips the batch permanently`: that would lose data; the connector resumes from its cursor.\n`Waits for a manual restart`: only errors that need a fix, like credentials, need you; transient errors are retried for you.", "lfc"],
["A162", 2, "In Lakeflow Connect, what kind of object is a connection?",
 ["A Unity Catalog securable", "A workspace secret scope", "A cluster policy", "A bundle variable"],
 "A connection is a Unity Catalog securable that holds the source's authentication details, so pipelines don't carry credentials. Because it's a securable, access to it is granted like any other UC object.\n`A workspace secret scope`: secret scopes store secrets for code; Lakeflow Connect uses a UC connection object.\n`A cluster policy`: controls compute settings, not source credentials.\n`A bundle variable`: a deployment-time value in a bundle, not a governed object.", "lfc"],
["A163", 2, "No managed connector exists for a source. Which Lakeflow Connect alternatives does Databricks describe?",
 ["SQL/Spark APIs, community connectors, or custom connectors", "Only Auto Loader", "Only COPY INTO", "Nothing; the source can't be ingested"],
 "When there's no managed connector, Databricks points to SQL and Spark APIs (less automation, broader source support), community connectors (open source) or custom connectors you build and run yourself.\n`Only Auto Loader`: Auto Loader is for files in cloud storage; it can't read an app or database API.\n`Only COPY INTO`: same limitation; it loads files.\n`Nothing; can't be ingested`: the API and custom-connector routes cover such sources.", "lfc"],
["A164", 2, "An Auto Loader stream with a provided schema (mode `none`) should still capture unexpected data. What must be set?",
 ["rescuedDataColumn", "cloudFiles.inferColumnTypes", "cloudFiles.schemaHints", "mergeSchema"],
 "In `none` mode the schema doesn't evolve, new columns are ignored, and data is not rescued unless you set `rescuedDataColumn`. Setting it captures unexpected or unparsable data in that column.\n`cloudFiles.inferColumnTypes`: turns on type inference for JSON, CSV and XML; it doesn't capture extra data.\n`cloudFiles.schemaHints`: fixes types for chosen columns in an inferred schema; it's for inference, not for a provided schema.\n`mergeSchema`: controls schema evolution on write to Delta, not rescue on read.", "als"],
["A165", 2, "`cloudFiles.maxBytesPerTrigger` is set to 1 GB, but one micro-batch read 1.3 GB. Why?",
 ["The byte limit is a soft limit", "The setting was ignored because a file limit wasn't set", "Auto Loader doubled the limit for a backlog", "It is a bug"],
 "`cloudFiles.maxBytesPerTrigger` is a soft limit: Auto Loader can process more bytes than the setting in a micro-batch. `maxFilesPerTrigger` is the hard limit and is never exceeded.\n`Ignored because no file limit was set`: the byte limit works alone; it's just not exact.\n`Doubled the limit for a backlog`: there's no such backlog rule.\n`It is a bug`: overshoot is documented behavior.", "alp"],
["A166", 2, "Auto Loader infers a schema from Parquet files where `amount` is INT in some files and BIGINT in others. What type is inferred?",
 ["BIGINT, the widest type", "INT, the first type seen", "STRING", "It fails"],
 "For Parquet, when a column's type differs between files, Auto Loader chooses the widest type, so INT and BIGINT become BIGINT. You can override this with schema hints.\n`INT, the first type seen`: inference merges the sampled files' schemas rather than taking the first.\n`STRING`: the default for JSON, CSV and XML, which carry no types; Parquet carries types, so they're used.\n`It fails`: compatible numeric types are widened, not rejected.", "als"],
["A167", 2, "Why does Databricks recommend running an Auto Loader stream that uses `addNewColumns` in a Lakeflow Job with retries?",
 ["The stream stops when a new column appears, and a retry restarts it with the updated schema", "Retries make inference faster", "Jobs are required for Auto Loader", "Retries stop the schema from changing"],
 "With `addNewColumns`, the stream fails with `UnknownFieldException` on purpose after adding the new columns to the schema. Running it in a Lakeflow Job that restarts it automatically lets it carry on with the updated schema, with no manual action.\n`Retries make inference faster`: retries don't affect inference speed.\n`Jobs are required for Auto Loader`: Auto Loader runs anywhere; the job is just for automatic restarts.\n`Retries stop the schema from changing`: the opposite; the restart is how the change takes effect.", "als"],
["A168", 2, "`COPY INTO ... VALIDATE` is run with no row count. How many rows does it check?",
 ["All rows", "The first 1,000 rows", "The first 50 rows", "None; it only checks the schema"],
 "With `VALIDATE` and no row count, `COPY INTO` validates all of the data that would be loaded, checking parsing, schema and constraints, without writing anything. `VALIDATE n ROWS` limits it.\n`First 1,000 rows`: there's no implicit sample size.\n`First 50 rows`: same; you only get a limit if you give one.\n`Only the schema`: it also checks that data parses and meets nullability and check constraints.", "copy"],
["A169", 2, "`COPY INTO` reads from a path that no Unity Catalog external location covers. How can it authenticate?",
 ["With an inline CREDENTIAL clause", "With READ FILES on the table", "It can't read the path", "With a bundle variable"],
 "If no Unity Catalog external location covers the path, `COPY INTO` can authenticate with an inline `CREDENTIAL` clause. When an external location does cover it, `READ FILES` on that location is used instead and no credential is needed.\n`READ FILES on the table`: `READ FILES` is granted on external locations or volumes, not tables, and there's no location here.\n`It can't read the path`: the inline credential is exactly for this case.\n`A bundle variable`: a deployment value, not a storage credential.", "copy"],
["A170", 2, "A CSV is uploaded through the Create or modify table file upload UI with default settings. How is it parsed?",
 ["First row as header, comma as delimiter", "No header, tab as delimiter", "No header, comma as delimiter", "First row as header, pipe as delimiter"],
 "The file upload UI treats the first row of a CSV as the header and uses a comma as the delimiter by default. You can change both before creating the table.\n`No header, tab as delimiter`: neither is the default; tab is used for TSV-style files only if you set it.\n`No header, comma`: comma is right, but the header option is on by default.\n`Header, pipe`: header is right, but the delimiter defaults to comma.", "upload"],

// ---------- S3 Transform & pipelines ----------
["A171", 3, "A new Lakeflow pipeline is created without changing the pipeline mode. Which mode does it use?",
 ["Triggered", "Continuous", "Streaming", "Development only"],
 "Triggered is the default pipeline mode. An update processes the data available when it starts, refreshes the tables, then stops, so compute isn't left running.\n`Continuous`: must be chosen; it keeps compute running to process data as it arrives.\n`Streaming`: not a pipeline mode; streaming tables run in either mode.\n`Development only`: development vs production is a separate setting about cluster reuse and retries, not the pipeline mode.", "pmode"],
["A172", 3, "Which statement about triggered vs continuous pipeline mode is correct?",
 ["Continuous keeps compute running for lower latency at higher cost; triggered stops after each update", "Triggered mode needs an always-running cluster", "Only continuous mode supports materialized views", "Continuous mode refreshes once a day"],
 "Continuous mode keeps compute running and processes data as it arrives, giving lower latency at higher cost. Triggered mode runs an update and then stops, using less compute.\n`Triggered needs an always-running cluster`: that describes continuous.\n`Only continuous supports materialized views`: both modes update materialized views and streaming tables.\n`Continuous refreshes once a day`: continuous runs constantly; a daily refresh is a scheduled triggered pipeline.", "pmode"],
["A173", 3, "`pipelines.trigger.interval` is set on a triggered pipeline. What effect does it have?",
 ["None; it only applies to continuous pipelines", "It sets the schedule", "It limits rows per update", "It sets the retry interval"],
 "`pipelines.trigger.interval` sets how often a continuous pipeline starts an update for each flow. It is used only with continuous pipelines, so on a triggered pipeline it does nothing.\n`It sets the schedule`: a triggered pipeline is scheduled by a job or a pipeline schedule.\n`It limits rows per update`: rate limits are source options such as `maxFilesPerTrigger`.\n`It sets the retry interval`: retries are handled by the update's run behavior, not this setting.", "pmode"],
["A174", 4, "A pipeline runs from a continuous Lakeflow Job, but its pipeline mode is set to triggered. Which wins?",
 ["The job: it sets the execution mode and takes precedence", "The pipeline mode setting", "Whichever was changed most recently", "The run fails with a conflict"],
 "When a pipeline runs from a continuous Lakeflow Job, the job determines the execution mode and takes precedence over the pipeline's mode setting. Databricks recommends this way of running continuous pipelines.\n`The pipeline mode setting`: it's overridden by the continuous job.\n`Whichever was changed most recently`: precedence is fixed, not time-based.\n`The run fails with a conflict`: there's no conflict; the job wins.", "pmode"],
["A175", 3, "What does a full refresh of a streaming table do?",
 ["Clears its data and checkpoints, then reprocesses all records from the source", "Processes only new records", "Recomputes only the last day", "Deletes the table definition"],
 "A full refresh of a streaming table clears its data and its flows' checkpoints, then reprocesses every record the source still holds. A normal refresh only processes records that arrived since the last update.\n`Processes only new records`: that's a normal refresh.\n`Recomputes only the last day`: there's no time-windowed refresh.\n`Deletes the table definition`: the definition stays; only data and state are reset.", "upd"],
["A176", 3, "A streaming table reads from Kafka with 7-day retention. Why is a full refresh risky?",
 ["Records no longer in the source are dropped from the target", "It duplicates every record", "It converts the table to a view", "It turns off expectations"],
 "A full refresh clears the target and rebuilds it from what the source currently holds. With 7-day Kafka retention, anything older than that is gone from Kafka and therefore disappears from the table.\n`It duplicates every record`: the table is cleared first, so records aren't doubled.\n`It converts the table to a view`: the object type doesn't change.\n`It turns off expectations`: expectations still apply during reprocessing.", "upd"],
["A177", 3, "How do you stop anyone from running a full refresh on a particular streaming table?",
 ["Set the table property pipelines.reset.allowed = false", "Set the pipeline to continuous mode", "Revoke SELECT on the table", "Set spark.sql.shuffle.partitions = 0"],
 "Setting the table property `pipelines.reset.allowed = false` blocks full refreshes for that table. Normal incremental updates continue.\n`Continuous mode`: changes how updates run, not whether a full refresh is allowed.\n`Revoke SELECT`: controls who can read the table, not pipeline operations.\n`spark.sql.shuffle.partitions = 0`: a shuffle tuning setting unrelated to refresh.", "upd"],
["A178", 3, "An engineer wants to catch wrong table or column names in a pipeline without writing any data. What should they run?",
 ["A validate update (dry run)", "A full refresh", "Refresh failed tables", "OPTIMIZE"],
 "A validate update (dry run, Public Preview) resolves the pipeline's datasets and flows and reports errors such as wrong table or column names, without materializing or publishing any data.\n`A full refresh`: processes and rewrites all data, the opposite of what's wanted.\n`Refresh failed tables`: reruns tables that failed; it writes data.\n`OPTIMIZE`: compacts files of an existing table; it doesn't check pipeline code.", "upd"],
["A179", 3, "A pipeline update is started with Run now in the UI (non-continuous). Which behavior applies?",
 ["The cluster is reused and pipeline retries are disabled, for fast debugging", "The cluster shuts down immediately and errors are retried", "A full refresh runs", "The pipeline switches to continuous mode"],
 "A non-continuous update started from the pipeline UI reuses the cluster to avoid restart overhead and disables pipeline retries, so errors show up straight away for debugging.\n`Cluster shuts down and errors are retried`: that's how job or API triggered updates behave.\n`A full refresh runs`: Start runs a normal update; full refresh is a separate choice.\n`Switches to continuous mode`: the mode is a pipeline setting and doesn't change because you clicked Start.", "upd"],
["A180", 4, "A pipeline update is triggered by a Lakeflow Job. Which behavior applies?",
 ["Recoverable errors are retried and the cluster shuts down right after the run", "The cluster stays up for two hours", "Retries are disabled", "Only failed tables are refreshed"],
 "Updates triggered by a job or the API restart the cluster for recoverable errors (such as memory leaks or stale credentials), retry on errors like cluster start failure, and shut the cluster down right after the run.\n`Cluster stays up for two hours`: keeping the cluster up applies to manual UI runs.\n`Retries are disabled`: also UI-run behavior, for fast debugging.\n`Only failed tables are refreshed`: that's the separate Refresh failed tables action.", "upd"],
["A181", 3, "'Refresh failed tables' is used after a partial failure. What gets refreshed?",
 ["The failed tables plus their downstream dependencies", "Every table in the pipeline", "Only tables that succeeded", "Only materialized views"],
 "Refresh failed tables reruns the failed materialized views and streaming tables and their downstream dependencies, so dependent tables catch up. Tables you exclude aren't refreshed, even if they depend on a failed one.\n`Every table in the pipeline`: that's a normal full update.\n`Only tables that succeeded`: the reverse of the purpose.\n`Only materialized views`: failed streaming tables are included too.", "upd"],
["A182", 3, "A standalone materialized view (created in Databricks SQL) is refreshed. Which mode does the refresh use?",
 ["Always triggered", "Always continuous", "Whatever the warehouse is set to", "Development mode"],
 "Refreshes of standalone materialized views and streaming tables created in Databricks SQL always use triggered mode. To keep them fresh you schedule refreshes rather than run them continuously.\n`Always continuous`: standalone refreshes never run continuously.\n`Whatever the warehouse is set to`: a SQL warehouse has no pipeline mode setting.\n`Development mode`: a pipeline setting about cluster reuse and retries, not the refresh mode.", "pmode"],
["A183", 3, "Which pipeline object does a normal (not full) refresh update incrementally when that's more efficient, and otherwise recompute?",
 ["Materialized view", "View", "Temporary view", "External table"],
 "A normal refresh of a materialized view checks the cost and refreshes incrementally when that's cheaper, otherwise it recomputes; either way results match a full recompute. Incremental refresh needs serverless compute; on classic compute the MV is fully recomputed.\n`View`: stores no data, so there's nothing to refresh; it's computed at query time.\n`Temporary view`: same, and it only exists within the pipeline update.\n`External table`: a table whose files live in your storage; a pipeline refresh doesn't maintain it.", "upd"],
["A184", 3, "A 50 GB fact table joins a 5 MB lookup table. AQE is off. Which side should get a broadcast hint?",
 ["The 5 MB lookup table", "The 50 GB fact table", "Both", "Neither; hints are ignored"],
 "A broadcast join copies the small side to every executor, so the large side is joined in place without a shuffle. With AQE off, Spark won't switch strategies for you, so hint the 5 MB lookup table.\n`The 50 GB fact table`: broadcasting it would copy 50 GB to every executor and likely run out of memory.\n`Both`: only one side is broadcast, and the 50 GB table should never be it.\n`Neither; hints are ignored`: join hints are honored when possible.", "hints"],
["A185", 3, "A job on a small dataset produces 200 tiny shuffle tasks per stage. Which change helps directly?",
 ["Set spark.sql.shuffle.partitions to auto, or let AQE coalesce partitions", "Raise spark.sql.shuffle.partitions to 2,000", "Turn off AQE", "Add more drivers"],
 "`spark.sql.shuffle.partitions` defaults to 200, which is too many for small data. Setting it to `auto` lets Databricks size partitions, and AQE coalesces small post-shuffle partitions into fewer tasks.\n`Raise to 2,000`: creates ten times more tiny tasks, making it worse.\n`Turn off AQE`: removes the partition coalescing that fixes this.\n`Add more drivers`: a cluster has one driver, and task count isn't a driver problem.", "aqe"],
["A186", 3, "Find customers who have never placed an order. Which join type returns only the customers with no match?",
 ["left_anti", "left_semi", "inner", "cross"],
 "A left anti join returns left rows that have no match on the right, so it gives customers with no orders.\n`left_semi`: the opposite: left rows that do have a match.\n`inner`: returns only matched pairs, so customers with no orders are excluded, and order columns are added.\n`cross`: pairs every customer with every order, with no matching at all.", "join"],
["A187", 3, "Find customers who have at least one order, without adding any order columns. Which join type?",
 ["left_semi", "left_anti", "full_outer", "cross"],
 "A left semi join returns left rows that have at least one match, with only the left columns, and each customer once even if they have many orders.\n`left_anti`: returns customers with no match, the opposite.\n`full_outer`: keeps unmatched rows from both sides and adds order columns.\n`cross`: pairs every customer with every order and adds order columns.", "join"],
["A188", 3, "Average order amount per region in PySpark?",
 ["df.groupBy('region').agg(avg('amount'))", "df.select(avg('amount')).groupBy('region')", "df.agg('region', avg('amount'))", "df.groupBy(avg('amount'))"],
 "`groupBy('region')` groups rows by region, then `agg(avg('amount'))` computes the average within each group, giving one row per region.\n`select(avg('amount')).groupBy('region')`: the select already collapses everything to one average and drops `region`, so the groupBy fails.\n`df.agg('region', avg('amount'))`: `agg` with no `groupBy` aggregates the whole DataFrame, and a bare column name isn't an aggregate.\n`groupBy(avg('amount'))`: you group by columns, not by an aggregate.", "pyfn"],
["A189", 3, "Rows where `email` is null must be removed. Which code?",
 ["df.filter(col('email').isNotNull())", "df.filter(col('email') != None)", "df.fillna({'email': ''})", "df.drop('email')"],
 "`isNotNull()` tests for null directly, so the filter keeps only rows with an email. `dropna(subset=['email'])` also works.\n`col('email') != None`: comparing with null returns null, not true, so this doesn't act as a null check; null-safe checks use `isNull`/`isNotNull`.\n`fillna({'email': ''})`: replaces nulls with an empty string, so the rows stay.\n`df.drop('email')`: removes the column, not the rows.", "pyspark"],
["A190", 3, "Keep only the latest record per `customer_id` by `updated_at`. Which approach?",
 ["row_number() over a window partitioned by customer_id ordered by updated_at desc, then keep row 1", "dropDuplicates(['updated_at'])", "distinct()", "groupBy('customer_id').count()"],
 "`row_number()` over a window partitioned by `customer_id` and ordered by `updated_at desc` numbers each customer's rows newest first. Keeping `row_number = 1` gives exactly one, latest, row per customer.\n`dropDuplicates(['updated_at'])`: dedupes on the timestamp, not the customer, and keeps an arbitrary row.\n`distinct()`: only removes fully identical rows; different versions of a customer differ.\n`groupBy('customer_id').count()`: returns counts per customer, not the records.", "pyfn"],
["A191", 3, "Which SQL operator combines two result sets and removes duplicate rows?",
 ["UNION", "UNION ALL", "JOIN", "CROSS JOIN"],
 "`UNION` combines two result sets with matching columns and removes duplicate rows. `UNION ALL` does the same but keeps duplicates.\n`UNION ALL`: keeps duplicates, so it's the near miss.\n`JOIN`: combines columns side by side by a condition, not rows from two result sets.\n`CROSS JOIN`: pairs every row of one set with every row of the other.", "join"],
["A192", 3, "Which Python decorator defines a materialized view in a Lakeflow pipeline?",
 ["@dp.materialized_view", "@dp.view_table", "@dlt.stream", "@dp.expect"],
 "In the Lakeflow pipelines Python API (`from pyspark import pipelines as dp`), `@dp.materialized_view` defines a materialized view. `@dp.table` defines a table such as a streaming table.\n`@dp.view_table`: not a decorator in the API.\n`@dlt.stream`: not a decorator; the legacy `dlt` module used `@dlt.table`.\n`@dp.expect`: adds a data quality expectation to a dataset; it doesn't define one.", "dlt"],
["A193", 3, "A gold aggregate is shown on a BI dashboard and must reflect late corrections to silver. Which object fits better than a streaming table?",
 ["Materialized view", "Streaming table with append flow", "Temporary view", "Volume"],
 "Streaming tables process each source row once and assume append-only input, so later updates to silver rows aren't reflected. A materialized view's result always matches its query, so corrections flow through on refresh.\n`Streaming table with append flow`: still append-only; updated source rows are missed.\n`Temporary view`: exists only inside the pipeline and isn't published for a dashboard to query.\n`Volume`: stores files, not query results.", "ldp"],

// ---------- S4 Lakeflow Jobs ----------
["A194", 4, "A notebook task must pass a computed row count to a downstream task. Which call sets it?",
 ["dbutils.jobs.taskValues.set(key='row_count', value=n)", "dbutils.widgets.set('row_count', n)", "spark.conf.set('row_count', n)", "dbutils.notebook.exit(n) only"],
 "`dbutils.jobs.taskValues.set(key=..., value=...)` publishes a small value that downstream tasks in the same job run can read. Keys are strings and values must be JSON-serializable.\n`dbutils.widgets.set`: widgets hold input parameters for a notebook, not values passed to downstream tasks.\n`spark.conf.set`: sets a config in this task's Spark session; other tasks run separately and can't see it.\n`dbutils.notebook.exit(n) only`: returns a value to a caller of `dbutils.notebook.run`, not a named value for downstream tasks.", "tv"],
["A195", 4, "What is the recommended way for a downstream task to read the task value `row_count` set by task `ingest`?",
 ["A task parameter with the dynamic reference {{tasks.ingest.values.row_count}}", "dbutils.jobs.taskValues.get with a default value", "Reading a temp file on the driver", "A global Spark config"],
 "Databricks recommends reading task values through task parameters with dynamic value references like `{{tasks.ingest.values.row_count}}`. This works in any task type that accepts parameters, not just Python notebooks.\n`taskValues.get with a default`: `get` works in Python notebooks, but defaults are discouraged because they hide missing keys.\n`A temp file on the driver`: tasks may run on different compute, so local files aren't shared.\n`A global Spark config`: Spark configs aren't shared between tasks.", "tv"],
["A196", 4, "What is the size limit for one task value?",
 ["48 KiB as JSON", "1 MB", "16 KB", "No limit"],
 "A single task value's JSON representation can't exceed 48 KiB. Task values are for small results like counts or flags, not datasets.\n`1 MB`: well over the limit.\n`16 KB`: below the actual limit.\n`No limit`: there is a 48 KiB cap; write larger results to a table or volume and pass a reference.", "tv"],
["A197", 4, "Which task types can call `dbutils.jobs.taskValues.set`?",
 ["Python notebooks", "SQL queries", "Dashboards", "Any task type"],
 "`taskValues.set` and `get` are Python functions, usable only in notebooks with Python selected as the language. Other task types read task values through dynamic value references in their parameters.\n`SQL queries`: can read values via `{{tasks...}}` references, but can't call `set`.\n`Dashboards`: can't set task values.\n`Any task type`: confuses reading via dynamic references, which any parameterized task can do, with setting, which is Python notebooks only.", "tv"],
["A198", 4, "Why does Databricks not recommend default values in `taskValues.get`?",
 ["They can hide a missing key or a wrong task name", "They slow the job down", "They aren't supported", "They overwrite the upstream value"],
 "If `get` has a default, a missing key or misspelled task name doesn't fail: the task quietly carries on with the default. Without one, the error is visible and you fix it.\n`Slow the job down`: performance isn't the concern.\n`Aren't supported`: they are supported (the `default` argument), just not recommended.\n`Overwrite the upstream value`: `get` only reads; it never changes the stored value.", "tv"],
["A199", 4, "A user wants to run a job once with a different `start_date` without editing the job. How?",
 ["Run now with different parameters", "Edit the task's static parameter", "Create a new job", "Change the notebook's widget default"],
 "Run now with different parameters overrides the job parameter values for that one run only; the job's defaults stay as they were. The REST API can do the same.\n`Edit the task's static parameter`: changes the job for every future run, not just once.\n`Create a new job`: unnecessary duplication.\n`Change the notebook's widget default`: also a permanent change, and job parameters override widget defaults anyway.", "params"],
["A200", 4, "How does a notebook task read a job parameter?",
 ["Through dbutils.widgets", "Through sys.argv", "Through spark.conf", "Through taskValues.get"],
 "Notebook tasks receive parameters as widgets and read them with `dbutils.widgets` (for example `dbutils.widgets.get('start_date')`).\n`sys.argv`: that's how Python script tasks get parameters, as command-line arguments.\n`spark.conf`: Spark settings, not job parameters.\n`taskValues.get`: reads values set by an upstream task, not parameters passed to the job.", "params"],
["A201", 4, "How can a task parameter set with a static value be changed?",
 ["Only by updating the task definition", "With Run now with different parameters", "With a task value", "It changes on every run"],
 "A task parameter given a static value is fixed in the task definition. The only way to change it is to update the task. Job parameters, by contrast, can be overridden per run.\n`Run now with different parameters`: overrides job parameters, not static task parameters.\n`A task value`: a value passed between tasks at runtime; it doesn't change a static parameter.\n`Changes on every run`: static means fixed; changing per run needs a dynamic reference or a job parameter.", "params"],
["A202", 4, "Which permission lets a user override job parameter values for a manual run?",
 ["CAN MANAGE RUN or higher", "CAN VIEW", "USE CATALOG", "READ FILES"],
 "Anyone with `CAN MANAGE RUN` or higher on the job can override job parameter values for a manual run. So a parameter's default isn't a security control.\n`CAN VIEW`: lets you see the job and its runs, but not trigger runs.\n`USE CATALOG`: a Unity Catalog data privilege, not a job permission.\n`READ FILES`: a Unity Catalog privilege on external locations or volumes, unrelated to jobs.", "params"],
["A203", 4, "Job parameters are defined at the job level. How do they reach notebook tasks that use key-value parameters?",
 ["They are pushed down to those tasks automatically", "Each task must copy them by hand", "Only through task values", "They don't; job parameters only apply to SQL tasks"],
 "Job parameters are pushed down automatically to tasks that use key-value parameters, such as notebook tasks, which then read them as widgets.\n`Each task must copy them by hand`: push-down avoids that.\n`Only through task values`: task values carry outputs between tasks at runtime, not job parameters.\n`Only SQL tasks`: any key-value parameter task gets them, notebooks included.", "params"],
["A204", 4, "A table update trigger watches `orders` and `customers`. The job must run only after both have new data. Which option?",
 ["All tables are updated", "Any table is updated", "Wait after last change", "Minimum time between triggers"],
 "A table update trigger can fire when any watched table updates or only when all of them have. `All tables are updated` waits until both `orders` and `customers` have new data.\n`Any table is updated`: fires on an update to either one alone, too early here.\n`Wait after last change`: an advanced timing setting that delays the run until updates go quiet; it doesn't require both tables.\n`Minimum time between triggers`: limits how often the job can fire, not which tables must update.", "tut"],
["A205", 4, "Which trigger fits best when a job should run whenever new files land in a Unity Catalog volume?",
 ["File arrival", "Table update", "Scheduled every minute", "Continuous"],
 "A file arrival trigger watches a Unity Catalog volume or external location and starts a run when new files land, checking about every minute (best effort).\n`Table update`: watches Unity Catalog tables, not files in a volume.\n`Scheduled every minute`: runs whether or not files arrived, wasting runs.\n`Continuous`: keeps a run going all the time instead of reacting to new files.", "fat"],
["A206", 4, "File events are enabled on the external location a file arrival trigger watches. What changes?",
 ["The 10,000-file limit no longer applies", "The trigger checks every second", "Retries are turned off", "The trigger becomes a table update trigger"],
 "Without file events, a file arrival trigger's location can hold at most 10,000 files (and a workspace has at most 50 such triggers). With file events on the external location, the file limit no longer applies.\n`Checks every second`: checking stays best effort around once a minute.\n`Retries are turned off`: file events don't affect job retries.\n`Becomes a table update trigger`: it stays a file arrival trigger.", "fat"],
["A207", 4, "A Lakeflow Job has a Pipeline task for a Lakeflow pipeline. What runs when the task runs?",
 ["An update of that pipeline", "A full refresh every time", "Only the pipeline's validation", "A new pipeline is created"],
 "A Pipeline task starts an update of that pipeline, as if you clicked Start, and gets job-run behavior: retries on recoverable errors and the cluster shuts down after the run.\n`A full refresh every time`: only if the task's full refresh option is turned on; by default it's a normal update.\n`Only the pipeline's validation`: a dry run is a separate action.\n`A new pipeline is created`: the task runs an existing pipeline.", "upd"],
["A208", 4, "A For each task's nested task sets a task value in every iteration. What does a downstream reference to the nested task's key return?",
 ["A list of every iteration's value, ordered by iteration", "Only the last iteration's value", "Only the first iteration's value", "An error"],
 "When a For each nested task sets a value in every iteration, a downstream reference to that key returns a list with every iteration's value, ordered by iteration index. An iteration that didn't set it shows as null (Beta).\n`Only the last iteration's value`: values aren't overwritten; they're collected.\n`Only the first iteration's value`: same, all iterations are kept.\n`An error`: the aggregated list is supported.", "tv"],

// ---------- S5 CI/CD ----------
["A209", 5, "In a Databricks Git folder, a developer switches branches with uncommitted changes that don't conflict. What happens to the changes?",
 ["They carry over to the new branch", "They are discarded", "They are committed automatically", "The switch is blocked"],
 "When you switch branches in a Git folder, uncommitted changes carry over and show as uncommitted on the new branch, as long as they don't conflict with its code. Discard them first if you don't want that.\n`They are discarded`: only if you discard them yourself.\n`Committed automatically`: Git folders never commit without you.\n`The switch is blocked`: not when the changes don't conflict.", "gitops"],
["A210", 5, "A developer switches to a branch that doesn't contain a notebook from the current branch. What happens to that notebook in the workspace?",
 ["It may be deleted; switching back recreates it with a new ID and URL", "It stays unchanged", "It is moved to the trash for 30 days", "It is committed to the new branch"],
 "Switching to a branch that lacks a workspace asset, such as a notebook, can delete it. Switching back recreates it with a new ID and URL, which can't be reversed, so links and bookmarks to the old one break.\n`It stays unchanged`: the workspace mirrors the checked-out branch, so it doesn't stay.\n`Moved to the trash for 30 days`: the docs describe deletion and recreation, not a trash.\n`Committed to the new branch`: switching never commits anything.", "gitops"],
["A211", 5, "What is required to commit from a Databricks Git folder?",
 ["A commit message, then Commit & Push", "A pull request", "A bundle deploy", "Admin approval"],
 "A Git folder commits directly to the working branch: the Git dialog lists changed files, a commit message is required, and Commit & Push sends the commit to the remote.\n`A pull request`: PRs are how you merge a branch into another, and they're opened in the Git provider. You don't need one to commit.\n`A bundle deploy`: deploys resources to a workspace. It has nothing to do with committing code.\n`Admin approval`: there's no approval step. Anyone with access to the folder and the repo can commit.", "gitops"],
["A212", 5, "A developer can't push to the default branch. Where does the Databricks doc say to open the pull request?",
 ["In the Git provider's interface, from a new branch", "In the Databricks Git dialog only", "With databricks bundle deploy", "In Catalog Explorer"],
 "Databricks Git folders don't create pull requests. If you can't push to the default branch, create a new branch, commit and push to it, then open the PR in your Git provider's interface (GitHub, GitLab, Azure DevOps and so on).\n`In the Databricks Git dialog only`: the dialog commits, pushes, pulls and merges, but the doc sends you to the Git provider for the PR.\n`databricks bundle deploy`: deploys bundle resources to a workspace, not a Git operation.\n`Catalog Explorer`: browses data objects and permissions, not Git.", "gitops"],
["A213", 5, "What does Pull do to an open notebook in a Git folder when upstream changes arrive?",
 ["Updates the files and clears the notebook state", "Nothing until the notebook is reopened", "Commits local changes first", "Creates a new branch"],
 "Pull brings the files up to the latest remote version, and any Git operation that pulls upstream changes clears the notebook state, so variables and outputs from the current session are gone.\n`Nothing until the notebook is reopened`: the files update straight away; you don't need to reopen anything.\n`Commits local changes first`: Pull never commits for you. Committing is a separate Commit & Push step.\n`Creates a new branch`: branches are only created when you ask for one.", "gitops"],
["A214", 5, "Why does Databricks recommend merge over rebase in Git folders for most users?",
 ["Merge doesn't force push or rewrite commit history", "Merge is faster", "Rebase isn't supported", "Merge deletes stale branches"],
 "Rebase rewrites commit history, so in a Git folder it ends with a force push, which can cause versioning trouble for collaborators on the same branch. Merge adds a merge commit and pushes normally, so nothing is rewritten.\n`Merge is faster`: speed isn't the reason given. The concern is force pushing and rewritten history.\n`Rebase isn't supported`: it is supported in Git folders. It's just riskier for most users.\n`Merge deletes stale branches`: merging never deletes branches.", "gitops"],
["A215", 5, "What does Reset do on a Git folder branch?",
 ["The same as git reset --hard plus git push --force: local and remote changes are lost", "Discards only uncommitted local changes", "Reverts the last commit only", "Deletes the Git folder"],
 "Reset in a Git folder behaves like `git reset --hard` followed by `git push --force`: you lose all uncommitted and committed changes in both the local and the remote version of the branch.\n`Discards only uncommitted local changes`: that's what discarding changes does. Reset also throws away committed work and rewrites the remote.\n`Reverts the last commit only`: that's closer to `git revert`. Reset resets the whole branch.\n`Deletes the Git folder`: the folder stays; only the branch's changes are lost.", "gitops"],
["A216", 5, "A notebook saved in source format (.py) is committed from a Git folder. Are its outputs committed?",
 ["No, not by default", "Yes, always", "Only images", "Only if under 10 MB"],
 "When notebooks are saved in a source file format such as `.py`, outputs are not included in commits by default. Only the `.ipynb` format has a setting to commit outputs.\n`Yes, always`: the default is the opposite for source formats.\n`Only images`: there is no images-only rule; no outputs are committed by default.\n`Only if under 10 MB`: size isn't what decides it. The file format and its output setting decide it.", "gitops"],
["A217", 5, "A merge in a Git folder hits a conflict. How is it resolved?",
 ["In the Git folders UI: edit or choose a side, mark resolved, then Continue Merge", "Only from a local terminal", "Databricks always takes the incoming changes", "The merge is cancelled automatically"],
 "Conflicts are resolved in the Git folders UI: edit the file by hand and select Mark As Resolved, or use Keep all current changes or Take all incoming changes, then click Continue Merge. Abort undoes the whole operation.\n`Only from a local terminal`: you can use a terminal, but it isn't required. The UI has full conflict resolution.\n`Databricks always takes the incoming changes`: taking incoming changes is one choice, not an automatic rule.\n`The merge is cancelled automatically`: the merge pauses for you to resolve it. Cancelling only happens if you click Abort.", "gitops"],
["A218", 5, "A bundle variable has a value in `variable-overrides.json` and a different value in the target's `variables`. No --var or env var is set. Which wins?",
 ["variable-overrides.json", "The target variables", "The default", "The deploy fails"],
 "Bundle variable precedence, highest first: `--var` on the command line, `BUNDLE_VAR_` environment variables, `variable-overrides.json`, the target's `variables`, then the variable's `default`. With no `--var` or env var, `variable-overrides.json` is the highest one set.\n`The target variables`: they rank below `variable-overrides.json`, so they're overridden here.\n`The default`: the default is the lowest level, only used when nothing else sets a value.\n`The deploy fails`: two values at different levels isn't an error. Precedence picks one.", "bvar"],
["A219", 5, "Which is a behavior of `mode: development` in a bundle target?",
 ["Concurrent runs are allowed and the deployment lock is off", "The Git branch is checked", "Pipelines are set to development: false", "A service principal is required"],
 "`mode: development` relaxes controls for fast iteration: it enables concurrent runs on deployed jobs, disables the deployment lock, pauses schedules and triggers, prefixes resource names with `[dev user]` and sets pipelines to `development: true`.\n`The Git branch is checked`: that's production mode, which checks the current branch matches the target's branch.\n`Pipelines are set to development: false`: production mode validates that. Development mode sets `development: true`.\n`A service principal is required`: service principals are recommended for production deployments, not required in development.", "bdm"],
["A220", 5, "A team promotes one codebase to dev, test and prod workspaces. What keeps environment-specific values out of the code?",
 ["Bundle variables with per-target values and overrides", "A separate Git repo per environment", "Hard-coded values in each notebook", "Cluster policies"],
 "Bundle variables let one codebase be deployed to every environment: each target (dev, test, prod) sets its own values, and overrides like `--var` can change them at deploy time, so nothing environment-specific is in the code.\n`A separate Git repo per environment`: this duplicates the code, which is the opposite of one codebase promoted through environments.\n`Hard-coded values in each notebook`: this puts the environment values in the code, the thing to avoid.\n`Cluster policies`: they limit how compute can be configured. They don't parameterize code per environment.", "bvar"],

// ---------- S6 Optimize & troubleshoot ----------
["A221", 6, "A liquid-clustered table gets new data every hour. What does a plain `OPTIMIZE` (no FULL) do?",
 ["Clusters data incrementally, mainly new data", "Rewrites every file", "Removes old versions", "Changes the clustering keys"],
 "On a liquid-clustered table, plain `OPTIMIZE` is incremental: it mainly clusters data that isn't clustered yet, such as newly written files. `OPTIMIZE FULL` is the one that reclusters every record, for example after the clustering keys change.\n`Rewrites every file`: that's `OPTIMIZE FULL`, not plain `OPTIMIZE`.\n`Removes old versions`: that's `VACUUM`, which deletes unreferenced data files. `OPTIMIZE` doesn't.\n`Changes the clustering keys`: keys are changed with `ALTER TABLE ... CLUSTER BY`, not by `OPTIMIZE`.", "lc"],
["A222", 6, "Which statement removes liquid clustering from a table?",
 ["ALTER TABLE t CLUSTER BY NONE", "ALTER TABLE t DROP CLUSTERING", "OPTIMIZE t FULL", "ALTER TABLE t ZORDER BY NONE"],
 "`ALTER TABLE t CLUSTER BY NONE` removes the clustering keys, so new data is no longer clustered. Existing files aren't rewritten. `CLUSTER BY AUTO` is the variant that lets Databricks choose the keys.\n`DROP CLUSTERING`: not valid syntax. Clustering is changed through `CLUSTER BY`.\n`OPTIMIZE t FULL`: reclusters all data with the current keys. It doesn't remove them.\n`ZORDER BY NONE`: not valid. Z-ordering is an `OPTIMIZE` option, and it isn't used with liquid clustering anyway.", "lc"],
["A223", 6, "Predictive optimization runs OPTIMIZE on a table. Which of these does it NOT do?",
 ["ZORDER", "Compact files", "Run on serverless compute", "Apply to UC managed tables"],
 "When predictive optimization runs `OPTIMIZE`, it doesn't run `ZORDER`, and it ignores files that were Z-ordered. Alongside `OPTIMIZE` it can run `VACUUM` and `ANALYZE`.\n`Compact files`: compaction is what its `OPTIMIZE` does, so it does do this.\n`Run on serverless compute`: it does. Its maintenance runs on serverless jobs compute.\n`Apply to UC managed tables`: that's exactly its scope. External tables are the ones it doesn't cover.", "po"],
["A225", 6, "In the Jobs UI, a task shows Upstream failed. Where is the root cause?",
 ["In an upstream task that failed; find it in the DAG view", "In this task's own code", "In the cluster policy", "In the bundle config"],
 "Upstream failed means this task never ran because a task it depends on failed and the Run if condition (default: all succeeded) wasn't met. The root cause is in the upstream task, which you can find in the run's DAG view.\n`In this task's own code`: the task didn't run, so its code can't have caused the failure.\n`In the cluster policy`: a policy problem would make the failing task fail, not show this status on the downstream one.\n`In the bundle config`: the bundle only defines the job. This status is about a dependency failing at run time.", "runif"],
["A226", 6, "AQE's skew handling splits skewed partitions. In which operations?",
 ["Joins", "Writes to Delta", "Reads from cloud storage", "UDF calls"],
 "AQE's skew handling works on joins: at run time it detects a partition that's much larger than the others in a sort-merge or shuffle-hash join and splits it into smaller pieces so one task doesn't do most of the work.\n`Writes to Delta`: AQE's skew split is a join optimization. Write file sizes are handled by features like optimized writes.\n`Reads from cloud storage`: read partitions are sized by file splitting, not by AQE skew handling.\n`UDF calls`: AQE doesn't split UDF work. It changes shuffle and join plans.", "aqe"],
["A227", 6, "A slow join stage shows a few huge tasks and many small ones. AQE is on. What else can reduce the skew?",
 ["Choose a join key or clustering that spreads data more evenly", "Lower the number of executors", "Turn off Photon", "Increase auto-termination"],
 "Skew happens because a few key values hold most of the rows. AQE can split skewed partitions, but choosing a join key, or clustering the data, so rows spread more evenly fixes the cause.\n`Lower the number of executors`: less parallelism makes the few huge tasks the bottleneck for even longer.\n`Turn off Photon`: Photon speeds up execution. Turning it off doesn't change how data is spread.\n`Increase auto-termination`: it only controls when an idle cluster shuts down.", "aqe"],

// ---------- S7 Governance ----------
["A228", 7, "Which SQL attaches row filter function `region_filter` to `sales`, using the `region` column?",
 ["ALTER TABLE sales SET ROW FILTER region_filter ON (region)", "ALTER TABLE sales ADD FILTER region_filter(region)", "CREATE ROW FILTER region_filter ON sales", "GRANT FILTER region_filter ON sales"],
 "A row filter is a SQL UDF attached with `ALTER TABLE <table> SET ROW FILTER <function> ON (<columns>)`. The listed columns are passed to the function, and rows where it returns FALSE are hidden.\n`ADD FILTER region_filter(region)`: not valid syntax. The keyword is `SET ROW FILTER`.\n`CREATE ROW FILTER`: there's no such statement. You create a normal function, then attach it with `ALTER TABLE`.\n`GRANT FILTER`: `GRANT` gives privileges. It doesn't attach filters.", "fm"],
["A229", 7, "Which SQL attaches mask function `mask_ssn` to the `ssn` column of `people`?",
 ["ALTER TABLE people ALTER COLUMN ssn SET MASK mask_ssn", "ALTER TABLE people SET MASK mask_ssn ON (ssn)", "CREATE MASK mask_ssn ON people.ssn", "GRANT MASK mask_ssn ON COLUMN ssn"],
 "A column mask is a SQL UDF bound to one column with `ALTER TABLE <table> ALTER COLUMN <column> SET MASK <function>`. The function returns what users see in that column in place of the real value.\n`SET MASK mask_ssn ON (ssn)`: this mixes up row filter syntax (`SET ROW FILTER ... ON`). Masks are set on the column with `ALTER COLUMN`.\n`CREATE MASK`: there's no such statement. The mask is an ordinary function attached with `ALTER TABLE`.\n`GRANT MASK`: `GRANT` gives privileges. It doesn't bind masks.", "fm"],
["A230", 7, "What does a row filter function return, and what happens to rows where it returns FALSE?",
 ["BOOLEAN; those rows are excluded from results", "A masked value; the row is shown masked", "An integer row count", "NULL; the query fails"],
 "A row filter function returns a boolean for each row at query time. Rows where it returns TRUE are kept and rows where it returns FALSE are left out, so users simply don't see them.\n`A masked value; the row is shown masked`: that's a column mask, which changes a column's value rather than removing the row.\n`An integer row count`: the function runs per row and returns a boolean, not a count.\n`NULL; the query fails`: a FALSE result excludes the row silently. It doesn't raise an error.", "fm"],
["A231", 7, "How many masks can one column have?",
 ["One", "Two", "Up to four", "Unlimited"],
 "Each column can have only one mask. If you need several rules, put them all in one mask function. It can take other columns as inputs, for example to decide based on another column's value.\n`Two`: wrong. The limit is one mask per column.\n`Up to four`: wrong. The limit is one.\n`Unlimited`: wrong. Combine the logic into one function instead.", "fm"],
["A232", 7, "Can a row filter or column mask be applied directly to a view?",
 ["No; use a dynamic view or apply them to the underlying table", "Yes, the same as a table", "Only to materialized views", "Only with ABAC"],
 "Row filters and column masks can't be applied to views. To secure data shown through a view, apply them to the underlying table or write a dynamic view that filters and masks with functions like `is_account_group_member()`.\n`Yes, the same as a table`: the docs list views as unsupported for row filters and column masks.\n`Only to materialized views`: the question is about views. The supported route there is the underlying table or a dynamic view.\n`Only with ABAC`: ABAC policies apply to tagged tables. They don't make filters attachable to a view.", "fm"],
["A233", 7, "A table has a row filter. A user tries `SELECT * FROM t VERSION AS OF 5`. What happens?",
 ["It isn't supported; time travel doesn't work on tables with row filters or column masks", "It returns filtered historical data", "It returns unfiltered historical data", "It works only for the table owner"],
 "Time travel isn't supported on tables with table-level row filters or column masks, so a `VERSION AS OF` query on that table doesn't run. This stops old versions from being used to get around the current rules.\n`It returns filtered historical data`: it doesn't run at all. The filter isn't applied to old versions.\n`It returns unfiltered historical data`: that would leak data. The query is blocked instead.\n`It works only for the table owner`: the limitation applies to everyone, not depending on who runs the query.", "fm"],
["A234", 7, "Who can set a table-level row filter or column mask?",
 ["The table owner or a user with MANAGE on the table", "Any user with SELECT", "Only the account admin", "Only the schema owner"],
 "Table-level row filters and column masks are set with `ALTER TABLE`, which needs the table owner or a user with `MANAGE` on the table. Those are the principals who manage access to the table.\n`Any user with SELECT`: `SELECT` only reads data. Changing who can see what needs ownership or `MANAGE`.\n`Only the account admin`: not required. The table owner can do it.\n`Only the schema owner`: the rule is about the table's owner or `MANAGE` on the table, not schema ownership.", "fm"],
["A235", 7, "Why does Databricks recommend ABAC policies over table-level filters when the same rule applies to many tables?",
 ["They are centralized, match governed tags, and apply automatically; table owners can't override them", "They run faster", "They support time travel", "They work on DBR 10.4"],
 "ABAC policies are defined once at catalog or schema level, match tables by governed tags, cover new tagged tables automatically and can't be overridden or removed by table owners. Table-level filters need setting up on each table separately.\n`They run faster`: the docs say policy evaluation is more efficient, but the reason for many tables is central, tag-based management.\n`They support time travel`: ABAC time travel support is only Beta and limited, and it isn't why they're recommended here.\n`They work on DBR 10.4`: filters and masks need DBR 12.2 LTS or above. Older runtimes fail securely.", "fm"],
["A236", 7, "A cluster on Databricks Runtime 11.3 queries a table with a column mask. What happens?",
 ["It fails securely and returns no data", "It returns unmasked data", "It returns masked data", "It upgrades the runtime"],
 "Runtimes below 12.2 LTS don't support row filters or column masks. They fail securely: querying a protected table from one of them returns no data, so nothing unmasked leaks.\n`It returns unmasked data`: that would be a leak. Failing securely means no data is returned.\n`It returns masked data`: the old runtime can't run the mask, so it can't return masked values either.\n`It upgrades the runtime`: Databricks never upgrades a cluster's runtime because of a query.", "fm"],
["A237", 7, "A team wants to share a curated, group-dependent version of a table by wrapping it in a view. Which approach is that?",
 ["A dynamic view using functions like is_account_group_member()", "A materialized view with ABAC", "A streaming table", "A volume"],
 "A dynamic view is a SQL view over base tables that filters rows or masks columns using functions such as `is_account_group_member()`. It's the way to share a curated, group-dependent version with users who don't have access to the underlying table.\n`A materialized view with ABAC`: a materialized view stores precomputed results. ABAC applies tag-based policies. Neither is a view that changes per group.\n`A streaming table`: it's for incremental ingestion, not access control.\n`A volume`: it holds files, not a filtered view of a table.", "fm"],
["A238", 7, "The UNDROP retention window is set to 3 days on a catalog and 10 days on a schema inside it. A table in that schema is dropped. Which window applies?",
 ["10 days, because the schema setting wins", "3 days, because the catalog setting wins", "7 days, the default", "The shorter of the two"],
 "The UNDROP recovery period defaults to 7 days and can be set with `ALTER CATALOG` or `ALTER SCHEMA`. When both are set, the schema-level setting wins because it's more specific, so this table gets 10 days.\n`3 days, because the catalog setting wins`: the catalog value only applies to schemas without their own setting.\n`7 days, the default`: the default only applies when neither level sets a value.\n`The shorter of the two`: there's no shortest-wins rule. The schema setting takes precedence.", "undrop"],
["A239", 7, "What is the difference between owning a catalog and holding MANAGE on it?",
 ["The owner has every privilege including data access; MANAGE can administer permissions without reading data", "There is no difference", "MANAGE includes SELECT; ownership doesn't", "Only MANAGE can drop the catalog"],
 "The owner of a securable has every privilege on it, including reading data. `MANAGE` lets someone administer the object (grant, revoke, transfer ownership and drop it) without giving data access such as `SELECT`.\n`There is no difference`: the difference is data access. `MANAGE` doesn't include it.\n`MANAGE includes SELECT; ownership doesn't`: it's the other way round.\n`Only MANAGE can drop the catalog`: owners can drop it too. `MANAGE` holders can also drop it.", "perm"],
["A240", 7, "After `UNSET MANAGED` rolls a table back to external, what happens to the managed copy of the data?",
 ["It is deleted 7 days after the rollback", "It is kept forever", "It is deleted immediately", "It becomes a second table"],
 "`UNSET MANAGED` points the table back at its original external location. Databricks keeps writes made in the managed location after conversion, then deletes the data in the managed location 7 days after the rollback.\n`It is kept forever`: wrong. It's cleaned up automatically.\n`It is deleted immediately`: there's a 7-day wait first.\n`It becomes a second table`: the managed copy isn't registered as a separate table. It's just removed later.", "conv"],
["A241", 7, "A service principal should own production tables instead of an engineer. Which statement transfers ownership?",
 ["ALTER TABLE main.sales.orders OWNER TO `prod-sp`", "GRANT OWNER ON main.sales.orders TO `prod-sp`", "GRANT MANAGE ON main.sales.orders TO `prod-sp`", "SET OWNER main.sales.orders = `prod-sp`"],
 "Ownership is transferred with `ALTER <object> ... OWNER TO <principal>`, which makes the service principal the new owner with all of an owner's rights.\n`GRANT OWNER`: ownership isn't a privilege you can `GRANT`.\n`GRANT MANAGE`: gives admin rights such as granting and dropping, but the engineer stays the owner.\n`SET OWNER ... =`: not valid SQL.", "perm"],
["A242", 7, "Analysts have USE CATALOG and SELECT on a table, but their queries fail with a permission error. What is most likely missing?",
 ["USE SCHEMA on the table's schema", "MODIFY on the table", "MANAGE on the catalog", "READ FILES on the volume"],
 "Reading a table needs three privileges: `USE CATALOG` on its catalog, `USE SCHEMA` on its schema and `SELECT` on the table. The analysts have the first and last, so the missing one is `USE SCHEMA`.\n`MODIFY on the table`: needed for insert, update and delete, not for reading.\n`MANAGE on the catalog`: gives admin rights and isn't needed to query.\n`READ FILES on the volume`: `READ FILES` is an external location privilege, and file access isn't involved in a table query.", "perm"]
);

// Batch 3: specific privileges, and choosing an ingestion method. Appended after batch 2.
Object.assign(LINKS, {
  priv: DOC + "data-governance/unity-catalog/manage-privileges/privileges",
  ingest: DOC + "ingestion/cloud-object-storage/",
});

BANK.push(
// ---------- S7 Specific privileges ----------
["A243", 7, "An engineer must create new tables in `main.sales`. Which privileges do they need?",
 ["USE CATALOG on main, USE SCHEMA and CREATE TABLE on main.sales", "CREATE TABLE on main.sales only", "MODIFY on main.sales", "ALL PRIVILEGES on the metastore"],
 "`CREATE TABLE` is granted on the schema (or on the catalog so it's inherited), and the user also needs `USE CATALOG` on the catalog and `USE SCHEMA` on the schema to reach it.\n`CREATE TABLE on main.sales only`: not enough. Without `USE CATALOG` and `USE SCHEMA` the user can't get to the schema.\n`MODIFY on main.sales`: `MODIFY` changes data in existing tables. It doesn't create tables.\n`ALL PRIVILEGES on the metastore`: far more than needed, and it breaks least privilege.", "priv"],
["A244", 7, "A job must run UPDATE and DELETE statements on `main.sales.orders`. Which privilege covers those statements?",
 ["MODIFY, plus SELECT on the table and USE CATALOG / USE SCHEMA", "SELECT only", "WRITE FILES", "MANAGE"],
 "`MODIFY` covers adding, updating and deleting data in a table. To use it, the user also needs `SELECT` on the table and `USE CATALOG` and `USE SCHEMA` on its parents.\n`SELECT only`: lets them read, not change data.\n`WRITE FILES`: writes files at external location paths, not rows in a table.\n`MANAGE`: lets them administer permissions and the object, but doesn't cover changing table data.", "priv"],
["A245", 7, "A loader should only append rows to a table, never update or delete. What is the least-privilege option?",
 ["INSERT (Beta), a fine-grained alternative to MODIFY", "MODIFY", "ALL PRIVILEGES", "WRITE VOLUME"],
 "`INSERT`, `UPDATE` and `DELETE` are Beta privileges that each cover one part of `MODIFY`. Granting just `INSERT` lets the loader append rows without being able to update or delete them.\n`MODIFY`: works, but it also allows updates and deletes, so it isn't least privilege.\n`ALL PRIVILEGES`: grants every privilege on the table, the opposite of least privilege.\n`WRITE VOLUME`: it's for files in a volume, not rows in a table.", "priv"],
["A246", 7, "Analysts need to read CSV files stored in a Unity Catalog volume. Which privilege on the volume?",
 ["READ VOLUME", "SELECT", "READ FILES", "BROWSE"],
 "`READ VOLUME` lets users read files in a Unity Catalog volume, together with `USE CATALOG` and `USE SCHEMA` on its parents.\n`SELECT`: reads tables and views, not files in a volume.\n`READ FILES`: reads cloud storage directly through an external location, not through a volume.\n`BROWSE`: shows metadata only. It doesn't read file contents.", "priv"],
["A247", 7, "A user must upload new files into a Unity Catalog volume. Which privilege on the volume?",
 ["WRITE VOLUME", "MODIFY", "WRITE FILES", "CREATE VOLUME"],
 "`WRITE VOLUME` lets a user add, remove or change files in a volume, together with `USE CATALOG` and `USE SCHEMA` on its parents.\n`MODIFY`: changes data in tables, not files in a volume.\n`WRITE FILES`: writes to an external location path directly, not through a volume.\n`CREATE VOLUME`: creates a new volume. It doesn't let you write to an existing one.", "priv"],
["A248", 7, "A user must call the SQL function `main.util.clean_phone()` in their queries. Which privilege on the function?",
 ["EXECUTE", "SELECT", "USE FUNCTION", "MODIFY"],
 "`EXECUTE` lets a user invoke a function (or load a registered model). Databricks recommends granting it on individual functions, and the user also needs `USE CATALOG` and `USE SCHEMA`.\n`SELECT`: it's for reading tables and views, not calling functions.\n`USE FUNCTION`: not a Unity Catalog privilege.\n`MODIFY`: changes table data. It doesn't apply to functions.", "priv"],
["A249", 7, "A new hire should see which tables exist and read their metadata in Catalog Explorer, but not query any data. Which privilege?",
 ["BROWSE on the catalog", "SELECT on the catalog", "USE CATALOG only", "MANAGE on the catalog"],
 "`BROWSE` lets users discover objects and view their metadata in Catalog Explorer without `USE CATALOG`, `USE SCHEMA` or data access. For tables and other data objects it can only be granted at catalog level.\n`SELECT on the catalog`: gives the right to read data in its tables, which the new hire shouldn't have.\n`USE CATALOG only`: it lets you enter the catalog, but on its own it doesn't show the tables inside.\n`MANAGE on the catalog`: admin rights over the catalog, far more than metadata viewing.", "priv"],
["A250", 7, "A team lead must create new schemas in the `analytics` catalog. Which privileges do they need?",
 ["CREATE SCHEMA and USE CATALOG on analytics", "CREATE TABLE on analytics", "USE SCHEMA on analytics", "MANAGE on the metastore"],
 "`CREATE SCHEMA` is granted on the catalog, and the user also needs `USE CATALOG` on that catalog.\n`CREATE TABLE on analytics`: allows creating tables in the catalog's schemas, not new schemas.\n`USE SCHEMA on analytics`: lets them access existing schemas, not create new ones.\n`MANAGE on the metastore`: not needed, and much broader than this job requires.", "priv"],
["A251", 7, "A user has ALL PRIVILEGES on a catalog. Which of these does that NOT include?",
 ["MANAGE", "SELECT", "MODIFY", "CREATE TABLE"],
 "`ALL PRIVILEGES` gives every applicable privilege except `MANAGE`, `EXTERNAL USE SCHEMA`, `EXTERNAL USE LOCATION` and `READ METADATA`. Administering permissions needs `MANAGE` granted separately.\n`SELECT`: included in `ALL PRIVILEGES`.\n`MODIFY`: included in `ALL PRIVILEGES`.\n`CREATE TABLE`: included in `ALL PRIVILEGES` on a catalog.", "priv"],
["A252", 7, "`SHOW GRANTS` is run for a user who holds ALL PRIVILEGES on a table. What does it list?",
 ["Only ALL PRIVILEGES, not the individual privileges it implies", "Every implied privilege, such as SELECT and MODIFY", "Nothing", "Only ownership"],
 "`SHOW GRANTS` lists only the `ALL PRIVILEGES` grant. It doesn't expand it into the privileges it implies, such as `SELECT` or `MODIFY`.\n`Every implied privilege`: the user does have them, but `SHOW GRANTS` doesn't list them separately.\n`Nothing`: the grant exists, so it is listed.\n`Only ownership`: `ALL PRIVILEGES` isn't ownership, and ownership isn't what's being shown.", "priv"],
["A253", 7, "An engineer must create external tables at paths under an external location. Where should CREATE EXTERNAL TABLE be granted?",
 ["On the external location", "On the storage credential, always", "On the metastore", "On the target table"],
 "`CREATE EXTERNAL TABLE` can be granted on an external location or a storage credential. Databricks recommends the external location because it's limited to a path, while a credential can reach much more storage.\n`On the storage credential, always`: allowed, but not recommended, and certainly not always.\n`On the metastore`: `CREATE EXTERNAL TABLE` isn't granted on the metastore.\n`On the target table`: the table doesn't exist yet, so you can't grant anything on it.", "priv"],
["A254", 7, "An admin must create a new external location. Which privileges are needed?",
 ["CREATE EXTERNAL LOCATION on both the metastore and the storage credential", "CREATE EXTERNAL LOCATION on the catalog", "READ FILES on the bucket", "MANAGE on a schema"],
 "Creating an external location needs `CREATE EXTERNAL LOCATION` on both the metastore and the storage credential that the location will use.\n`On the catalog`: external locations are metastore-level objects, so the catalog isn't where it's granted.\n`READ FILES on the bucket`: you grant `READ FILES` on an external location after it exists. It doesn't let you create one.\n`MANAGE on a schema`: schema admin rights don't cover metastore-level objects.", "priv"],
["A255", 7, "A user has WRITE FILES on an external location but no other privileges on it. Can they write files there?",
 ["No; WRITE FILES also requires READ FILES on the same external location", "Yes", "Only to managed tables", "Only with a SQL warehouse"],
 "`WRITE FILES` only works when `READ FILES` is also granted on the same external location. With `WRITE FILES` alone, the user can't write.\n`Yes`: missing the `READ FILES` requirement.\n`Only to managed tables`: managed tables aren't written through `WRITE FILES` paths.\n`Only with a SQL warehouse`: the compute type doesn't change the privilege rule.", "priv"],
["A256", 7, "Who can grant USE CATALOG on a catalog?",
 ["The catalog owner or a user with MANAGE on it", "Any user with SELECT", "Any user with USE CATALOG", "Only the workspace admin"],
 "Only the catalog's owner or a user with `MANAGE` on the catalog can grant `USE CATALOG`. Having a privilege doesn't let you pass it on.\n`Any user with SELECT`: they can read data but can't grant privileges.\n`Any user with USE CATALOG`: holding it doesn't let you grant it to others.\n`Only the workspace admin`: Unity Catalog privileges are controlled by owners and `MANAGE` holders, not by workspace admin status alone.", "priv"],
["A257", 7, "An analyst must manually trigger a refresh of a materialized view they don't own. Which privilege?",
 ["REFRESH on the materialized view", "MODIFY on the materialized view", "SELECT on the materialized view", "EXECUTE on the pipeline"],
 "`REFRESH` on a materialized view lets a user trigger a refresh without owning it. They also need `USE CATALOG` and `USE SCHEMA` on its parents.\n`MODIFY`: changes data in tables. You can't change a materialized view's data directly.\n`SELECT`: lets them read it, not refresh it.\n`EXECUTE on the pipeline`: `EXECUTE` is for functions and models. Refreshing a materialized view is governed by `REFRESH`.", "priv"],
["A258", 7, "A data steward must add tags to tables and columns. Which privilege, and what extra is needed for a governed tag?",
 ["APPLY TAG; plus ASSIGN on the governed tag", "MODIFY; nothing else", "MANAGE; plus BROWSE", "SELECT; plus EXECUTE"],
 "`APPLY TAG` lets users add and edit tags on objects. A governed tag has controlled values, so applying one also needs `ASSIGN` on that specific governed tag.\n`MODIFY; nothing else`: `MODIFY` changes table data, not tags.\n`MANAGE; plus BROWSE`: admin rights and metadata viewing, neither the tagging privilege.\n`SELECT; plus EXECUTE`: read data and call functions, unrelated to tagging.", "priv"],
["A259", 7, "A schema needs its own managed storage location in a different bucket. Which privilege on that bucket's external location?",
 ["CREATE MANAGED STORAGE", "CREATE EXTERNAL TABLE", "WRITE FILES", "CREATE EXTERNAL VOLUME"],
 "`CREATE MANAGED STORAGE` on an external location lets you use a path in it as the managed storage location for a catalog or schema, overriding the default above it.\n`CREATE EXTERNAL TABLE`: creates external tables at the location, not managed storage.\n`WRITE FILES`: writes files directly. It doesn't let you set managed storage.\n`CREATE EXTERNAL VOLUME`: creates external volumes there, not a schema's managed storage.", "priv"],
["A260", 7, "`GRANT SELECT ON CATALOG main TO analysts` is the only grant. Can analysts query `main.sales.orders`?",
 ["No; they also need USE CATALOG on main and USE SCHEMA on main.sales", "Yes; SELECT on the catalog is enough", "Yes, but only views", "Only after a metastore admin approves"],
 "Privileges granted on a catalog are inherited by its schemas and tables, so `SELECT` covers the table. But reaching any object also needs `USE CATALOG` on the catalog and `USE SCHEMA` on the schema, and those weren't granted.\n`Yes; SELECT on the catalog is enough`: it's missing the `USE CATALOG` and `USE SCHEMA` requirement.\n`Yes, but only views`: views need the same `USE` privileges, and there's no views-only rule.\n`Only after a metastore admin approves`: Unity Catalog has no approval step. Access comes from grants.", "perm"],
["A261", 7, "A user was granted SELECT directly on a table and also through its schema. The schema-level grant is revoked. Can they still read the table?",
 ["Yes; the direct table grant still applies", "No; revoking at schema level removes all access below it", "Only for 7 days", "Only through views"],
 "Unity Catalog privileges are additive: each grant stands by itself. Revoking `SELECT` at the schema removes only that grant, so the direct `SELECT` on the table still lets the user read it, provided they still have the `USE` privileges.\n`No; revoking at schema level removes all access below it`: revoking removes that one grant and the access it gave through inheritance, not other separate grants.\n`Only for 7 days`: grants don't expire. Seven days is unrelated (for example, the UNDROP default).\n`Only through views`: the direct grant is on the table itself.", "perm"],
["A262", 7, "Where does Databricks recommend governing access to files, instead of granting READ FILES on external location paths?",
 ["Unity Catalog volumes", "DBFS root", "Cluster init scripts", "Workspace files"],
 "`READ FILES` reads cloud storage directly through an external location, which Databricks recommends against. Volumes put files under Unity Catalog governance with `READ VOLUME` and `WRITE VOLUME`.\n`DBFS root`: not governed by Unity Catalog, and Databricks recommends against storing data there.\n`Cluster init scripts`: they configure compute at startup. They don't govern access to files.\n`Workspace files`: for code and small files, not governed access to data files in cloud storage.", "priv"],
["A263", 7, "Which principals can receive a Unity Catalog GRANT?",
 ["Users, groups and service principals", "Only users", "Only groups", "Only service principals"],
 "Unity Catalog privileges can be granted to any principal: users, groups and service principals. Granting to groups is the usual practice.\n`Only users`: groups and service principals can also receive grants.\n`Only groups`: recommended, but not the only option.\n`Only service principals`: they're used for automation, but users and groups can receive grants too.", "perm"],

// ---------- S2 Choosing an ingestion method ----------
["A264", 2, "About a few thousand files will land in cloud storage over the life of a project. The team prefers SQL. Which method does the doc suggest is enough?",
 ["COPY INTO", "A Lakeflow Connect managed connector", "A Kafka streaming job", "The file upload UI"],
 "The docs say `COPY INTO` is fine when files are in the order of thousands over time. It's SQL, idempotent and simple. Auto Loader is the recommendation once you reach millions of files.\n`A Lakeflow Connect managed connector`: these pull from apps, databases and other systems, not files already landing in cloud storage.\n`A Kafka streaming job`: the source is files, not a message bus.\n`The file upload UI`: it's for a few files from your machine (up to 10, under 2 GB in total), not ongoing ingestion.", "ingest"],
["A265", 2, "Files arriving will reach millions over time. Which method does the doc recommend?",
 ["Auto Loader", "COPY INTO", "The file upload UI", "A manual dbutils.fs.ls loop"],
 "At millions of files, Auto Loader is less expensive and more efficient: it tracks which files it has seen and can use file notifications, so it needs fewer operations to discover new files.\n`COPY INTO`: the docs position it for thousands of files. At millions its file discovery is less efficient.\n`The file upload UI`: manual uploads of a few files only.\n`A manual dbutils.fs.ls loop`: listing millions of files by hand is slow and you have to track what's been loaded yourself.", "ingest"],
["A266", 2, "The source JSON schema changes often, with new fields each month. Which method handles this best?",
 ["Auto Loader", "COPY INTO", "The file upload UI", "A Lakeflow Connect SaaS connector"],
 "Auto Loader has better primitives for schema inference and evolution: it can infer the schema, track it, add new columns when they appear and rescue unexpected data.\n`COPY INTO`: it can merge schemas, but the docs say Auto Loader is better when the schema changes often.\n`The file upload UI`: a one-off manual load with no ongoing schema evolution.\n`A Lakeflow Connect SaaS connector`: for SaaS apps like Salesforce, not JSON files in storage.", "ingest"],
["A267", 2, "A partner re-uploads a handful of corrected files. An Auto Loader stream is running on the same folder. How can the team reload just those files?",
 ["Run COPY INTO on that subset while the stream keeps running", "Delete the Auto Loader checkpoint", "Restart the stream with a full refresh", "Use the file upload UI"],
 "The docs say reloading a subset of re-uploaded files is easier with `COPY INTO`, and it can run on those files while the Auto Loader stream keeps going.\n`Delete the Auto Loader checkpoint`: this makes the stream reprocess everything, not just the corrected files.\n`Restart the stream with a full refresh`: also reloads everything, far more than a handful of files.\n`The file upload UI`: uploads from a local machine. It doesn't reload files already in the cloud folder.", "ingest"],
["A268", 2, "A SQL-only team wants scalable, incremental file ingestion without writing Python. What do the docs point to?",
 ["Streaming tables, which use Auto Loader under the hood", "COPY INTO for millions of files", "A Python notebook with cloudFiles", "The file upload UI"],
 "Auto Loader lets SQL users use streaming tables: a streaming table defined in SQL uses Auto Loader underneath, so they get scalable, incremental file ingestion without Python.\n`COPY INTO for millions of files`: `COPY INTO` is SQL, but the docs position it for thousands of files, not millions.\n`A Python notebook with cloudFiles`: that is Auto Loader, but in Python, which the team wants to avoid.\n`The file upload UI`: manual and one-off, not scalable or incremental.", "ingest"],
["A269", 2, "Ingestion code is moving to production and needs production-ready infrastructure. What do the docs recommend?",
 ["Lakeflow pipelines", "An interactive notebook on all-purpose compute", "Manual COPY INTO runs", "The file upload UI"],
 "Lakeflow pipelines focus on deploying production-ready infrastructure: they're defined declaratively and run as managed pipelines instead of interactive notebook runs.\n`An interactive notebook on all-purpose compute`: fine for development, but not the production-ready setup the docs recommend.\n`Manual COPY INTO runs`: need someone to run them, which isn't production automation.\n`The file upload UI`: one-off manual loads only.", "ingest"],
["A270", 2, "Sales data lives in Salesforce. The team wants a governed, incremental feed into Unity Catalog with no custom code. Which method?",
 ["A Lakeflow Connect managed SaaS connector", "Auto Loader", "COPY INTO", "A file arrival trigger"],
 "Lakeflow Connect managed SaaS connectors pull data from apps like Salesforce, HubSpot, Jira and Workday into Unity Catalog incrementally, with no custom code, and they're governed by Unity Catalog.\n`Auto Loader`: reads files from cloud storage. It can't connect to the Salesforce API.\n`COPY INTO`: also loads files from storage, not data from a SaaS app.\n`A file arrival trigger`: starts a job when files land. It doesn't ingest anything itself.", "lfc"],
["A271", 2, "An on-premises SQL Server database has change data capture enabled. Changes must flow into Unity Catalog tables. Which method?",
 ["A Lakeflow Connect database connector (with an ingestion gateway)", "COPY INTO", "Auto Loader on the database files", "The file upload UI"],
 "Lakeflow Connect database connectors read changes from databases like SQL Server using CDC or change tracking. They run an ingestion gateway and staging storage to capture changes continuously into Unity Catalog tables.\n`COPY INTO`: loads files from cloud storage. It can't read database changes.\n`Auto Loader on the database files`: Auto Loader reads data files landing in storage. It can't read a running database's internal files or change capture.\n`The file upload UI`: one-off manual uploads only.", "lfc"],
["A272", 2, "The Salesforce connector must keep the full history of every changed account record. Which setting?",
 ["Turn on history tracking (SCD type 2)", "Leave the default (SCD type 1)", "Use COPY INTO with force = true", "Use Auto Loader rescue mode"],
 "SCD type 2 (history tracking) keeps a new row version for each change with start and end markers, so the full history of a record is kept. Turn it on in the connector where it's supported.\n`Leave the default (SCD type 1)`: type 1 overwrites the row in place, so earlier values are lost.\n`COPY INTO with force = true`: reloads files regardless of load history. It has nothing to do with Salesforce history.\n`Auto Loader rescue mode`: saves unexpected fields from files, not record history.", "scd"],
["A273", 2, "Files land in S3 daily, about 200 per day, with a stable schema. The SQL team wants re-runnable loads that never load a file twice. Which method fits with the least setup?",
 ["COPY INTO", "A Lakeflow Connect database connector", "A continuous Auto Loader stream in directory listing mode", "The file upload UI"],
 "`COPY INTO` is SQL and idempotent: it remembers which files it has loaded and skips them on re-runs. At about 200 files a day, the scale is well within its thousands range, so it's the simple fit.\n`A Lakeflow Connect database connector`: for databases with CDC, not files in S3.\n`A continuous Auto Loader stream in directory listing mode`: would work, but it's more than this scale and stable schema needs, and the team prefers SQL re-runs.\n`The file upload UI`: manual uploads, not repeatable daily loads from S3.", "ingest"],
["A274", 2, "A one-off spreadsheet export from a manager's laptop must become a table today. Which method?",
 ["The file upload UI (Create or modify table)", "Auto Loader", "A Lakeflow Connect connector", "COPY INTO from the laptop path"],
 "The file upload UI (Create or modify table) turns local files into a table straight away: up to 10 files at a time, under 2 GB in total, in formats like CSV, JSON and Excel.\n`Auto Loader`: for ongoing ingestion from cloud storage, too much setup for a one-off file on a laptop.\n`A Lakeflow Connect connector`: connects to apps and databases, not a file on someone's machine.\n`COPY INTO from the laptop path`: `COPY INTO` reads from cloud storage, and it can't reach a laptop's local disk.", "upload"],
["A275", 2, "An internal app has no managed connector, and the team needs full control over how it's read. What do the docs suggest?",
 ["SQL/Spark APIs or a custom connector", "A Lakeflow Connect managed connector anyway", "COPY INTO", "The file upload UI"],
 "With no managed connector, the docs point to the SQL and Spark APIs or a custom connector. They give up some automation in exchange for broader source support and full control over how the source is read.\n`A Lakeflow Connect managed connector anyway`: there isn't one for this app, so this isn't an option.\n`COPY INTO`: loads files from cloud storage, not data from an app.\n`The file upload UI`: manual one-off uploads only.", "lfc"],
["A276", 2, "Which ingestion option is described as low-code and scalable, from a variety of sources, using third-party tools?",
 ["Partner integrations", "COPY INTO", "Auto Loader", "The file upload UI"],
 "Partner integrations are validated third-party tools (for example through Partner Connect) that ingest from many sources into Databricks with little code.\n`COPY INTO`: Databricks' own SQL command for files in cloud storage, not a third-party tool.\n`Auto Loader`: a built-in Databricks feature for files, not third-party.\n`The file upload UI`: built-in and manual, not scalable.", "ingest"],
["A277", 2, "A team needs RabbitMQ messages ingested into Unity Catalog with a managed experience. Which kind of Lakeflow Connect connector covers it?",
 ["A streaming connector", "A SaaS connector", "A database CDC connector", "A file source connector"],
 "Lakeflow Connect streaming connectors read continuously from message buses and event streams. RabbitMQ is listed under them.\n`A SaaS connector`: for enterprise apps like Salesforce and Workday, not message buses.\n`A database CDC connector`: reads change data from databases like SQL Server, MySQL and PostgreSQL.\n`A file source connector`: ingests files from services like SharePoint and Google Drive.", "lfc"],
["A278", 2, "Which statement correctly contrasts Auto Loader and Lakeflow Connect?",
 ["Auto Loader reads files from cloud storage; Lakeflow Connect pulls from apps, databases and other systems", "They are two names for the same feature", "Auto Loader is only for databases", "Lakeflow Connect only reads Parquet files"],
 "Auto Loader (like `COPY INTO`) ingests files that land in cloud object storage. Lakeflow Connect pulls data from other systems: SaaS apps, databases, file services and streaming sources.\n`Two names for the same feature`: they cover different sources and are separate features.\n`Auto Loader is only for databases`: Auto Loader is for files. Database ingestion is Lakeflow Connect.\n`Lakeflow Connect only reads Parquet files`: it connects to apps, databases and streams, not just one file format.", "lfc"]
);


BANK.push(
// ---------- Added 2026-11-25: autoloader ----------
["A279", 2, "A SQL analyst defines `CREATE OR REFRESH STREAMING TABLE raw_orders AS SELECT * FROM ...` over JSON files in a volume. Each refresh should pick up only files that arrived since the last refresh. Which `FROM` clause fits?",
 ["`FROM STREAM read_files('/Volumes/main/raw/orders', format => 'json')`", "`FROM read_files('/Volumes/main/raw/orders', format => 'json')`", "`FROM read_files('/Volumes/main/raw/orders', format => 'json', schemaEvolutionMode => 'addNewColumns')`", "`FROM read_files('/Volumes/main/raw/orders', format => 'json', includeExistingFiles => false)`"],
 "`read_files` only uses Auto Loader, and so only reads new files incrementally, when it is called with the `STREAM` keyword inside a streaming table.\n`FROM read_files(...)` without `STREAM`: a batch read that discovers every file under the directory each time it runs.\n`schemaEvolutionMode => 'addNewColumns'`: controls how new columns are handled; without `STREAM` it is still a batch read of every file.\n`includeExistingFiles => false`: a streaming option evaluated when a stream first starts; it doesn't turn a batch read into a stream.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/sql/language-manual/functions/read_files"],
["A280", 2, "An engineer moves a notebook Auto Loader stream into a Lakeflow pipeline as a `@dp.table` function. The notebook version set `cloudFiles.schemaLocation` and a `checkpointLocation`. What should happen to those settings?",
 ["Drop both; the pipeline manages the schema and checkpoint locations", "Keep both, pointing at a Unity Catalog volume owned by the pipeline", "Keep `checkpointLocation` only; the pipeline cannot infer schemas", "Keep `cloudFiles.schemaLocation` only; the pipeline manages checkpoints"],
 "In a Lakeflow pipeline you don't provide a schema or checkpoint location for Auto Loader: the pipeline manages both for every streaming table.\n`Keep both` in a volume: that is the right pattern for a standalone Structured Streaming job, but a pipeline doesn't need either path.\n`Keep checkpointLocation only`: Auto Loader in pipelines still handles schema inference and evolution, and checkpoints are managed too.\n`Keep cloudFiles.schemaLocation only`: the schema location is managed by the pipeline just like the checkpoint.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ingestion/cloud-object-storage/auto-loader/"],
["A281", 2, "An Auto Loader stream was started last week with default options and loaded all historical files. The team now sets `cloudFiles.includeExistingFiles` to `false` and restarts it with the same checkpoint. What changes?",
 ["Nothing; the option is only evaluated when the stream first starts", "Rows already loaded from historical files are removed from the target", "Files already in the path but not yet processed are now skipped", "The stream fails because the option conflicts with the checkpoint"],
 "`cloudFiles.includeExistingFiles` is read only the first time a stream starts; after that the checkpoint decides what has been processed, and the docs say changing the option on restart has no effect.\n`Rows ... removed`: Auto Loader never deletes data it has already written to the target.\n`not yet processed ... skipped`: that would only happen if the option were set before the very first start.\n`The stream fails`: the docs describe the change as simply having no effect, not as an error.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ingestion/cloud-object-storage/auto-loader/options"],
["A282", 2, "A new Auto Loader stream points at a bucket with five years of files. Only files modified after 1 January 2026 should be loaded, including those already in the bucket. Which option fits?",
 ["`modifiedAfter` set to the 1 January 2026 timestamp", "`cloudFiles.includeExistingFiles` set to `false`", "`pathGlobFilter` with a pattern matching 2026 dates", "`cloudFiles.backfillInterval` set to the 2026 start date"],
 "`modifiedAfter` filters on each file's modification timestamp, so it keeps 2026 files that already exist and drops older ones.\n`includeExistingFiles = false`: skips every file present when the stream first starts, including the wanted 2026 files.\n`pathGlobFilter`: matches file names, not modification times, so it only works if names happen to contain dates.\n`backfillInterval`: sets how often Auto Loader runs asynchronous backfills, not a date cut-off.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ingestion/cloud-object-storage/auto-loader/options"],
["A283", 2, "A vendor overwrites the same file, `prices.csv`, in place every day. Auto Loader runs with default settings. What should the team expect?",
 ["The path is processed once, with no guarantee which version is read", "Each overwrite is ingested again as a new batch of rows", "The stream fails with an error when the file is overwritten", "Rows from the new version update matching rows in the target"],
 "With the default `cloudFiles.allowOverwrites = false`, each file is processed exactly once, and if it is appended to or overwritten Auto Loader can't guarantee which version it read.\n`Each overwrite is ingested again`: that is what `allowOverwrites` is for, and it is off by default.\n`The stream fails`: an overwrite doesn't raise an error; the change just isn't reliably picked up.\n`update matching rows`: Auto Loader appends rows; updating needs a `MERGE` or AUTO CDC downstream.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ingestion/cloud-object-storage/auto-loader/faq"],
["A284", 2, "Processed files in `s3://landing/orders/` should be archived automatically to cut clutter in the landing folder. Which Auto Loader configuration is valid?",
 ["`cleanSource` = `MOVE`, destination `s3://landing/archive/orders/`", "`cleanSource` = `MOVE`, destination `s3://landing/orders/archive/`", "`cleanSource` = `DELETE`, `retentionDuration` = `3 days`", "`cleanSource` = `MOVE`, destination in a different S3 bucket"],
 "A `MOVE` destination must be outside the source directory but in the same external location or volume, so a sibling folder in the same bucket is valid.\n`s3://landing/orders/archive/`: a child of the source, so archived files would be ingested again.\n`DELETE` with `3 days`: `DELETE` needs a retention duration greater than 7 days, and it deletes rather than archives.\n`different S3 bucket`: cross-bucket moves aren't supported and result in an error.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ingestion/cloud-object-storage/auto-loader/clean-source"],
["A285", 2, "Two Auto Loader streams read the same landing directory into different tables. One team enables `cloudFiles.cleanSource = DELETE` on their stream. What is the risk?",
 ["The faster stream removes files before the slower one ingests them", "Both streams fail because clean source needs an exclusive directory", "Files are deleted at once, before the retention duration has passed", "The other stream reprocesses all files when they are deleted"],
 "Clean source acts on the files themselves, so whichever stream cleans first removes them for everyone; Databricks advises against it when several streams share a source directory.\n`Both streams fail`: it isn't blocked or an error, just not recommended.\n`deleted at once`: files only become candidates after the retention duration (default 30 days) measured from commit time.\n`reprocesses all files`: a deleted file can't be read again; the slower stream simply never sees it.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ingestion/cloud-object-storage/auto-loader/clean-source"],
["A286", 2, "An Auto Loader stream uses legacy file notification mode (`cloudFiles.useNotifications`). The business requires that every file is eventually ingested even if a notification is lost. What do the docs recommend?",
 ["Set `cloudFiles.backfillInterval` to trigger regular backfills", "Set `cloudFiles.includeExistingFiles` to `true` and restart", "Point the stream at a fresh checkpoint location each week", "Set `cloudFiles.allowOverwrites` to `true` on the stream"],
 "`cloudFiles.backfillInterval` runs periodic asynchronous backfills that catch any missed files within an SLA, without creating duplicates. With file events on the external location Databricks handles this for you instead.\n`includeExistingFiles` and restart: the option is only read on a stream's first start, so a restart changes nothing.\n`fresh checkpoint`: starts a brand-new stream that re-ingests every file, creating duplicates.\n`allowOverwrites`: governs re-reading changed files, not finding missed ones.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ingestion/cloud-object-storage/auto-loader/file-notification-mode"],
["A287", 2, "An Auto Loader stream uses file events on its external location but only runs as a monthly `availableNow` job. What does Databricks warn about?",
 ["The file events cache can expire, so Auto Loader falls back to a full directory listing", "File events are dropped after 7 days, so files that landed earlier are skipped", "The checkpoint expires after 30 days, so the stream restarts from the beginning", "`availableNow` isn't supported with file events, so the job fails on start"],
 "File events discovery relies on a cache that can expire if the stream runs rarely; Auto Loader then falls back to directory listing, so Databricks says to run it at least every 7 days.\n`files ... skipped`: nothing is lost; discovery just becomes a slower, costlier full listing.\n`checkpoint expires`: the checkpoint stores progress so the stream resumes where it left off; it doesn't time out.\n`availableNow isn't supported`: scheduled `availableNow` jobs are a recommended way to run Auto Loader.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ingestion/cloud-object-storage/auto-loader/production"],
["A288", 2, "Files land at random times a few times a day. Data should appear soon after each drop, but the team doesn't want to pay for compute while nothing arrives. Which Auto Loader setup fits?",
 ["An `availableNow` job started by a file arrival trigger", "A stream with `processingTime='1 minute'` running all day", "A job using `trigger(once=True)` on an hourly schedule", "A stream with a `continuous='1 second'` trigger"],
 "`availableNow` processes what is waiting and then stops, so compute only runs when there is work, and a file arrival trigger starts the job as files land, keeping latency low.\n`processingTime='1 minute'`: low latency, but the cluster runs all day, and it is especially costly in directory listing mode.\n`trigger(once=True)`: deprecated in favour of `availableNow`, and an hourly schedule adds up to an hour of delay.\n`continuous`: not supported on Databricks; real-time mode replaces it.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ingestion/cloud-object-storage/auto-loader/production"],
["A289", 2, "Auto Loader infers the schema of nested JSON. The array `items` holds structs, and every `qty` inside them must be typed INT while the rest stays inferred. Which schema hint is correct?",
 ["`items.element.qty INT`", "`items[*].qty INT`", "`items.value.qty INT`", "`items.qty.element INT`"],
 "Schema hints use dot notation for nested fields and the keyword `element` for an array's elements, as in the docs' example `users.element.id INT`.\n`items[*].qty`: JSON-path style syntax, which schema hints don't use.\n`items.value.qty`: `value` isn't the array-element keyword; arrays use `element`.\n`items.qty.element`: puts `element` in the wrong place, treating `qty` as the array instead of `items`.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ingestion/cloud-object-storage/auto-loader/schema"],
["A290", 2, "Dozens of partners send JSON whose structure changes often and differs by partner. The bronze table must never stop on schema changes, must keep key case and nulls as sent, and analysts will pick out fields later. Which ingestion choice fits?",
 ["Auto Loader with `singleVariantColumn` into a VARIANT column", "Auto Loader with `schemaEvolutionMode` set to `addNewColumns`", "Auto Loader with `schemaEvolutionMode` set to `failOnNewColumns`", "Auto Loader with `cloudFiles.inferColumnTypes` set to `true`"],
 "`singleVariantColumn` stores each whole record in one VARIANT column, which tolerates schema and type changes and keeps case sensitivity and nulls from the source.\n`addNewColumns`: evolves the schema, but the stream stops with an `UnknownFieldException` each time a new column appears.\n`failOnNewColumns`: fails on a new column and stays failed until someone fixes the schema.\n`inferColumnTypes`: only changes how column types are inferred; new columns still stop the stream under the default mode.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ingestion/variant"],
["A292", 2, "Millions of scanned PDF contracts will land in a volume over the coming years. Each new file must be loaded once, as raw bytes, into a Delta table for later AI processing. Which approach fits?",
 ["Auto Loader with `cloudFiles.format` = `binaryFile` and `pathGlobFilter` = `*.pdf`", "Auto Loader with `cloudFiles.format` = `text` and the `wholetext` option set", "A nightly `spark.read.format('binaryFile')` overwrite of the Delta table", "A nightly `COPY INTO` with `FILEFORMAT = BINARYFILE` and a `FILES` list"],
 "Auto Loader supports `binaryFile`, which returns each file's raw bytes in a `content` column, tracks processed files in its checkpoint, and scales to millions of files; `pathGlobFilter` keeps only PDFs.\n`text` with `wholetext`: reads files as strings, not raw bytes, which doesn't suit binary PDFs.\n`spark.read ... overwrite`: a batch read with no state, so it re-reads every file each night.\n`COPY INTO`: suggested for thousands of files; at millions Databricks points to Auto Loader, and `FILES` takes at most 1,000 names.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ingestion/cloud-object-storage/auto-loader/patterns"],
["A293", 2, "Auto Loader writes to the Unity Catalog external table `bronze.orders` stored at `s3://lake/tables/orders`. An engineer sets the checkpoint to `s3://lake/tables/orders/_checkpoint`. What is the problem?",
 ["Unity Catalog doesn't allow checkpoint files nested under the table directory", "Checkpoints for Unity Catalog tables must be on the driver's local disk", "Auto Loader can't write to external tables, only to managed ones", "The checkpoint must sit under the source path so files can be tracked"],
 "Unity Catalog doesn't allow checkpoint or schema evolution files inside a table's directory; Databricks recommends a separate UC-governed location such as a volume.\n`driver's local disk`: checkpoints must be durable storage; the docs recommend Unity Catalog-managed storage.\n`can't write to external tables`: it can, as long as the table location is in an external location and the checkpoint uses a separate path.\n`under the source path`: Auto Loader tracks files in the checkpoint itself, which doesn't need to be near the source.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ingestion/cloud-object-storage/auto-loader/unity-catalog"],
["A294", 2, "To change a setting, an engineer points a running Auto Loader stream at a new, empty checkpoint location, leaving other options at their defaults. What happens on restart?",
 ["It runs as a new stream and ingests all existing files again", "It resumes from the old progress, which is copied across", "It only picks up files that arrive after the restart", "It fails because the target table already holds data"],
 "The checkpoint holds the record of processed files, so a new checkpoint means a brand-new stream, and with the default `cloudFiles.includeExistingFiles = true` it re-ingests every file already in the path, duplicating rows.\n`resumes from the old progress`: nothing is copied; the old checkpoint is simply abandoned.\n`only picks up files that arrive after`: that needs `includeExistingFiles = false` set before the first start.\n`fails because the target holds data`: Auto Loader appends to the existing table without error.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ingestion/cloud-object-storage/auto-loader/faq"],
// ---------- Added 2026-11-25: pipelines ----------
["A295", 3, "A silver streaming table joins a stream of `orders` to a static `customers` table to add `region`. A customer moves region, but their old orders still show the old region. What explains this, and what keeps the joined result current?",
 ["Processed rows are not rejoined; define the join as a materialized view", "The static table is cached per cluster; restart the pipeline's compute", "Stream-static joins need a watermark; add one on the `customers` read", "The read needs `skipChangeCommits` on `customers` to pick up updates"],
 "In a stream-static join, each update joins only the new stream rows to the current snapshot of the static table. Rows already written are not recalculated unless you run a full refresh. A materialized view recomputes the join when the dimension changes.\n`restart the pipeline's compute`: the static side is already read fresh on each update. Restarting doesn't rejoin rows that were already processed.\n`watermark`: a watermark limits how long state is kept for late data. It has no effect on rows that were already joined.\n`skipChangeCommits`: this tells a streaming read to ignore update and delete commits. `customers` is the static side here, and the option wouldn't rejoin anything.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ldp/transform"],
["A296", 3, "In a SQL pipeline, `CREATE OR REFRESH STREAMING TABLE customers_silver` must read only the new rows of `customers_bronze`, a streaming table in the same pipeline. Which `FROM` clause does that?",
 ["`FROM STREAM(customers_bronze)`", "`FROM LIVE.customers_bronze`", "`FROM table_changes('customers_bronze', 0)`", "`FROM read_files('customers_bronze')`"],
 "A streaming table's query must be a streaming query. The `STREAM` keyword gives the read streaming semantics, so each update processes only rows added since the last one.\n`LIVE.customers_bronze`: `LIVE` is the virtual schema from the legacy publishing mode. It names the table but doesn't make the read incremental.\n`table_changes(...)`: this reads a Delta change data feed as a batch query. It isn't how a pipeline streaming table reads a streaming table upstream.\n`read_files(...)`: this reads files from a path or volume, not a table name.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ldp/developer/ldp-sql-ref-create-streaming-table"],
["A297", 3, "A streaming table `events` already ingests a Kafka topic for the EU region. A US topic must now feed the same table, without a full refresh of `events`. What should the engineer add?",
 ["A second `@dp.append_flow` that targets the existing `events` table", "A `UNION ALL` of both topics inside the `events` table query", "A materialized view that unions the EU and US topics together", "An `AUTO CDC` flow into `events` keyed on the event ID"],
 "Flows can be defined separately from their target, and any number of append flows can write to one streaming table. Docs list two reasons to use them: adding a new streaming source without a full refresh, and combining sources without a `UNION`.\n`UNION ALL inside the query`: this rewrites the table's definition instead of adding a flow, and docs point to append flows for exactly this case.\n`materialized view that unions`: this would recompute from both topics instead of incrementally adding the US stream to the existing `events` table.\n`AUTO CDC flow`: AUTO CDC applies change events (upserts and deletes). New Kafka events are plain appends.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ldp/flows"],
["A298", 3, "A streaming table `users` gets new rows from a stream. Two years of history sit in a static table `user_archive` and must be loaded into `users` once. Which definition fits?",
 ["`CREATE FLOW backfill AS INSERT ONCE INTO users BY NAME SELECT * FROM user_archive`", "`CREATE FLOW backfill AS INSERT INTO users BY NAME SELECT * FROM STREAM(user_archive)`", "`CREATE TEMPORARY VIEW backfill AS SELECT * FROM user_archive`, unioned into `users`", "`CREATE MATERIALIZED VIEW backfill AS SELECT * FROM user_archive UNION ALL users`"],
 "`INSERT ONCE INTO` makes the flow a batch query that runs one time, as a backfill does. It runs again only if the target is fully refreshed. A separate streaming flow keeps feeding new rows.\n`INSERT INTO ... STREAM(user_archive)`: a flow without `ONCE` is a streaming query that runs on every update. A one-off load of a static history table doesn't need that.\n`temporary view ... unioned into users`: this changes the table's query, and the archive would become part of every refresh instead of loading once.\n`materialized view ... UNION ALL users`: this creates a separate dataset and doesn't load the history into `users`.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ldp/developer/ldp-sql-ref-create-flow"],
["A299", 3, "A pipeline's source code is split across several files. The silver file appears before the bronze file in the folder, and silver reads from bronze. What does the engineer need to do so bronze runs first?",
 ["Nothing; the pipeline infers the dependencies and builds a DAG", "Rename the files so they sort with bronze first", "List the bronze file first in the pipeline's source settings", "Split the files into separate tasks of a Lakeflow Job"],
 "Pipelines infer the dependencies between datasets from the references in the code and arrange them in a DAG. Upstream datasets are computed first, so code can sit across files in any order.\n`Rename the files`: file names and sort order don't decide run order. The dependency graph does.\n`List the bronze file first`: the order of source files in the settings doesn't matter either, for the same reason.\n`separate tasks of a Lakeflow Job`: this adds manual orchestration for something the pipeline already does, and loses running them as one unit.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ldp/concepts/pipelines"],
["A300", 3, "The same pipeline source code is deployed to a `dev` catalog and a `prod` catalog. How should tables be referenced in the code?",
 ["By unqualified name, with each pipeline's default catalog and schema set", "By fully qualified name, with a different copy of the code per catalog", "With the `LIVE.` prefix, which resolves to the pipeline's target", "By three-part name built from a widget read at the start of the code"],
 "A Unity Catalog pipeline publishes its tables to the default catalog and schema set on the pipeline. Unqualified names let the same code target `dev` or `prod` just by changing those settings, which is why docs advise against fully qualified names in source code.\n`fully qualified name, a copy per catalog`: this hard-codes the environment and means keeping several copies of the code in sync.\n`LIVE. prefix`: `LIVE` belongs to the legacy publishing mode used by older pipelines. It isn't how current pipelines resolve names.\n`widget read at the start`: dataset code should only define datasets. The pipeline's default catalog and schema settings already handle this.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ldp/unity-catalog"],
["A301", 3, "A complex silver transformation is split into steps. One intermediate step must be checked with expectations, is not needed outside the pipeline, and should add no storage cost. Which dataset type fits that step?",
 ["Temporary view", "Materialized view", "Private streaming table", "View published to the schema"],
 "Temporary views are private to the pipeline, aren't added to the catalog, and support expectations. Like other views, they suit validating intermediate steps without paying to store the result.\n`Materialized view`: it supports expectations, but it persists data and is published, so it adds storage cost.\n`Private streaming table`: `private` hides it from the catalog, but it still stores data for the life of the pipeline.\n`View published to the schema`: views created in a pipeline don't support expectations, and they are visible outside it.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ldp/developer/ldp-sql-ref-create-temporary-view"],
["A302", 3, "An engineer adds `CONSTRAINT positive_amount EXPECT (amount > 0) ON VIOLATION DROP ROW` to a `CREATE VIEW` in a pipeline, so analysts see only valid rows. The update fails. Why?",
 ["Views created in a pipeline don't support expectations", "`DROP ROW` is only allowed on streaming tables", "A view's expectations must use `ON VIOLATION FAIL UPDATE`", "Expectations need the Python API, not SQL"],
 "The pipeline `CREATE VIEW` reference says views don't support expectations (`CONSTRAINT` clauses). Expectations work on streaming tables, materialized views and temporary views, so the check belongs upstream or on a temporary view.\n`DROP ROW only on streaming tables`: `DROP ROW` also works on materialized views and temporary views. The problem is the view type, not the action.\n`must use FAIL UPDATE`: no action is allowed on a view, so changing the action doesn't help.\n`need the Python API`: SQL supports expectations through `CONSTRAINT ... EXPECT`. Only grouping several expectations with one collective action is Python-only.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ldp/developer/ldp-sql-ref-create-view"],
["A303", 3, "Invalid trip records must not reach the silver table, but the data team must still be able to review every one of them, and the pipeline must keep running. Which approach fits?",
 ["Flag rows as `is_quarantined` in a private table and split them with views", "Apply `@dp.expect_or_drop` to the silver table for each rule", "Apply `@dp.expect_or_fail` so failing batches are held back for review", "Apply `@dp.expect` so invalid rows are written and counted in metrics"],
 "The quarantine pattern combines expectations with a private table and views. Rows are flagged, valid rows go to silver and invalid rows go somewhere else, so nothing is lost and the pipeline keeps running.\n`expect_or_drop`: silver stays clean, but the dropped rows are gone and the team can't review them.\n`expect_or_fail`: this fails the update and rolls back the write. The pipeline stops instead of holding the bad rows aside.\n`expect` (warn): invalid rows are counted in metrics but still written to silver, which the requirement forbids.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ldp/expectation-patterns"],
["A304", 3, "A pipeline has a validation materialized view with `@dp.expect_or_fail` checking that silver and gold row counts match. Gold tables read from silver, not from the validation view. The team wants gold to stop updating whenever the check fails. What should they do?",
 ["Put validation and gold in separate pipelines ordered by a Lakeflow Job", "Nothing; a failed expectation stops every table in the pipeline", "Change the check to `@dp.expect_or_drop` so failing rows block gold", "Make the gold tables temporary views so they wait for validation"],
 "Expectations decide which records reach a dataset, not whether other parts of the pipeline run, so a validation table doesn't stop tables that don't read from it. To halt downstream work, docs recommend separate pipelines coordinated by a job.\n`a failed expectation stops every table`: in a triggered pipeline, a failing flow doesn't stop parallel flows, so gold can still update.\n`expect_or_drop`: dropping rows from the validation view doesn't affect gold, which reads from silver.\n`temporary views`: making gold temporary views doesn't create a dependency on the validation view. It also stops publishing the gold tables.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ldp/expectation-patterns"],
["A305", 3, "In a triggered pipeline, table `orders_silver` has an `expect_or_fail` expectation that a row violates. `customers_silver` is an independent flow running in parallel. What happens?",
 ["`orders_silver` is rolled back; `customers_silver` carries on", "Both tables are rolled back and the whole update stops", "`orders_silver` keeps its valid rows and drops the bad one", "`orders_silver` writes all rows and logs a warning"],
 "A fail expectation rolls back that table's transaction atomically. In a triggered pipeline, one flow failing doesn't stop flows running in parallel, so `customers_silver` finishes.\n`Both tables are rolled back`: this is too broad. Only the failing flow is affected. (In continuous mode, a failure stops that flow and the flows that depend on it.)\n`keeps its valid rows and drops the bad one`: that is `expect_or_drop` behaviour, not fail.\n`writes all rows and logs a warning`: that is the default warn behaviour of `expect`.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ldp/expectations"],
["A306", 3, "A data quality lead wants a dashboard of how many rows each `expect_or_drop` rule removed per day across several pipelines. Where should the data come from?",
 ["The pipeline event log", "The `system.access.audit` table", "The Lakeflow Jobs run history", "The target table's `_rescued_data` column"],
 "Data quality metrics for warn and drop expectations appear in the pipeline UI and can be queried from the pipeline event log, which makes the event log the source for a dashboard across pipelines.\n`system.access.audit`: this records user and service actions in the workspace, not rows dropped by expectations.\n`Lakeflow Jobs run history`: this shows task runs and durations, not per-expectation row counts.\n`_rescued_data`: this is an Auto Loader column for data that doesn't match the schema. Dropped rows never reach the target table.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ldp/expectations"],
["A307", 3, "A silver streaming table reads the Delta table `bronze.orders` with `spark.readStream`. An engineer runs `MERGE INTO bronze.orders` to fix some prices. What happens on the next pipeline update?",
 ["The read fails, since Delta streaming sources accept only appends", "The changed rows are read again and upserted into the silver table", "The changed rows are skipped and the stream carries on", "The silver table is fully refreshed to pick up the corrections"],
 "Structured Streaming only accepts appends from a Delta source. An `UPDATE`, `DELETE`, `MERGE` or overwrite on the source makes the stream fail unless the read is configured otherwise.\n`read again and upserted`: streaming tables process each row once and don't apply source updates. That needs AUTO CDC on a change feed, or a materialized view.\n`skipped and the stream carries on`: this only happens if you set `skipChangeCommits`, and even then the corrections never reach silver.\n`fully refreshed`: a full refresh never happens automatically. Someone has to run it.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/structured-streaming/delta-lake"],
["A308", 3, "A small team is choosing between a Lakeflow pipeline and notebooks that run Structured Streaming in a Lakeflow Job. They don't want to manage checkpoints, retries or the order tables run in, and want row-level quality checks built in. What fits?",
 ["A Lakeflow pipeline with expectations", "Notebooks with `writeStream` and job retries", "Notebooks using Auto Loader with `availableNow`", "Scheduled Databricks SQL queries with `MERGE`"],
 "Lakeflow pipelines handle orchestration, checkpointing, retries and incremental processing for you. Expectations check data quality on every row of every run, which matches every requirement.\n`writeStream and job retries`: the job retries failed tasks, but the team still manages checkpoints, run order and quality checks.\n`Auto Loader with availableNow`: this is a good choice for ingesting files, but it is still hand-written streaming code with no built-in expectations or dependency handling.\n`Databricks SQL queries with MERGE`: these are batch queries the team must schedule and order themselves, with no managed checkpoints or row-level expectations.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ldp/concepts/how-to-use-pipelines"],
["A309", 3, "A pipeline on classic compute has a gold materialized view that aggregates a large silver table. Every update recomputes the whole view, even when little has changed. Which change lets it refresh incrementally?",
 ["Move the pipeline to serverless compute", "Switch the pipeline to continuous mode", "Enable Photon on the classic cluster", "Add a `SCHEDULE` clause to the view"],
 "Incremental refresh of materialized views requires serverless compute. On classic compute, a materialized view is fully recomputed on every update, whatever changed.\n`continuous mode`: this changes how often updates run, not how much of the view each update recomputes.\n`Photon`: this makes each recompute faster but still recomputes the whole view.\n`SCHEDULE clause`: this sets when a refresh runs, not whether it is incremental.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ldp/materialized-views"],
["A310", 3, "A function decorated with `@dp.materialized_view` builds a DataFrame, calls `df.count()` to log a row count, then runs `df.write.saveAsTable('audit.snapshot')` before returning `df`. What is wrong?",
 ["The code may run several times, so it must only return a DataFrame", "The function must return a streaming DataFrame from `spark.readStream`", "`saveAsTable` must target a schema in the pipeline's default catalog", "`count()` is allowed, but the write must use `mode('overwrite')`"],
 "A pipeline evaluates its source code several times during planning and runs, so a dataset function should only define and return a DataFrame. Docs say never to write to tables there and to avoid actions such as `count()`, `collect()` and `saveAsTable()`.\n`must return a streaming DataFrame`: that applies to streaming tables. A materialized view reads in batch.\n`schema in the default catalog`: the write's location isn't the problem. Any write inside dataset code repeats as a side effect.\n`mode('overwrite')`: changing the write mode still leaves a write, and a `count()` action, inside dataset code.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ldp/developer/python-ref"],
// ---------- Added 2026-11-25: compute_ops ----------
["A311", 1, "A nightly notebook job on serverless compute finishes hours before anyone needs the data. The team wants to cut cost and doesn't mind a few extra minutes of startup. What should they change?",
 ["Switch the job to standard performance mode", "Keep the default performance-optimized mode", "Move it to a Pro SQL warehouse", "Run it on an always-on all-purpose cluster"],
 "Serverless jobs default to performance-optimized mode for fast startup. Standard mode trades a slower start (typically 4 to 6 minutes) for fewer DBUs, which fits a job with slack in its deadline.\n`performance-optimized mode`: it's the default and is built for interactive and time-sensitive work, so it costs more and buys nothing here.\n`Pro SQL warehouse`: a SQL warehouse serves SQL and BI queries; it doesn't run notebook job tasks on serverless jobs compute.\n`always-on all-purpose cluster`: it bills while idle between runs, and Databricks doesn't recommend all-purpose compute for production jobs.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/jobs/run-serverless-jobs"],
["A312", 1, "Analysts run ad hoc BI queries a few times a day, and the warehouse sits idle in between. They want queries to start within seconds after idle, and they need no custom networking. Which compute fits?",
 ["A serverless SQL warehouse", "A Pro SQL warehouse", "A Classic SQL warehouse", "An all-purpose cluster with auto termination"],
 "Serverless SQL warehouses typically start in 2 to 6 seconds and support all Databricks SQL performance features, so intermittent BI use pays little for idle time and doesn't wait on startup.\n`Pro SQL warehouse`: its compute runs in your AWS account and takes about 4 minutes to start; choose it when serverless isn't available in the region or you need custom networking.\n`Classic SQL warehouse`: also about 4 minutes to start, and it gives only entry-level performance, without Predictive IO or Intelligent Workload Management.\n`all-purpose cluster with auto termination`: it's general Spark compute, not optimised for BI, and a restart after termination takes minutes.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/compute/sql-warehouse/warehouse-types"],
["A313", 1, "A security reviewer asks where Databricks compute resources run. Which statement is correct?",
 ["Classic compute runs in your AWS account; serverless compute runs in the Databricks account", "Classic and serverless compute both run in your AWS account", "Serverless compute runs in your AWS account; classic compute runs in the Databricks account", "Both run in the control plane, which sits in your AWS account"],
 "The control plane (Databricks' backend services) lives in the Databricks account. Classic compute plane resources run in your AWS account, while serverless compute plane resources run in a compute layer inside the Databricks account.\n`both run in your AWS account`: true for classic only; serverless resources don't run in your cloud account.\n`serverless in your AWS account, classic in Databricks`: this swaps the two planes.\n`both run in the control plane`: compute runs in a compute plane, not the control plane, and the control plane is in the Databricks account, not yours.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/getting-started/high-level-architecture"],
["A314", 1, "A legacy notebook uses `spark.sparkContext` and RDD transformations, and it must run on Databricks Runtime for ML. Which compute can run it?",
 ["Classic compute with dedicated access mode", "Classic compute with standard access mode", "Serverless compute for notebooks", "A serverless SQL warehouse"],
 "Dedicated compute is the fallback when a workload needs something standard access mode doesn't support. Standard mode rules out RDD APIs and Databricks Runtime for ML, so this notebook needs classic compute in dedicated access mode.\n`standard access mode`: it doesn't support RDD APIs or Databricks Runtime for ML.\n`serverless compute for notebooks`: it supports only Spark Connect APIs, so RDD code won't run, and you can't pick an ML runtime.\n`serverless SQL warehouse`: it runs SQL, not Python notebooks using `sparkContext`.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/compute/access-mode-limitations"],
["A315", 1, "A production job runs on a shared all-purpose cluster that stays up between runs, and the Spark UI shows long idle gaps. Which change follows Databricks guidance and stops paying for idle time?",
 ["Run the job's tasks on serverless compute", "Attach an instance pool to the all-purpose cluster", "Pick a larger driver for the all-purpose cluster", "Point the job's tasks at a Pro SQL warehouse"],
 "Gaps on all-purpose compute usually mean the cluster has nothing to do, yet it still bills. Databricks recommends serverless compute, where you don't pay for idle time, and doesn't recommend all-purpose compute for production jobs.\n`instance pool`: a pool reserves instances to cut startup time; it doesn't stop an always-on cluster from billing while idle.\n`larger driver`: that's the fix for an overloaded driver, and here the cluster is idle, not overloaded.\n`Pro SQL warehouse`: it runs SQL tasks only and also bills while running until it auto stops.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/optimizations/spark-ui-guide/spark-job-gaps"],
["A316", 6, "An admin adds a cluster-scoped init script to a cluster, and now the cluster won't start. The script exits with code 1. What is happening, and how should it be investigated?",
 ["The non-zero exit fails the launch; enable cluster log delivery and read the init script log", "The script is skipped and the cluster starts; check the Spark UI's Executors tab", "The cluster starts without workers; check the driver's stdout in the notebook", "The cluster retries the script forever; check the job's matrix view for retries"],
 "A cluster-scoped init script that returns a non-zero exit code makes the cluster launch fail. To find out why, configure cluster log delivery and read the init script log; the compute event log also shows `INIT_SCRIPTS_STARTED` and `INIT_SCRIPTS_FINISHED`.\n`script is skipped and the cluster starts`: a failing script isn't skipped; the launch fails.\n`starts without workers`: the launch fails as a whole, and with no running cluster there's no notebook output to read.\n`retries the script forever`: the launch simply fails, and the job matrix view shows task runs, not init script attempts.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/init-scripts/cluster-scoped"],
["A317", 6, "A cluster has `pandas==2.0.3` installed as a compute-scoped library. A notebook runs `%pip install pandas==2.2.2` and then restarts Python. Which version does `import pandas` load in that notebook?",
 ["2.2.2, because notebook-scoped libraries take precedence", "2.0.3, because compute-scoped libraries take precedence", "The version bundled with Databricks Runtime", "Neither; the import fails with a version conflict"],
 "When names clash, the library with higher precedence is imported. Notebook-scoped `%pip` installs rank above compute-scoped libraries, which rank above libraries bundled in Databricks Runtime. So this notebook gets 2.2.2, and other notebooks on the cluster still get 2.0.3.\n`2.0.3, compute-scoped takes precedence`: the order is the other way round; compute-scoped sits below notebook-scoped.\n`version bundled with Databricks Runtime`: runtime libraries have the lowest precedence of the three.\n`import fails with a version conflict`: precedence resolves the clash; both versions can coexist on the cluster.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/libraries/"],
["A318", 6, "In a notebook, an engineer imports a library, then runs `%pip install --upgrade` for it. The next cell still reports the old version. What should they do?",
 ["Run `dbutils.library.restartPython()` and re-import", "Install it again as a compute-scoped library", "Detach and re-run `%pip install` with `--force`", "Restart the cluster from the compute page"],
 "`%pip` doesn't restart the Python process, so a module that was already imported stays loaded in memory. Running `dbutils.library.restartPython()` restarts Python so the next import picks up the new version.\n`install it as a compute-scoped library`: compute-scoped libraries rank below notebook-scoped ones, and that wouldn't unload the module already imported.\n`re-run %pip with --force`: reinstalling doesn't change what the running Python process has already imported.\n`restart the cluster`: it would work but is heavy-handed, interrupts everyone on the cluster, and drops notebook-scoped libraries, which must be reinstalled.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/libraries/notebooks-python-libraries"],
["A319", 6, "A wide aggregation fails with `ExecutorLostFailure ... Remote RPC client disassociated`. Shuffle partitions are set to a small fixed number. Which change is the most direct fix?",
 ["Set `spark.sql.shuffle.partitions` to `auto`", "Add more workers of the same instance type", "Select a larger driver instance type", "Raise the broadcast join threshold"],
 "`ExecutorLostFailure ... Remote RPC client disassociated` often means executors ran out of memory (the docs note the error can be generic). Too few shuffle partitions makes each one too big for memory; `auto` lets Spark choose the count.\n`more workers of the same instance type`: the memory-to-core ratio matters, not the cluster's total memory, so each task still gets the same memory.\n`larger driver instance`: the failure is on executors, not the driver.\n`raise the broadcast join threshold`: a large broadcast is itself a cause of executor memory problems, so raising it makes things worse.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/optimizations/spark-ui-guide/spark-memory-issues"],
["A320", 6, "The Spark UI shows a large shuffle stage with spill statistics on its stage page, and no sign of skew. Which change does Databricks suggest first?",
 ["Use auto-optimized shuffle partitions", "Add a REBALANCE hint to the output", "Salt the join keys before the shuffle", "Run OPTIMIZE with ZORDER on the input"],
 "Spill means partitions didn't fit in executor memory and were written to disk. Databricks' first fixes are auto-optimized shuffle (`spark.sql.shuffle.partitions=auto`), holding less data in memory, or instances with more memory.\n`REBALANCE hint`: a skew fix that evens out output partitions; there's no skew here.\n`salt the join keys`: also a skew fix, for a few hot keys; it doesn't help when every partition is simply too big.\n`OPTIMIZE with ZORDER`: it improves data skipping on reads; it doesn't change how much memory each shuffle partition needs.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/optimizations/spark-ui-guide/long-spark-stage-page"],
["A321", 6, "An all-purpose cluster runs several Structured Streaming queries, and a dozen idle notebooks are attached. The Metrics tab shows the driver under heavy load. Which change helps?",
 ["Move some streams to separate compute and detach unused notebooks", "Add more worker nodes and enable autoscaling", "Raise `spark.sql.shuffle.partitions` on the cluster", "Turn on Photon and the disk cache for the cluster"],
 "The most common cause of an overloaded driver is too many concurrent operations, and the driver also keeps state for every attached notebook. Moving workloads to separate compute and detaching unused notebooks cuts what one driver coordinates.\n`more workers and autoscaling`: the bottleneck is the single driver; more executors add capacity it still has to coordinate.\n`raise spark.sql.shuffle.partitions`: that changes executor task sizes, not driver load.\n`Photon and the disk cache`: these speed up executor reads and processing; they don't reduce the driver's concurrency or state.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/optimizations/spark-ui-guide/spark-driver-overloaded"],
["A322", 6, "A job's task fails now and then, but not on every run. Which Lakeflow Jobs view shows each task's status and duration across recent runs at a glance?",
 ["The matrix view on the job's Runs tab", "The timeline view of a single job run", "The compute event log for the job cluster", "The Spark UI jobs timeline for the last run"],
 "The matrix view on the job's Runs tab shows run history for the job and every task. Colour shows status (red for failed) and bar height shows duration, so an intermittently failing task stands out across runs.\n`timeline view of a single run`: it shows slow and overlapping tasks within one run, not a pattern across runs.\n`compute event log`: it records cluster lifecycle events, not task outcomes across job runs.\n`Spark UI jobs timeline`: it shows the Spark jobs of one compute session, not Lakeflow task statuses across runs.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/jobs/monitor"],
["A323", 4, "A job must run a `.sql` file of several statements, versioned in a remote Git repository, on a SQL warehouse after an ingest task. Which task configuration fits?",
 ["A SQL task with the File type and Git provider source", "A SQL task with the Query type and a saved query", "A Python script task on serverless compute", "A Pipeline task that points at the Git repository"],
 "A SQL task of type File runs a `.sql` file that can hold several statements on a serverless or Pro SQL warehouse. The Git provider source pulls it from a remote repository, which Databricks recommends for versioned scheduled assets. A dependency on the ingest task orders it.\n`Query type with a saved query`: it runs one saved query from the workspace, not the file in Git.\n`Python script task`: it runs Python, not a `.sql` file, and wouldn't run on a SQL warehouse.\n`Pipeline task`: it runs an update of an existing Lakeflow pipeline, not an arbitrary SQL file.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/jobs/sql"],
["A324", 4, "After a nightly ETL job, a published AI/BI dashboard must refresh and email its pages as a PDF to stakeholders. The refresh must never run before the ETL finishes. What fits best?",
 ["A Dashboard task with subscribers, dependent on the ETL task", "A schedule set in the dashboard UI for a fixed morning time", "A SQL task with the Alert type, dependent on the ETL task", "A Pipeline task with full refresh at the end of the job"],
 "A Dashboard task refreshes a published dashboard inside the job and emails subscribers, optionally with PDF attachments. Making it depend on the ETL task means it only runs after the ETL succeeds.\n`schedule in the dashboard UI`: it runs at a fixed time whether or not the ETL has finished.\n`SQL task with the Alert type`: it evaluates a SQL alert and notifies on the result; it doesn't refresh the dashboard or send its pages.\n`Pipeline task with full refresh`: it reprocesses a Lakeflow pipeline's data; it doesn't refresh or email a dashboard.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/jobs/dashboard"],
["A325", 4, "Three jobs each need the same existing ingestion job to run first, with a different `region` parameter each time. The team doesn't want to copy the ingestion tasks into every job. What should they use?",
 ["A Run Job task that overrides the `region` parameter", "A For each task that loops over the ingestion tasks", "A Pipeline task that points at the ingestion job", "An If/else condition task that checks the `region` value"],
 "A Run Job task triggers another existing job, so the ingestion logic lives in one place. Job parameters are pushed down, so each caller can override defaults such as `region`. Avoid cycles and more than three levels of nesting.\n`For each task`: it loops one nested task over a list of inputs; it doesn't call a separate existing job.\n`Pipeline task`: it can only run a Lakeflow pipeline update, not a job.\n`If/else condition task`: it branches on a value within one job; it doesn't run another job.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/jobs/run-job"],
["A326", 4, "A Lakeflow pipeline should run continuously and be orchestrated from Lakeflow Jobs. What does Databricks recommend?",
 ["A Pipeline task in a continuous job, with Pipeline mode left as Triggered", "A Pipeline task in a scheduled job, with Pipeline mode set to Continuous", "A Pipeline task in a continuous job, with Pipeline mode set to Continuous", "A notebook task that starts a pipeline update through the REST API"],
 "Databricks recommends running a continuous pipeline from a continuous job. The job's trigger sets the execution mode, and keeping the pipeline's own `Pipeline mode` at Triggered (the default) avoids unexpected behaviour.\n`scheduled job with Pipeline mode Continuous`: a scheduled trigger starts one update per run and overrides the pipeline's setting, so it doesn't run continuously.\n`continuous job with Pipeline mode Continuous`: it's the near miss; Databricks specifically advises leaving the pipeline at Triggered inside a continuous job.\n`notebook task calling the REST API`: it reinvents the Pipeline task without its built-in integration, and it isn't the recommended pattern.",
 "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/jobs/pipeline"]
);
